# -*- coding: utf-8 -*-
# Transcribed question bank from the 4 image-based docs:
#   PMP 1 (180 single-answer MCQ), PMP 2 (180 mostly multi-select),
#   PMP 3 (2 Arabic MCQ), Important Questions on PMP (48, mixed/Arabic).
# Each entry: src, n, q, opts (list[str]), c (list[str] correct letters),
#             e (explanation), lang ("en" or "ar"), type ("mcq" default).
# Appended in batches as images are transcribed.

NEW = [
{"src":"PMP 1","n":1,"lang":"en",
 "q":"A company is moving its headquarters to another city. The project manager responsible for the assignment has been using a predictive approach during the transition. However, the information systems will be migrated using an agile approach. The project manager needs to decide which kind of contract to sign with the vendor responsible for migrating the systems. The project manager is concerned about controlling finances while delivering results. How should the project manager address this situation?",
 "opts":["Use fixed-price increments as user stories are completed.","Sign a single agreement for the whole project.","Negotiate payment after all project migration is completed.","Confirm payment after each project retrospective."],
 "c":["A"],
 "e":"For agile work where the buyer wants financial control while still getting results, fixed-price increments tied to completed user stories give incremental, transparent payments and keep cost predictable. A single up-front agreement, or paying only at the very end, removes the cost control and flexibility agile delivery needs."},

{"src":"PMP 1","n":2,"lang":"en",
 "q":"A new building project is in the middle of its life cycle. The municipality informs the project manager that due to new regulations, the parking lot around the project has to accommodate twice the number of cars than was initially planned. What should the project manager do next?",
 "opts":["Assess the impact of the required change and consult with the executive sponsors to determine the best course of action.","Have the company's lawyers write to the municipality and reject the change as it was not part of the scope approved by the municipality.","Ask the designers to start designing a solution for this new requirement while the project is running and determine the funding for this change.","Request the project sponsor to fund this change as it is a new regulation, then order the materials in order to start working."],
 "c":["A"],
 "e":"A mandatory external (regulatory) change still goes through change control: first analyze the impact on scope, schedule, and cost, then consult the sponsors/CCB to decide. Rejecting it, or starting design/ordering before assessing impact and getting approval, skips analysis and proper change control."},

{"src":"PMP 1","n":3,"lang":"en",
 "q":"An agile project has a documented list of requirements prioritized in high, medium, and low categories. The project team has expressed that it is difficult to know what to work on next as so many items are listed as high-priority. What should the project manager recommend?",
 "opts":["Ask the team to prioritize the high-priority items based on what they feel is a logical sequence to build.","Discard the current categorization of requirements as 'high, medium, and low' and prioritize numerically.","Ensure the product owner determines the priority based on a product roadmap, release plan, and iteration plan.","Ask the team to prioritize the high-priority items based on the release plan and what allows for testing most effectively."],
 "c":["C"],
 "e":"In agile, the product owner owns the backlog and sets priority based on business value, using the product roadmap, release plan, and iteration plan. The team builds; it does not decide business priority. Re-categorizing or letting the team self-prioritize by feel ignores the PO's accountability for value."},

{"src":"PMP 1","n":4,"lang":"en",
 "q":"A company is transitioning from a predictive approach to an agile approach. The team members work remotely, and some have personal conflicts that make them unavailable for some project ceremonies. The product owner has made it clear that the agenda is very tight and the team should make every effort to participate in all ceremonies. What should the project manager, as a servant leader, do to address this situation?",
 "opts":["Ask the team members to decide collectively on how to support the team.","Perform the ceremonies without the team members and send them notes by email.","Engage in separate ceremonies with these team members to allow them to stay on the project.","Evaluate alternative options and try to find resources with similar skills."],
 "c":["A"],
 "e":"A servant leader empowers the team to self-organize and solve the problem collectively, fostering ownership. Excluding members, running duplicate ceremonies, or quietly replacing people undercuts collaboration and the agile value of individuals and interactions."},

{"src":"PMP 1","n":5,"lang":"en",
 "q":"A project manager needs the team to define the steps and identify the risks of migrating data to a new system. What type of leadership should the project manager use to accomplish this?",
 "opts":["Transformational","Servant","Dynamic","Laissez-faire"],
 "c":["B"],
 "e":"Servant leadership facilitates the team's own discovery of the steps and risks, providing support and removing impediments while the experts contribute their knowledge. Laissez-faire is too hands-off for risk work, and 'dynamic' is not a recognized PMI leadership style."},

{"src":"PMP 1","n":6,"lang":"en",
 "q":"A project involves exporting 150 packages to the customer. However, only 30 packages have been cleared by the customer for shipment. The logistics manager provided details to the customer 2 weeks ago and set up a weekly conference call to effectively communicate with them, but the customer has not been participating in the calls. How should the project manager handle this situation?",
 "opts":["Request a delivery date extension from the customer","Request a change in the contract to include the shipment in the project management plan","Inform the customer that subsequent packages cannot be manufactured","Find the root cause of the issue and discuss the customer's current engagement"],
 "c":["D"],
 "e":"Understand before acting: find out why the customer is disengaged and not clearing packages, then address the real concern. Requesting extensions, changing the contract, or threatening to stop manufacturing all act before the root cause is known."},

{"src":"PMP 1","n":7,"lang":"en",
 "q":"A project manager is using an agile approach. During the sprint planning meeting, the product owner flagged a backlog item as high business value and easy to implement. However, the other team members identified a high dependency between this item and another item that is flagged as low business value and high complexity. What should the project manager do to support the backlog prioritization?",
 "opts":["Facilitate the discussion until the team reaches an agreement about the two items.","Prioritize the item flagged as high business value and low complexity for this sprint.","Prioritize the item flagged as a dependency with low business value and high complexity.","Support the team to move both items to the next sprint when the team will know more."],
 "c":["A"],
 "e":"As a servant leader the PM facilitates dialogue so the team and PO surface the dependency and reach consensus, rather than imposing a priority or deferring the decision. Collaboration produces the best-informed prioritization."},

{"src":"PMP 1","n":8,"lang":"en",
 "q":"A project to implement a new online landing platform has started. After the first sprint, the project sponsor requested a change. What should the project manager do next?",
 "opts":["Inform the stakeholders of the change.","Validate the work completed.","Escalate the change request to the sponsor.","Facilitate the update of the product backlog."],
 "c":["D"],
 "e":"In agile, requested changes are handled by updating and re-prioritizing the product backlog so future sprints reflect the new need. Informing stakeholders or escalating comes later; the change is managed through the backlog, not formal change control."},

{"src":"PMP 1","n":9,"lang":"en",
 "q":"While following up on a deliverable, a team member expresses concern to the project manager about working with another team member. This team member states that the other team member, who is a functional manager, is often defensive and aggressive during meetings. How should the project manager handle this situation?",
 "opts":["Suggest that the team member ignore the disruptive team member and focus only on the work","Encourage both team members to meet as soon as possible and resolve the problem","Monitor the situation for a few weeks to determine if the disruptive team member's attitude changes","Report the disruptive team member to the human resource (HR) department"],
 "c":["B"],
 "e":"Conflict should be resolved directly and early by the parties involved (collaborate). Ignoring it, waiting weeks, or escalating to HR all avoid the prompt, direct conversation that resolves interpersonal conflict best."},

{"src":"PMP 1","n":10,"lang":"en",
 "q":"A key team member informs the project manager that they have been accepted to a master's degree program. The team member explains that the first semester of the master's program will coincide with the last phase of the project. What should the project manager do?",
 "opts":["Ask the team member to inform human resources (HR) about their enrollment in the master's program","Ask the team member to delegate some of their tasks to another colleague during the last phase of the project","Suggest the team member postpone their enrollment in the master's program until the project is completed","Assess the team member's commitment to the master's program and its impact on project performance"],
 "c":["B"],
 "e":"A supportive, collaborative solution lets the team member balance study and project work by delegating some tasks, protecting both the person's growth and delivery. Asking them to postpone or pushing it to HR is intrusive and not servant-leadership."},

{"src":"PMP 1","n":11,"lang":"en",
 "q":"A project manager implementing a hybrid project is dealing with multiple stakeholders in a rapidly changing business environment. How should the project manager ensure that the needs of stakeholders are consistently met?",
 "opts":["Use iterative life cycles involving stakeholders to enable the team to improve the product end result through successive prototypes","Identify all the critical stakeholders during project initiation","Add more iterative techniques to improve stakeholder learning","Assign one team member to one stakeholder to ensure that the responsible team member is conversant with the needs of the stakeholder"],
 "c":["A"],
 "e":"Iterative cycles with successive prototypes give stakeholders frequent review-and-feedback points, so the product adapts as requirements change. This continuous engagement fits a hybrid project in a fast-changing environment better than a one-time identification or a rigid 1:1 assignment."},

{"src":"PMP 1","n":12,"lang":"en",
 "q":"A scrum team's daily meetings are conducted by teleconference between three teams, each located in different countries. The meetings typically last 45 minutes with many misunderstandings due to language barriers. How should the project manager resolve this issue for the next iteration?",
 "opts":["Instruct the meeting participants to speak only about blockers during the meeting","Train the three-team leads to conduct a local meeting, then run an overall status meeting","Transfer half of the backlog items to the next iteration to allow more time to complete the meetings","Extend the meeting to 1 hour and request that all of the participants bring up all of the issues"],
 "c":["B"],
 "e":"Local meetings in each team's own language reduce misunderstanding, and a short overall status meeting then coordinates cross-team dependencies. Restricting topics or extending the meeting does not fix the underlying language barrier."},

{"src":"PMP 1","n":13,"lang":"en",
 "q":"In an iteration-based marketing project, a product is scheduled to be launched at the sixth iteration. In the third iteration demo, the marketing team expressed that they need five iterations to launch the product and they have not started yet. What should the project team have done to avoid this issue?",
 "opts":["Held a touchpoint with marketing before the demo to discuss any impediments","Scheduled a demo presentation of the product during the first iteration","Asked the marketing team to start working on the product immediately","Confirmed marketing requirements and constraints when adding to the backlog"],
 "c":["D"],
 "e":"Capturing each group's requirements and constraints (like marketing's lead-time) when items enter the backlog prevents late surprises. Knowing marketing needed five iterations up front would have shaped sequencing and planning."},

{"src":"PMP 1","n":14,"lang":"en",
 "q":"One team member is complaining about an agreement made during the last meeting. That team member was present at the meeting and participated in the decisions registered in the meeting minutes. What should the project manager do next?",
 "opts":["Meet with the team member and search for the root cause of their behavior","Arrange an additional meeting with the team to communicate the agreement made during the last meeting","Talk with the team member and explain that once the meeting ended, the agreement must be respected","Schedule a new meeting to evaluate the agreement and negotiate a new one"],
 "c":["A"],
 "e":"Understand before acting: meet the member to uncover the real reason behind their objection, since a participant disputing a documented agreement signals a deeper concern. Simply insisting the agreement stands, or renegotiating, treats the symptom not the cause."},

{"src":"PMP 1","n":15,"lang":"en",
 "q":"Halfway through project execution, stakeholders ask for equipment that was expected to be procured. The project manager explains that the equipment was purchased, but differs from what the stakeholders are currently asking for. This is because the initial requirements were changed during a previous meeting. What should the project manager have done to avoid this situation?",
 "opts":["Ensured changing stakeholder needs and requests were properly monitored","Ensured project requirements were validated and communicated with the procurement team","Ensured procurement contracts were able to be canceled when situations like this arise","Ensured that communications were sent to all stakeholders when requirements change"],
 "c":["D"],
 "e":"Keeping all stakeholders informed whenever requirements change creates the transparency that prevents mismatched expectations. Had the change been communicated to everyone, stakeholders would not have expected the old equipment."},

{"src":"PMP 1","n":16,"lang":"en",
 "q":"A company has decided to initiate a project to eliminate a sales channel that has been unprofitable for the past 3 years. Some members of the project team are pessimistic about the project. What should the project manager do in this situation?",
 "opts":["Provide team members with specific and clear instructions on the project objectives and tasks","Escalate the team's concerns and ask the project sponsor to change the team's composition","Engage the functional managers to describe the project's impact to the team members directly","Communicate the project's impact and plans to engage the project team throughout the project"],
 "c":["D"],
 "e":"Continuous communication and engagement addresses pessimism by helping the team understand the why and stay motivated. Replacing the team or delegating the messaging to functional managers avoids the PM's own engagement responsibility."},

{"src":"PMP 1","n":17,"lang":"en",
 "q":"A senior employee with experience relevant to a highly complex project is seeking a promotion to be able to join the project. The employee has made a recommendation regarding the project. The project charter was recently approved. How should the project manager address the employee's request?",
 "opts":["Ask the project sponsor to revisit the project charter to consider the employee's recommendation","Create a salience model to determine if the employee will have influence on the project","Evaluate the employee's recommendation by reviewing the lessons learned from a similar project","Assess the influence and power of the employee to see if the employee will add value"],
 "c":["D"],
 "e":"Assess the employee's influence, power, and potential value before acting, so any involvement aligns with project objectives. Reopening the just-approved charter or building a full salience model for one person is premature."},

{"src":"PMP 1","n":18,"lang":"en",
 "q":"A new development project is about to start, and the project manager knows that a high-profile customer makes very specific and frequent requests in all sprints. This project will demand contract coordination with third parties and the project manager must find a better way to manage customer and partner expectations. What strategy should the project manager use?",
 "opts":["Work very closely with the customer and third parties to meet their needs","Focus on the implementation of a software as a service (SaaS) solution for the customer","Specify the scope and features to be deployed in the contract clearly","Change the marketing and product management definitions to meet stakeholder needs"],
 "c":["A"],
 "e":"Close, proactive collaboration with the customer and the third parties keeps communication open and lets the team adapt to frequent, evolving requests. Locking scope rigidly or changing definitions does not manage the ongoing expectation flow."},

{"src":"PMP 1","n":19,"lang":"en",
 "q":"In a project, one core team member tends to do all the work and ensures all tasks are completed. This core team member is soon leaving the project, which may make the project vulnerable. What should the project manager do?",
 "opts":["Assess a mechanism for knowledge transfer among the team members","Ensure the activities of this team member are distributed across the project team","Ask human resources (HR) to counsel the team member to balance the workload","Motivate the team member to stay by providing them with incentives"],
 "c":["B"],
 "e":"Distributing the work across the team removes the single point of failure before the member departs, building resilience and shared ownership. Knowledge transfer alone, HR counseling, or retention incentives do not fix the over-reliance on one person."},

{"src":"PMP 1","n":20,"lang":"en",
 "q":"A multimillion-dollar project has started and is in the execution phase. A local regulatory authority has just announced changes to the requirements that will need to be implemented in 6 months, prior to completing the project execution phase. What should the project manager do next?",
 "opts":["Proceed with the defined project requirements and delay implementing the new requirements","Add the new requirements to the project scope immediately since they are mandatory changes","Ignore the new requirements as the current budget and schedule do not allow for any changes","Review the new requirements with the compliance department to determine if a change request is needed"],
 "c":["D"],
 "e":"Even mandatory regulatory changes are first reviewed (here, with compliance) to assess impact and decide whether a change request is needed, then routed through change control. Adding scope immediately skips impact analysis; ignoring or delaying risks non-compliance."},

{"src":"PMP 1","n":21,"lang":"en",
 "q":"A project manager joins an existing project. One project stakeholder is unhappy with the frequency and types of project updates received and has made a request for more information to be shared. What should the project manager do next?",
 "opts":["Consult the scope baseline document to track project updates","Update the project communications management plan immediately","Inform the stakeholder to wait for the annual stakeholder review meeting","Schedule a meeting with the stakeholder to better understand their request"],
 "c":["D"],
 "e":"Understand the stakeholder's specific needs first by meeting them, then tailor communications accordingly. Updating the comms plan before understanding the request, or making them wait, fails to address the concern properly."},

{"src":"PMP 1","n":22,"lang":"en",
 "q":"A new marketing vice president just joined a software startup and is concerned that the agile model does not provide the needed accountability for their role. What should the responsible project manager do to ensure the vice president is comfortable with the project meeting its agreements?",
 "opts":["Require proper approval of all requirements from the marketing vice president and formally document the agreements","Establish an executive review board where all stakeholders discuss and approve features and requirements","Foster an environment of open communication where all parties can discuss issues and agree on objectives","Leverage the use of the review/demo sessions with the vice president to acknowledge that the agreements were met"],
 "c":["D"],
 "e":"Agile's sprint review/demo is the built-in accountability mechanism: it shows the VP that committed work was delivered as agreed. Adding sign-off gates or a separate review board imposes heavy predictive governance that agile already addresses through demos."},

{"src":"PMP 1","n":23,"lang":"en",
 "q":"On a project with multiple international locations, a trend for delays has been flagged. When looking into the issue, the project manager realized that some of the key activities were scheduled over the holiday periods in the overseas offices. What should the project manager do next?",
 "opts":["Request that the offices in overseas locations work overtime to mitigate schedule slippages","Update the schedule to reflect the holidays and notify the project sponsor of the delayed completion","Hire additional personnel on a temporary basis to cover the extra workload during the holiday seasons","Update the schedule to reflect the holidays and share the workload among offices to mitigate schedule slippage"],
 "c":["D"],
 "e":"Update the schedule for the real holiday calendars and rebalance work across offices to recover the slippage proactively. Forcing overtime or just notifying the sponsor of a delay is reactive and ignores the available cross-office capacity."},

{"src":"PMP 1","n":24,"lang":"en",
 "q":"A project has recently kicked off. The project manager observes that there are different expectations among the project team members. These growing misunderstandings are affecting team productivity. Which document should the project manager use as a guide to resolve the issue?",
 "opts":["Stakeholder register","Team charter","Project organization charts","Project charter"],
 "c":["B"],
 "e":"The team charter records the team's ground rules, values, and shared expectations/agreements, so it is the guide for resolving expectation clashes among members. The project charter authorizes the project; the stakeholder register lists stakeholders."},

{"src":"PMP 1","n":25,"lang":"en",
 "q":"During project execution, the project manager observes lower velocity and a high volume of tasks in the testing queue. The project is using a hybrid approach and there are only a few sprints remaining. What should the project manager do?",
 "opts":["Add a new resource with testing skills to the team","Provide testing training to all the project team members","Work with the team to understand and solve the issues","Increase the sprint duration so the testing can be completed"],
 "c":["C"],
 "e":"Collaborate with the team to find the root cause of the drop in velocity and the testing bottleneck, then adapt together. Adding people, mass training, or lengthening sprints are premature fixes applied before the real cause is understood."},

{"src":"PMP 1","n":26,"lang":"en",
 "q":"During an agile team retrospective, a project team member mentions that the code quality is degrading, which might result in future rework that could become unmanageable with time. What should the project manager do?",
 "opts":["Help the team create the definition of done (DoD) to improve delivery quality","Expand the team in order to improve the quality of the project","Add testing items to the backlog to assess quality periodically","Compare the cost of improving quality against the cost of failure and adjust the budget"],
 "c":["A"],
 "e":"A clear, shared Definition of Done that includes code-quality and testing criteria prevents incomplete, low-quality work from being accepted, addressing the root cause. Expanding the team or budgeting does not establish the quality standard itself."},

{"src":"PMP 1","n":27,"lang":"en",
 "q":"The marketing team is transitioning to using hybrid approaches for their projects. The marketing director is not knowledgeable about hybrid methodologies, and the project manager needs the director's support as the marketing director is a key stakeholder. What should the project manager do to obtain the support?",
 "opts":["Mentor the marketing director on hybrid approaches.","Ask the marketing director to participate in daily meetings.","Ask the marketing team members to coach the director.","Train the marketing team and keep the director informed."],
 "c":["A"],
 "e":"Mentoring the key stakeholder directly on hybrid approaches builds their understanding and earns their support. Delegating the coaching to team members, or only keeping the director informed, does not give this influential stakeholder the knowledge they need to back the change."},

{"src":"PMP 1","n":28,"lang":"en",
 "q":"During a daily standup meeting, a roadblock was raised that is preventing one of the team members from proceeding with work. The project manager figures out that it is due to a technical issue. What should the project manager do to prioritize this critical impediment?",
 "opts":["Perform a brainstorming session to address the issue and add the solution to the sprint.","Solve the issue for the team member.","Escalate the issue to the software vendor for technical resolution.","Coach the team to come up with their own solution and add it to the next sprint."],
 "c":["D"],
 "e":"Agile teams are self-managing, so the servant-leader PM refers the impediment back to the team to solve and schedules the fix. Solving it for them or escalating externally undercuts self-organization and isn't the first move."},

{"src":"PMP 1","n":29,"lang":"en",
 "q":"During the implementation stage of a project, a newly appointed team leader approaches the project manager to verify the team's tasks and schedule. The work did not go as planned due to the team leader's lack of management experience. This could cause delays for the project. What should the project manager do?",
 "opts":["Provide mentoring to the newly appointed team leader.","Ask the human resources director to appoint a senior team leader.","Inform the project sponsor about the possible delay.","Directly manage the project team to avoid project delays."],
 "c":["A"],
 "e":"A new leader in the forming stage needs guidance, so the PM mentors them to build capability. Replacing them, escalating, or taking over the team directly removes the chance to develop the leader and isn't servant leadership."},

{"src":"PMP 1","n":30,"lang":"en",
 "q":"A new project manager learns the work assigned to the team has already been decomposed to its lowest level. The project manager reviews the work and feels some additional, useful information is missing. What should the project manager review?",
 "opts":["The requirements traceability matrix","The project charter and scope statement","The WBS dictionary","The business requirement documents (BRD)"],
 "c":["C"],
 "e":"The WBS dictionary provides the detailed information for each work package (descriptions, deliverables, acceptance criteria, resources), which is the missing implementation detail behind the decomposed work. The traceability matrix and charter serve different purposes."},

{"src":"PMP 1","n":31,"lang":"en",
 "q":"A product company is transforming the way it develops and releases products in the market. Executives believe that this is a high-risk initiative, and this initiative must be successful. What should the project manager do in this scenario?",
 "opts":["Develop a communications management plan to inform the employees about the new business model.","Develop a detailed sprint plan with clear deliverables.","Adopt an iterative rollout approach that delivers the highest business value earlier.","Develop a business case with assumptions for the new model."],
 "c":["C"],
 "e":"For a high-risk, must-succeed initiative, an iterative rollout that delivers the highest-value increments first reduces risk and proves value early through feedback. A single detailed plan or comms plan does not manage the uncertainty the way incremental delivery does."},

{"src":"PMP 1","n":32,"lang":"en",
 "q":"A project manager receives a voicemail from a business analyst on the project team. The business analyst indicates that an internal stakeholder is upset that they were not included before the project began. The stakeholder is asking for an addition to the requirements traceability matrix. What should the project manager do to improve the stakeholder relationship?",
 "opts":["Update the stakeholder engagement plan to ensure the stakeholder is included in the project stakeholder list.","Consult the project scope document to make sure the addition is in scope.","Initiate a project change request so the change control board (CCB) can decide if it is in scope.","Engage the stakeholder to solicit more information before responding to the request."],
 "c":["D"],
 "e":"Identify and engage the newly surfaced stakeholder first to understand their request, then update the stakeholder register (a living document needing no CCB approval). Jumping to a change request or scope check skips understanding the stakeholder and their actual need."},

{"src":"PMP 1","n":33,"lang":"en",
 "q":"A project manager is acting as a scrum master in a hybrid project and is dealing with a conflict between team members. What strategy should be used to handle this conflict?",
 "opts":["Place conflicts on a shared conflict list and ensure it is visible.","Sort conflicts into two groups: positive and negative.","Place conflicts into categories: structural and interpersonal.","Review conflicts during daily Scrum so they are handled in a timely manner."],
 "c":["B"],
 "e":"Conflict can be constructive (exploring issues more fully) or destructive (emotional, damaging communication); sorting it into positive vs. negative lets the scrum master encourage the useful kind and address the harmful kind. The daily Scrum is not the venue for working interpersonal conflict."},

{"src":"PMP 1","n":34,"lang":"en",
 "q":"A retail chain is evaluating a project to replace payment systems across all its stores in multiple locations. The project does not pass the financial threshold but is also expected to increase market share, improve customer services, and retain more customers. The project is planned as a phased implementation, building on learning from the retrospectives during each phase. How should the business increase the value of the project?",
 "opts":["Quantify the expected tangible and intangible benefits in the benefits management plan for each phase.","Consult with experts on methods to reduce costs and increase the financial value of the project.","Ask the benefits owner to reassess the identified risks that are impacting the outcomes of the financial benefits.","Use a fishbone diagram to find the root cause of the lower financial benefits with the benefits owner."],
 "c":["A"],
 "e":"Capturing both tangible and intangible benefits (market share, customer service, retention) in the benefits management plan per phase shows the project's full value beyond the financial threshold. Cost-cutting or root-cause analysis ignores the intangible value driving the initiative."},

{"src":"PMP 1","n":35,"lang":"en",
 "q":"A project is being executed. The project's life cycle is defined as predictive; however, a major deliverable will be handed over incrementally to the customer. The assigned resources are experienced and reliable and are willing to make decisions that the project manager used to make after each incremental delivery on other projects. What should the project manager do?",
 "opts":["Inform the team that although this is a hybrid environment, the project manager must still be responsible for making decisions.","Consult the product owner about letting the resources make decisions.","Support the decisions of the team and transfer the decision making responsibility to them.","Schedule a steering committee meeting and obtain their approval on the request."],
 "c":["C"],
 "e":"With incremental delivery and experienced, reliable resources, the servant-leader PM empowers the team to make decisions, fostering ownership and faster adaptation. Insisting on retaining all decisions or escalating for approval contradicts the empowerment the hybrid/incremental context invites."},

{"src":"PMP 1","n":36,"lang":"en",
 "q":"A project manager just started managing agile projects. The project manager realizes that due to the lack of a clear definition of project objectives and definition of done (DoD), their colleague's projects have had many difficulties. Sponsors keep asking for more features and the projects have yet to be completed. What should the project manager do to avoid these issues?",
 "opts":["Arrange meetings to ensure every necessary task to complete the project is included in the project management plan.","Convince the project sponsor to incorporate quality experts so the product can be tested and accepted as completed.","Convince the product owner to approach the team and look for options to find a solution to the problem.","Schedule meetings with key stakeholders to build the agile project charter and set clear expectations for the project."],
 "c":["C"],
 "e":"Defining clear objectives and the Definition of Done is the product owner's accountability, so the PM engages the PO to work with the team on a solution. The other options bypass the PO's ownership of value, priority, and acceptance criteria."},

{"src":"PMP 1","n":37,"lang":"en",
 "q":"A verbal announcement is made that the new team member joining the Scrum team has limited mobility. The scrum master is aware that some team members are unsure how the dynamics of the team will change. What should the scrum master do?",
 "opts":["Create a stakeholder engagement plan.","Set up a meeting to discuss accommodations the team will have to make.","Design a communications management plan.","Update the team charter accordingly."],
 "c":["B"],
 "e":"Hold a preparatory meeting to understand the new member's needs and the accommodations the team should make, addressing the uncertainty directly and inclusively. Updating plans or the charter comes after the team understands what is actually needed."},

{"src":"PMP 1","n":38,"lang":"en",
 "q":"The project manager has learned that the project sponsor is unhappy with the development of the project requirements. In order to realign the project with the sponsor's expectations, what should the project manager do?",
 "opts":["Perform a stakeholder evaluation.","Review the project goal diagram with the team.","Confirm which templates the team should be using.","Review the risk management plan."],
 "c":["B"],
 "e":"Revisiting the project goal with the team realigns the requirements work to the intended objectives and the sponsor's expectations. Stakeholder evaluations, templates, or the risk plan do not directly re-anchor the requirements to the project goal."},

{"src":"PMP 1","n":39,"lang":"en",
 "q":"A project manager is appointed to simultaneously deploy a new solution to branches located in different countries. Deployment teams in each country will integrate and deploy the solution. The project manager discovers that the deployment teams each encounter similar issues. However, each team addresses the issues separately. What should the project manager do to help ensure more collaboration between the teams?",
 "opts":["Ask each deployment team to provide a very detailed schedule and action plan.","Ask the project sponsor to appoint new managers for the deployment teams.","Organize regular meetings with all of the deployment teams to share issues and solutions","Propose that some of the teams delay deployments in their countries."],
 "c":["C"],
 "e":"Regular cross-team meetings let the teams share common issues and reuse solutions instead of solving the same problems in isolation. Detailed schedules, new managers, or delays do not create the collaboration and knowledge-sharing that is missing."},

{"src":"PMP 1","n":40,"lang":"en",
 "q":"A research and development department is planning to develop a product that will introduce a new line of business for the organization. What should the project manager do to increase the project's chances of success?",
 "opts":["Start developing the project management plan based on a previous project template from the project management office (PMO).","Conduct an impact analysis of the new initiative to determine how the project should be rolled out.","Plan a working session focusing on the scope, vision, and mission of the initiative.","Conduct benchmarking to determine the business viability of the initiative."],
 "c":["C"],
 "e":"For a brand-new line of business, first establishing a shared scope, vision, and mission aligns everyone on what success looks like before detailed planning. Reusing an old template or benchmarking does not set the foundational direction this novel initiative needs."},

{"src":"PMP 1","n":41,"lang":"en",
 "q":"At the end of a project with multiple suppliers, the project manager is reviewing the closing process and finds that an unexpected financial balance is remaining. What will help the project manager understand why this has occurred?",
 "opts":["Lessons learned","Procurement audit","Organizational process assets","Project audit"],
 "c":["B"],
 "e":"A procurement audit reviews the contracts and procurement processes for completeness, accuracy, and effectiveness, which is exactly what explains a leftover financial balance across multiple suppliers. Lessons learned and OPAs do not analyze the contract finances."},

{"src":"PMP 1","n":42,"lang":"en",
 "q":"The project is delayed due to the high amount of major changes requested by different stakeholders at different stages of the project. If the project continues on the same path, it will consume twice the time and budget planned. What two actions can the project manager take to be more cost effective? (Choose two)",
 "opts":["Conduct a stakeholder analysis.","Update the project management plan with the new timeline.","Adopt an incremental approach.","Leverage work between team members.","Reinforce team communication."],
 "c":["A","C"],
 "e":"A stakeholder analysis identifies who is driving the constant changes so they can be engaged and managed, while an incremental approach absorbs evolving requirements efficiently. Simply updating the timeline or boosting communication does not address the root cause: unmanaged, changing stakeholder demands."},

{"src":"PMP 1","n":43,"lang":"en",
 "q":"An external project manager is managing the construction of new corporate offices for a large company. The project management plan states that it is necessary to contract a highly trained external resource to revise and validate an important project component. The client has rejected hiring the external resource because of the high cost, even though it is within the project budget. What should the project manager do to resolve this issue?",
 "opts":["Hire the resource in order to achieve the project objectives.","Evaluate the consequences and meet with the client to explain the possible scenarios.","Try to accomplish the task internally by leveling resources from other projects.","Ask the client for a meeting and explain that the task will not be performed."],
 "c":["B"],
 "e":"Evaluate the impact of not hiring the resource, then meet the client to explain the scenarios and consequences so they can make an informed decision. Overriding the client, improvising internally, or simply refusing the task all bypass understanding and transparent dialogue."},

{"src":"PMP 1","n":44,"lang":"en",
 "q":"A technical subject matter expert (SME) identifies an occurrence that should be captured in the project's lessons learned. The SME asks the project manager to write, document, and include it in the lessons learned register. What should the project manager do?",
 "opts":["Take ownership of documenting this information.","Ask the SME to document and capture the occurrence.","Request the SME to wait until the project closure phase.","Assign the documenting task to a project team member."],
 "c":["A"],
 "e":"Maintaining the lessons learned register is the project manager's responsibility, so the PM should document it accurately for future projects. Lessons learned are captured throughout the project, not deferred to closure, and shouldn't be pushed onto the SME or others."},

{"src":"PMP 1","n":45,"lang":"en",
 "q":"During the course of a project, the project manager's immediate director is communicating directly with the client to approve changes without the project manager's knowledge. This is causing confusion in the project team where the scope is changing in the background, but only being mentioned in project meetings. How should the project manager handle this?",
 "opts":["Review the stakeholder engagement plan and put in place any existing actions accordingly.","Communicate directly with the director and inform them of the communications management plan.","Discuss the issues with the project team and let them know that the project manager will handle scope changes.","Communicate directly with the client stating that any scope changes must go through the project manager."],
 "c":["B"],
 "e":"The director bypassing the PM is a breakdown of the communications plan, so the PM addresses the director directly and reaffirms the agreed communication and change channels. Going around the director to the client, or only briefing the team, does not fix the source of the problem."},

{"src":"PMP 1","n":46,"lang":"en",
 "q":"The sponsor is supportive in leveraging agile approaches. The project manager is contemplating how to roll out the agile approach and gain buy-in from both project team members and stakeholders. What should the project manager do first?",
 "opts":["Determine how to transform the organization using agile techniques, including organizational training and communications.","Select a current predictive project, define the approach, and provide agile training to execute the remainder of the project.","Start with a pilot project of appropriate complexity, and provide agile training to those who are impacted.","Determine which agile software tools will be required to support agile delivery within the organization."],
 "c":["C"],
 "e":"A pilot project of suitable complexity, with training for those affected, demonstrates value and builds buy-in safely before any broader rollout. A full org-wide transformation or tool selection is premature, and converting a running predictive project mid-stream is risky."},

{"src":"PMP 1","n":47,"lang":"en",
 "q":"A project manager is implementing a new software system. Some department staff members are questioning the necessity of the change and feel the new system will impede their current processes. What should the project manager have done at the start of the project to prevent this resistance?",
 "opts":["Ensured that the company culture encourages changes before accepting the project.","Involved stakeholders from all levels of the company so everyone understands the change.","Encouraged leadership to discuss the change with departments that are typically resistant to change.","Created a communications management plan that outlined the method of communicating to stakeholders."],
 "c":["B"],
 "e":"Engaging stakeholders from all levels early builds understanding and surfaces their needs, expectations, and requirements, which prevents resistance. A comms plan alone, or relying on culture/leadership, does not create the broad-based buy-in that early involvement does."},

{"src":"PMP 1","n":48,"lang":"en",
 "q":"A project team has recently completed the initial sprint for developing an automated payroll system for a company. The project manager has scheduled a sprint planning meeting with the product owner and team members to discuss which features should be worked on next. Which two pieces of information does the project manager need in order to make this meeting productive and effective? (Choose two)",
 "opts":["Product backlog","Sprint charter","Company mission and vision","Sprint goal","Burndown chart"],
 "c":["A","D"],
 "e":"Sprint planning uses the sprint goal (what the increment should achieve) and the prioritized product backlog (the candidate items) to build the sprint backlog. Mission/vision and the burndown chart are not the planning inputs for selecting next features."},

{"src":"PMP 1","n":49,"lang":"en",
 "q":"A large multi-year project requires highly skilled staff for successful deployment. The management team and the project sponsor have analyzed the resource requirements and agreed to have permanent staff hired for the project to avoid potential budget overrun. However, there is a risk of staff turnover during the course of the project, which may have an impact on the delivery. Which two actions should the project manager take to mitigate this risk? (Choose two)",
 "opts":["Introduce a recognition program to motivate and reward resource performance.","Enhance the competencies of the project team through training, mentoring, and coaching.","Increase the trained staff to create back-up for resources.","Convince senior management to hire third-party staff.","Meet with management to discuss dividing the project into smaller projects."],
 "c":["A","C"],
 "e":"A recognition program improves retention by motivating staff, and cross-training extra staff creates back-ups so a departure has less impact, directly mitigating turnover risk. Training competencies alone, third-party staff, or splitting the project do not address keeping and backing up the skilled people."},

{"src":"PMP 1","n":50,"lang":"en",
 "q":"The product owner decides to launch a product after a couple of releases, knowing that the minimum viable product (MVP) lacks some features. One of the key stakeholders, the marketing vice president, is not happy with the results and questioned the release decision. What should the project manager do?",
 "opts":["Coach the team on the decision-making process, assuming a risk-averse strategy towards product releasing.","Support the product owner's decision and seek better alignment with this stakeholder in order to avoid this type of issue in the future.","Assume the responsibility for product releases, making the final call on when something is ready for customer launch.","Escalate the issue to the sponsor, questioning the authority of the key stakeholder regarding the product launch decision."],
 "c":["B"],
 "e":"The product owner owns the release decision, so the PM supports it and works on better stakeholder alignment to prevent recurrence. Taking over release authority or escalating against the stakeholder undermines the PO's role and the relationship."},

{"src":"PMP 1","n":51,"lang":"en",
 "q":"The project manager has observed that two team members have been arguing with each other openly at meetings. To push their ideas through, each team member individually spoke with the sponsor directly and made decisions without informing the project manager or other team members. What two actions should the project manager take? (Choose two)",
 "opts":["Reiterate ground rules at the next meeting and ensure that all team members are clear about these rules.","Ask the team members to work it out themselves.","Address the behavioral issue with each team member.","Discuss the behavior directly with their managers and request them to correct the behavior.","Discuss this situation with other project managers and ask for advice."],
 "c":["A","C"],
 "e":"Reaffirming the team's ground rules sets the expected behavior for everyone, and addressing the issue individually with each member tackles the specific conduct. Escalating to their managers or asking other PMs avoids the PM's own responsibility to manage the team."},

{"src":"PMP 1","n":52,"lang":"en",
 "q":"A project manager has been managing a project for several months when an issue that had not been registered as a risk appeared. This issue may have a big impact on the project. What should the project manager do?",
 "opts":["Hold a meeting with the project team and relevant stakeholders to agree on the best way to manage the issue.","Inform the sponsor that the issue has arisen and that the project's success may be uncertain.","Delay the project until the issue is addressed and no longer presents as a risk to the project.","Avoid managing the issue as it was not registered as a risk for the project and there is no planned response to it."],
 "c":["A"],
 "e":"Collaborate with the team and relevant stakeholders to analyze the issue and agree on a response, then log and manage it. Alarming the sponsor, delaying the project, or ignoring it because it wasn't pre-registered are all poor responses to an emergent issue."},

{"src":"PMP 1","n":53,"lang":"en",
 "q":"A project sponsor commonly asks the project manager to skip project retrospectives due to time constraints. However, the project manager persists in running this critical ceremony by reducing the time for preparation and for discussion. What are two issues that these actions by the project manager could cause? (Choose two)",
 "opts":["A lack of direction and motivation for the team in the workshop","A focus on the negative and a disinterest in further improvements","A lot of discussions that yield no results or possibly too many results","Lessons learned from other teams to not be considered","Time management plan for the retrospective workshop to not be updated"],
 "c":["C","D"],
 "e":"Cutting preparation and discussion time makes the retrospective unfocused (discussions that go nowhere or sprawl) and means relevant lessons learned aren't properly captured or considered. The ceremony loses its value as a structured improvement workshop."},

{"src":"PMP 1","n":54,"lang":"en",
 "q":"A company's project management office (PMO) has started to implement iterative tools. A project manager is starting a new project and has identified an opportunity to use the iterative tools. One of the senior managers is concerned about this modified framework as it is fairly new to the company. What should the project manager do first?",
 "opts":["Organize individual sessions with the stakeholders to build awareness and trust in the new framework.","Escalate the senior manager's concern to the project sponsor and distribute the status to the team.","Send documentation to the senior manager about the advantages of the new framework.","Look for external training on the new framework to eliminate the risk of low engagement from stakeholders."],
 "c":["A"],
 "e":"Transparent, individual sessions with stakeholders build awareness and trust in the unfamiliar framework, addressing the concern directly. Escalating, emailing documents, or jumping to external training does not personally engage the concerned manager first."},

{"src":"PMP 1","n":55,"lang":"en",
 "q":"During the implementation of a project, the project team discovered a new opportunity. After an internal review, the project team agreed that the opportunity is outside of the project scope. The project manager decided to update the risk register and escalate the opportunity to a higher level. What should the project manager do next with this opportunity?",
 "opts":["No more action is required.","Share this opportunity with another project.","Establish a contingency reserve to support the opportunity.","Further monitor the opportunity"],
 "c":["C"],
 "e":"Per the source key, a contingency reserve is set aside so the opportunity can still be exploited/supported. (Note: in strict PMI practice, once a risk is escalated and accepted by a higher level, no further PM action is typically required — worth confirming via the Analyze-with-Claude button.)"},

{"src":"PMP 1","n":56,"lang":"en",
 "q":"A vendor informed the project manager that a critical resource will be on a long leave of absence. The project team reviewed the pending vendor deliverables and identified an alternative solution, but the solution will incur an additional cost. The project is currently on schedule and slightly under budget. What should the project manager do next?",
 "opts":["Perform a cost-benefit analysis.","Perform Integrated Change Control","Implement risk responses.","Request a replacement resource."],
 "c":["B"],
 "e":"Because the alternative solution adds cost, it changes the cost baseline and must go through Perform Integrated Change Control for evaluation and approval. Acting on the change without change control would bypass governance."},

{"src":"PMP 1","n":57,"lang":"en",
 "q":"The following table shows the business value created and the development effort required to implement various features in a product. (A: value 6, effort 2; B: value 10, effort 4; C: value 4, effort 4; D: value 8, effort 8.) Which feature should the team prioritize first?",
 "opts":["Feature A","Feature B","Feature C","Feature D"],
 "c":["A"],
 "e":"Prioritize by value-to-effort ratio: A = 6/2 = 3, B = 10/4 = 2.5, C = 4/4 = 1, D = 8/8 = 1. Feature A delivers the highest value for the least effort, so it is the top backlog item."},

{"src":"PMP 1","n":58,"lang":"en",
 "q":"Due to delays on some activities, one of the project team members has increased the scope without any approval. What should the project manager do next?",
 "opts":["Evaluate the impacts of the changes that were made to the project.","Update project documentation with the new scope.","Remove the changes to match the original requirements.","Add team members to the project to avoid more schedule delays."],
 "c":["A"],
 "e":"First evaluate the impact of the unauthorized scope change before deciding what to do, then route it through change control. Immediately accepting it, reverting it, or adding resources all act before understanding the impact."},

{"src":"PMP 1","n":59,"lang":"en",
 "q":"A multinational company has a plan to expand their business into a new country where they currently have no presence. A project manager is assigned to initiate this project. After reviewing the business case, what should be considered first when developing an appropriate implementation strategy?",
 "opts":["Identify and monitor the risks closely since the company does not have lessons learned for this country.","Evaluate the environmental and regulatory factors and identify high-level risks and assumptions.","Acquire project resources from the new country to mitigate the risk of uncertainty.","Ensure that project sponsors are in agreement on the project deliverables and timeline during the project."],
 "c":["B"],
 "e":"Entering a new country first requires understanding the enterprise environmental and regulatory factors, then identifying high-level risks and assumptions to shape the strategy. Detailed risk monitoring, hiring, or sponsor agreement come after that foundational assessment."},

{"src":"PMP 1","n":60,"lang":"en",
 "q":"A project manager is struggling to figure out the performance of the project teams in an agile environment. For the same scope of work, team A has calculated 100 story points and team B has calculated 125 story points. Which team is performing better?",
 "opts":["Team A as they have calculated fewer story points than team B.","Team B as they have calculated more story points than team A.","The team that completes the most stories selected from the sprint backlog.","The team with the least amount of defects in their deliverables."],
 "c":["D"],
 "e":"Story points are relative estimates and can't be compared across teams, so point counts don't indicate who performs better. Quality matters: the team with the fewest defects is delivering stories that are truly done and ready for customer use."},

{"src":"PMP 1","n":61,"lang":"en",
 "q":"A senior business stakeholder with many years of experience is pressuring an agile team to deliver most of the promised functionality to the customer 18 months in the future as opposed to multiple incremental deliveries over the course of the 18 months. What should the project lead do next?",
 "opts":["Work with the agile team to schedule the incremental deliveries to enable delivery of business value sooner to the customer.","Schedule meetings with the customer to determine their ability to support multiple incremental deliveries.","Provide details of the concern to the agile team and adjust their goals and release plans to conform to the stakeholder's request.","Work with the stakeholder to understand what the concerns are while, at the same time, conveying the benefits of incremental deliveries."],
 "c":["D"],
 "e":"Engage the stakeholder to understand their concern and educate them on the value of incremental delivery (earlier value, feedback, lower risk). Simply complying with the big-bang request, or acting without understanding the concern, abandons agile's core benefit."},

{"src":"PMP 1","n":62,"lang":"en",
 "q":"The change control board (CCB) rejects a change request submitted by a subject matter expert (SME). The SME refuses to accept this rejection and does not want to continue the project without the change. What should the project manager have done to avoid this situation?",
 "opts":["Submitted the change request directly to the CCB.","Requested that the sponsor approved the change request first.","Ensured that the change request was aligned with the project scope.","Assessed the change's overall impact to the project before submission."],
 "c":["D"],
 "e":"Assessing the change's full impact before submitting it would have surfaced why it might be rejected and set realistic expectations with the SME. Skipping impact analysis led to an unprepared submission and the SME's frustration."},

{"src":"PMP 1","n":63,"lang":"en",
 "q":"A company starts implementing Scrum. About halfway through the first sprint, communication issues develop. Some project team members are not in sync with other team members. What is the cause for this communication gap?",
 "opts":["The communications management plan has not been developed.","The project status dashboard has not been updated.","Daily standup meetings were not held or enforced.","The project sprint board has not been updated."],
 "c":["A"],
 "e":"Every project needs a communications management plan that sets out team meetings, channels, and timing known to all; its absence is the root cause of members being out of sync. Dashboards, boards, and standups are mechanisms that flow from that plan."},

{"src":"PMP 1","n":64,"lang":"en",
 "q":"After a project has been approved, a key stakeholder tells the project manager that the current project management strategy is not well defined. The project manager is also informed that the key stakeholder does not want to hold a working session. What should the project manager do first?",
 "opts":["Proceed with a working session for teams that do not belong to that key stakeholder.","Ask the project sponsor to exert authority on the key stakeholder in order to maintain the approved timeline.","Invite the key stakeholder to the project's change control board (CCB) to discuss adjustment of the project strategy.","Share the project documents and invite the key stakeholder to discuss any concerns."],
 "c":["D"],
 "e":"Share the relevant documents and invite the stakeholder to discuss their concerns, engaging them collaboratively even though they declined a formal session. Forcing authority through the sponsor or routing to the CCB escalates instead of first understanding the concern."},

{"src":"PMP 1","n":65,"lang":"en",
 "q":"During the execution of a project, the project manager notices that only one team member has one of the technical skills needed to build the product. The project manager is afraid this might have a future impact on the quality and schedule, because most of the upcoming activities will require at least three people with this specific skill. What two actions should the project manager take to prevent the team from being unqualified? (Choose two)",
 "opts":["Teach the entire team the new skill.","Encourage working in pairs and knowledge sharing.","Increase the time estimates on the activities that require such abilities.","Facilitate a training event with an external trainer.","Exclude the activities from the roadmap."],
 "c":["B","D"],
 "e":"Pairing and knowledge sharing spreads the scarce skill across the team, and an external training event builds the needed capability in more members. The PM, as a servant leader/facilitator, arranges learning rather than personally teaching, padding estimates, or dropping the work."},

{"src":"PMP 1","n":66,"lang":"en",
 "q":"During the retrospective ceremony, the agile team is talking about the product owner's expectations not being met. What should the project manager have done at the beginning of the project?",
 "opts":["Communicated project vision and sprint goals clearly to the team.","Defined the expected agile team contribution in the creation of the backlog.","Assessed the capabilities of the agile team and planned for the required training.","Defined the team ground rules and shared project vision."],
 "c":["A"],
 "e":"Clearly communicating the project vision and sprint goals up front aligns the team's work with the product owner's expectations, preventing the mismatch. The other options address backlog roles, capability, or ground rules rather than the shared goal/vision that drives meeting expectations."},

{"src":"PMP 1","n":67,"lang":"en",
 "q":"A project team with members from many different countries is struggling to cooperate. The project manager accepted these difficulties during the storming phase of team development, but the team has not moved to the next phase. The project is beginning to fall behind schedule. What can the project manager do to move the team to the norming phase?",
 "opts":["Figure out who is behind the issues and apply progressive disciplinary techniques.","Show the team the schedule impact of their communication issues and encourage them to put their differences aside.","Plan social activities to help foster stronger interpersonal relationships and identify shared goals.","Speak with the project sponsor about changing the team composition."],
 "c":["C"],
 "e":"Team-building/social activities build the interpersonal relationships and shared goals that move a team from storming to norming. Discipline, schedule pressure, or replacing members does not develop the cohesion the team needs to reach cooperation."},

{"src":"PMP 1","n":68,"lang":"en",
 "q":"As a member of an organizational change management team, a project manager must pass on any changes to the project team. The project manager needs to avoid any misunderstandings or resistance to help ensure that the project team will support any organizational change. What should the project manager do?",
 "opts":["Meet with the team on a regular basis to ensure they are aware of changes.","Check with the team members on their willingness to change.","Conduct meetings with resistant members to facilitate smooth project implementation.","Influence the team to communicate any changes."],
 "c":["A"],
 "e":"Meeting the team regularly keeps everyone continuously informed and aligned as changes occur, preventing misunderstanding and resistance. Targeting only resistant members or checking willingness is reactive and partial compared to consistent, proactive communication."},

{"src":"PMP 1","n":69,"lang":"en",
 "q":"A new resource has joined an iterative project team. The project manager notices that the resource is unable to complete the deliverables on time. How should the project manager address this situation?",
 "opts":["Provide performance feedback as part of the retrospective ceremony.","Request a more efficient replacement resource from the project sponsor.","Address the lack of performance by coaching the new team member.","Discuss options to improve performance with the resource manager."],
 "c":["C"],
 "e":"For a new member still ramping up, the servant-leader PM coaches them directly to improve performance. Replacing them, escalating to the resource manager, or waiting for the retrospective skips the immediate coaching they need."},

{"src":"PMP 1","n":70,"lang":"en",
 "q":"A project manager is assigned to a project within a program. The high-level requirements of the project are known, but the priorities of some of the requirements are uncertain as they are interdependent on some other pipeline projects within the program. How should the project manager prepare for the next steps of the project?",
 "opts":["Wait for the other project estimates before planning the schedule.","Prioritize and estimate the milestones for the high-level requirements based on historical data.","Ask the project management office (PMO) to estimate the tasks for all the projects and share the project management plan.","Escalate to the management team to increase the timeline based on the complexity of the project."],
 "c":["B"],
 "e":"Use rolling-wave planning: prioritize and estimate the known high-level requirements at the milestone level using historical data, refining as dependencies clarify. Waiting on other projects or escalating stalls progress that can begin now."},

{"src":"PMP 1","n":71,"lang":"en",
 "q":"A project for Company A was successfully delivered within scope, schedule, and budget by the end of the last iteration. However, during the project celebration ceremony, one of the business stakeholders says they perceive the project as a failure as it did not add any business value. What should the project manager have done to avoid this perception?",
 "opts":["Confirmed that there is a communications management plan in place to make business stakeholders aware of the success.","Involved the business stakeholders in the sprint review.","Defined product backlog priorities with the sponsor and key stakeholders to deliver business benefits.","Implemented a closing survey for key stakeholders."],
 "c":["C"],
 "e":"Prioritizing the backlog with the sponsor and key stakeholders ensures the work delivers real business value, which is what was missing despite hitting scope/schedule/budget. Communication plans, reviews, or surveys don't fix a backlog that wasn't aligned to business benefits."},

{"src":"PMP 1","n":72,"lang":"en",
 "q":"A project manager is approaching the end of a project, and several tasks are now practically complete and ready for handover to the client. How should the project manager proceed?",
 "opts":["Update the quality management plan for the overall project.","Close out the project tasks that have been completed.","Update the communications management plan for the overall project.","Ask the client to verify and accept the tasks that have been completed."],
 "c":["D"],
 "e":"Deliverables must be formally verified and accepted by the customer before they can be closed out. Closing tasks before client acceptance reverses the correct order."},

{"src":"PMP 1","n":73,"lang":"en",
 "q":"A company that is heavily focused on delivering projects using predictive approaches on-boards a new project manager who uses hybrid approaches. The scope of the project contains a number of unclear requirements. How should the project manager plan the delivery of the project?",
 "opts":["Escalate the issue of unclear requirements to the management team and wait for final authorization.","Break down the requirements and prioritize the requirements into iterative work packages.","Ignore the unclear requirements and focus on the delivery plan of the project.","Wait for the requirements to be more clear before any further action is taken on the project."],
 "c":["B"],
 "e":"Unclear, evolving requirements suit an iterative/agile approach: break them into prioritized iterative work packages and refine as you learn. Escalating, ignoring, or waiting stalls progress instead of adapting to the uncertainty."},

{"src":"PMP 1","n":74,"lang":"en",
 "q":"While managing a project, the project manager has just noticed that the last several monthly invoices have been challenged by the client when they were clearly defined. The actual invoice payments received have also been late and were outside of the contracted payment schedule. Recent invoices have only been partially paid. What should the project manager do?",
 "opts":["Add nonpayment to the issue log and keep tracking payments.","Hold a meeting with the client to address the issue.","Review past projects for payment-related lessons learned.","Conduct a meeting with the project team to update the procurement management plan."],
 "c":["B"],
 "e":"Meet the client directly to understand and resolve the payment disputes. Merely logging the issue, reviewing old lessons learned, or updating an internal plan does not address the client relationship driving the nonpayment."},

{"src":"PMP 1","n":75,"lang":"en",
 "q":"During the planning stage of a project the project manager realizes that a standard stakeholder engagement approach will not suffice. One of the client representatives, who is not a key decision maker, is extremely opinionated. This client representative could become a roadblock to progress due to their perceived level of authority during meetings. How should the project manager handle this moving forward?",
 "opts":["Update the project schedule to cater to this particular stakeholder.","Update the risk register to consider the possible project impacts.","Allocate time to gain buy-in from the stakeholder prior to key decision meetings.","Ask that only key decision makers attend the project meetings."],
 "c":["C"],
 "e":"Proactively gaining the opinionated stakeholder's buy-in before key meetings converts a potential roadblock into cooperation. Excluding them or only logging a risk does not engage the stakeholder, and adjusting the schedule misses the point."},

{"src":"PMP 1","n":76,"lang":"en",
 "q":"Two weeks after the approval of the project management plan for a global project, the project manager noticed that it was approved based on a different level of understanding by international stakeholders and is not what the project manager presented for approval. What should the project manager have done to prevent this from happening?",
 "opts":["Ran separate kick-off meetings for each culture and time zone.","Validated each stakeholders understanding during the kick-off meeting.","Sent the meeting minutes to the stakeholders after the kick-off meeting.","Reviewed all the approvals immediately after the kick-off meeting was over."],
 "c":["B"],
 "e":"Confirming each stakeholder's understanding during the kick-off ensures everyone interprets the plan the same way before approval, preventing divergent understandings. Minutes, separate meetings, or after-the-fact reviews don't verify comprehension at the moment it matters."},

{"src":"PMP 1","n":77,"lang":"en",
 "q":"During the course of a project, a junior team member approaches the project manager for some direction and advice on risk mitigation. How should the project manager respond?",
 "opts":["Refer the junior team member to the senior team member who develops the risk mitigation strategy.","Inform the junior team member that risk mitigation is not part of their work.","Offer guidance and follow up with some developmental activity for the junior team member.","Tell the junior team member that the information is available in lessons learned."],
 "c":["C"],
 "e":"A servant-leader PM guides and develops team members, so offering direction plus a developmental activity grows the junior member's risk skills. Deflecting them to others or dismissing the request misses a coaching opportunity."},

{"src":"PMP 1","n":78,"lang":"en",
 "q":"The project team follows a hybrid framework for its delivery. During the execution of a project, the team learns of a new compliance requirement that has to be delivered before any other requirements. How should the project manager address this situation?",
 "opts":["Collaborate with the compliance team member to review and prioritize the requirement's delivery.","Include the compliance lead in the stakeholders list and wait for the next status meeting.","Add the new compliance requirement to the backlog as the technical team does not have any capacity.","Ask the team to include the compliance requirement in the current sprint and deliver it."],
 "c":["A"],
 "e":"Collaborate with the compliance expert to properly review and prioritize the mandatory requirement so it's delivered first and correctly. Waiting for a status meeting, dumping it in the backlog, or forcing it into the current sprint without analysis are weaker responses."},

{"src":"PMP 1","n":79,"lang":"en",
 "q":"A vendor supplying a piece of specialized equipment for a project has reported the potential for a delivery delay due to a technical issue with the sub-supplier. To stay on schedule, the vendor proposed replacing the sub-supplier's component with a similar part that is different from the one that is stipulated in the project specifications. What should the project manager do next?",
 "opts":["Insist on using the component per the project specifications and air freight the equipment to mitigate the delay.","Accept the proposed replacement in order to keep procurement activities on track.","Instruct the vendor to use the part that is compliant with the project specifications and update the schedule.","Consult with a subject matter expert (SME) to see if the proposed change is acceptable."],
 "c":["D"],
 "e":"Consult an SME to determine whether the substitute part is technically acceptable before deciding; that informs whether to allow it or require the compliant part. Accepting or rejecting outright without expert assessment risks quality or unnecessary delay."},

{"src":"PMP 1","n":80,"lang":"en",
 "q":"Some members of a project team are frustrated by a new team member who previously worked in a Scrum setting. This new team member approaches members of the project team on a daily basis in what appears to be an attempt to engage in individual standup meetings. What should the project manager do?",
 "opts":["Mentor the new team member on the methodology the project team is using.","Schedule a meeting for the project team to review the communications management plan.","Send an email to the project team outlining the communications strategy.","Tell the rest of the project team to continue discussions with the new team member."],
 "c":["A"],
 "e":"The new member is applying Scrum habits that don't fit this team's methodology, so the PM mentors them on the approach actually in use. A team-wide meeting or email doesn't address the individual's need to learn the current way of working."},

{"src":"PMP 1","n":81,"lang":"en",
 "q":"During a retrospective review, many project team members say they are not completing tasks during the iterations because of interruptions and requests for help from other teams. As a team facilitator, what should the project manager do to avoid this?",
 "opts":["Reunite with the project team members affected and tell them not to reduce the team's capacity.","Identify the problem's root causes and define the ground rules with all project team members to minimize interferences.","Recommend to the project team members that they should improve their planning and make their stories smaller.","Hire a coach to help all project teams to improve their abilities to solve problems on their own."],
 "c":["B"],
 "e":"Find the root cause of the interruptions and set team ground rules to protect focus, addressing the actual problem collaboratively. Blaming capacity, telling them to plan better, or hiring a coach doesn't tackle the external interference."},

{"src":"PMP 1","n":82,"lang":"en",
 "q":"A transportation company is transforming its operational processes and the main system this company uses to move freight. The scope statement is in place. An iterative delivery approach has been agreed by all stakeholders. What does the project manager need to do next to help the team understand the work that needs to be done?",
 "opts":["Start working on the project management plan with work packages, a detailed schedule, and work assignments for team members.","Work directly with stakeholders on the work breakdown structure (WBS) and assign tasks with a required timeline for each team member to start the work.","Work with the team to decompose the scope into a WBS and work packages in order to create required deliverables and timelines.","Create the requirements management plan to outline how project requirements will be collected, analyzed, and documented, and then assign requirements to team members."],
 "c":["C"],
 "e":"With the scope statement already in place, the next step is to work with the team to decompose the scope into a WBS and work packages, defining the deliverables and timelines. Building a full detailed plan or doing the WBS without the team skips the collaborative breakdown that helps the team understand the work."},

{"src":"PMP 1","n":83,"lang":"en",
 "q":"A project manager is assigned to lead a newly formed agile team. The functional manager has assigned tasks to team members, but the team members do not feel empowered. How can the project manager promote empowerment and accountability among the team?",
 "opts":["Assign the tasks individually based on the role of the team member to ensure expertise on the subject.","Encourage and assign the team to perform demos of their products or software with the customers.","Give the team autonomy to make their own decisions on how to perform the tasks.","Encourage the team to keep the product owner involved when choosing ways to resolve the given task."],
 "c":["C"],
 "e":"Giving the team autonomy over how they do the work transfers ownership and builds the empowerment and accountability that self-organizing agile teams need. Assigning tasks top-down keeps control with managers and does the opposite of empowering."},

{"src":"PMP 1","n":84,"lang":"en",
 "q":"A newly formed team has become accustomed to agile practices. The project lead has noticed that while they are performing according to expectations, there is boredom with daily team practices among many of the team members. What should the project lead do in this situation?",
 "opts":["Allow the team to self-organize and have them analyze the situation in their retrospective session and self correct.","Challenge the team to find new ways to achieve higher levels of performance to improve the situation.","Speak with individual team members to determine what they would like to do to improve the situation in the team.","Have the team inform senior management of the situation and ask for recommendations."],
 "c":["A"],
 "e":"Self-organizing teams use the retrospective to inspect and adapt their own practices, so let them analyze the boredom and self-correct. Imposing challenges, handling it one-on-one, or escalating to management bypasses the team's own improvement mechanism."},

{"src":"PMP 1","n":85,"lang":"en",
 "q":"After reviewing the project management plan with key stakeholders, the project manager was told that an 18-month release plan was unacceptable and the product should launch in six months. Based on the new timeline, what should the project manager do?",
 "opts":["Review the project backlog looking for high-priority items and come up with a minimum viable product (MVP) that fits the expected timeline.","Go over the critical path with stakeholders, explaining why it is not possible to deliver all the scope in the expected timeline.","Seek approval with the sponsor to triple the project budget, hiring more people to compensate for the compressed schedule.","Plan for overtime, apply crashing and fast tracking, and share the risk of failure with project stakeholders."],
 "c":["A"],
 "e":"Define an MVP from the highest-priority backlog items to deliver the most valuable subset within the six months. Insisting it's impossible, tripling budget, or crashing/overtime are less effective than scoping to an MVP in an agile delivery."},

{"src":"PMP 1","n":86,"lang":"en",
 "q":"A team has been struggling with various issues during the course of an iteration. The project lead facilitates a discussion of the issues and the team forms new team agreements. During the next iteration, some of the same issues appear again. What should the project lead do next?",
 "opts":["Let the team self-organize and determine the best means to prevent the issues from occurring again.","Challenge the team to determine if the issues surfaced again because agreements were not followed.","Post the agreements in a conspicuous place where all of the team members can see them.","Notify senior management of the issues, and have them review the importance of agreements with the team."],
 "c":["C"],
 "e":"Since the same issues recurred, making the agreements highly visible keeps them top-of-mind so the team actually follows them. Re-delegating to self-organize again, just questioning the team, or escalating to management doesn't reinforce the agreements as effectively."},

{"src":"PMP 1","n":87,"lang":"en",
 "q":"A member of the board of directors is the sponsor of a project that is important to the organization. This stakeholder has many other priorities and is not currently engaged in the project activities. How should the stakeholder be engaged in the project?",
 "opts":["Consult with the stakeholder and increase their level of interest.","Involve the stakeholder in governance and decision making.","The stakeholder needs to be made aware of the project progress.","Keep the stakeholder informed and consult with them based on their needs."],
 "c":["D"],
 "e":"A busy senior sponsor is best engaged by keeping them informed and consulting them according to their specific needs, matching engagement to their availability and role. Forcing more interest or pulling them into day-to-day governance ignores their limited bandwidth."},

{"src":"PMP 1","n":88,"lang":"en",
 "q":"A stakeholder on a project has a reputation for being easily angered and unreasonably difficult on projects. The project manager would like to minimize the impact of the stakeholder's behavior on the development team. What training should the project manager choose for their team to help with this situation?",
 "opts":["Emotional intelligence training","Communication training","Agile training","Negotiation training"],
 "c":["A"],
 "e":"Emotional intelligence training helps the team recognize and manage emotions—their own and the stakeholder's—reducing the impact of difficult behavior. Communication or negotiation training is narrower and doesn't build the self-awareness and empathy EI provides."},

{"src":"PMP 1","n":89,"lang":"en",
 "q":"In an industrial plant, the owner has decided to add more capacity by installing a new machine and updating the software used in production. This capacity enlargement will occur while the plant is still functioning and producing. The software update will be accomplished using an agile approach in order to minimize risk. The machine procurement, installation, and integration will be accomplished using predictive approaches. What types of communications will the project manager have to design into the schedule management plan to ensure the project will be on schedule?",
 "opts":["Weekly colocated meetings with the relevant stakeholders","Biweekly written status reports from the relevant stakeholders","Weekly one-on-one meetings with each of the relevant stakeholders","Biweekly conference calls with the relevant stakeholders"],
 "c":["D"],
 "e":"Biweekly conference calls give an efficient, regular communication cadence that keeps distributed stakeholders coordinated across the hybrid effort. Colocated or one-on-one weekly meetings are heavier and less practical, and written reports alone lack interactive coordination."},

{"src":"PMP 1","n":90,"lang":"en",
 "q":"A marketing team is developing ideas in order to revamp an existing product. The marketing executive reaches out to the project manager to make sure that necessary measures are in place for a successful launch. On what should the project manager focus?",
 "opts":["Ensuring that the scope management plan is in place to cover all related objectives.","Ensuring that the team is also focused on the overall change management plan.","Planning for regular project updates to cover the status of critical deliverables.","Determining and aligning performance indicators that will help in assessing successful delivery."],
 "c":["D"],
 "e":"Defining and aligning the right performance indicators (KPIs/success metrics) makes 'successful launch' measurable and keeps the team focused on outcomes. Scope plans, change plans, and status updates are supporting activities, not the measure of launch success."},

{"src":"PMP 1","n":91,"lang":"en",
 "q":"During the review of a project's list of deliverables, one of the stakeholders expressed concern that one of the requirements will not be addressed. What should the project manager do?",
 "opts":["Work with the stakeholder to improve the requirements' acceptance criteria.","Review the requirements traceability matrix with the concerned stakeholder.","Discuss with the stakeholder which additional requirements should be added.","Check the approved list of requirements to see if there are any missing items."],
 "c":["B"],
 "e":"The requirements traceability matrix maps each requirement to its deliverables, so reviewing it with the stakeholder shows whether the requirement is actually covered. Adjusting acceptance criteria or adding requirements assumes a gap before confirming one exists."},

{"src":"PMP 1","n":92,"lang":"en",
 "q":"The rollout of a new finance system is in the execution phase. A number of issues have surfaced in the last month that are slowing the project down. A new project sponsor has recently started with the company but has not had time to meet the project manager. The project is at the point where the issues are significantly impacting delivery of the project. How should the project manager alert the new project sponsor about these concerns?",
 "opts":["Complete a risk analysis outlining the delays and impacts, and email a copy of the risk register, urging the project sponsor to respond.","Send an email to the project sponsor summarizing the project status and key concerns, and request an immediate face-to-face meeting to discuss them.","Send an email invitation to the project sponsor to attend all of the project team's weekly meetings, and hope the sponsor will find time to attend.","Continue emailing updated project status reports highlighting the key risks and issues, and wait for the project sponsor to request a meeting."],
 "c":["B"],
 "e":"Summarize the situation and request an immediate face-to-face meeting—direct conversation is the most effective way to brief a new sponsor on serious, time-sensitive issues. Emailing a register and hoping for a response, or waiting passively, delays the urgent engagement needed."},

{"src":"PMP 1","n":93,"lang":"en",
 "q":"A project manager has been selected to deliver one of an organization's key flagship solutions. Critical success factors include agility, market adaptation, and a quick response to market offerings. What is one of the key activities that the project manager should undertake to ensure company success?",
 "opts":["Review the project objectives with the project sponsor to agree on more realistic and achievable project plans and deliverables.","Request more funding from the project sponsor to hire additional skilled resources and to incorporate additional quality in the project delivery approach and outcome.","Conduct periodical reviews of the project plans, objectives, and deliverables to ensure all relevant data are captured to continue making informed project decisions.","Create a business case after performing a market review, and then submit it to the sponsor to modify the project deliverable to meet the newly defined objectives."],
 "c":["C"],
 "e":"Periodic reviews of plans, objectives, and deliverables keep the project responsive to changing market conditions, supporting agility and informed decisions. Renegotiating objectives, requesting more funding, or building a new business case don't provide the continuous inspect-and-adapt cadence agility requires."},

{"src":"PMP 1","n":94,"lang":"en",
 "q":"A product owner presented the backlog to the team, and the team raised concerns over implementing some of the features due to various impediments. What should the project manager suggest that the product owner do next?",
 "opts":["Estimate the financial impact of the impediments and request additional funding.","Capture the impediments and inform management that their assistance is needed in resolving the impediments.","Ensure that the impediments are captured and prioritized based upon the highest valued features.","Begin to remove the impediments, starting with the easiest ones first, to foster team accomplishment."],
 "c":["C"],
 "e":"The product owner should capture the impediments and prioritize addressing them based on the highest-value features, keeping the focus on delivering business value. Escalating to management, seeking funding, or tackling the easiest first ignores value-based prioritization."},

{"src":"PMP 1","n":95,"lang":"en",
 "q":"A project manager receives an update that the development flow is not going well. Some team members cannot start their work because the delivery is late. Looking at the Kanban board and the work in progress (WIP) lines, the project manager observes a bottleneck. The board columns are: New, Specification, Code, Test, Document, Done. Which queue/column is the bottleneck that requires additional resources to relieve it?",
 "opts":["Test","Code","Document","Specification"],
 "c":["B"],
 "e":"On a Kanban board the bottleneck is the column where work accumulates against its WIP limit and blocks downstream flow—here the Code column—so that is where extra resources are needed. Reading the WIP and queue lengths identifies where to add capacity."},

{"src":"PMP 1","n":96,"lang":"en",
 "q":"A project manager is managing an innovation project for a big corporation. The project manager is planning to use email as the main communication channel; however, the product owner prefers to use another approved communication tool instead of email. What should the project manager do?",
 "opts":["Update the communications management plan based on the product owner's preferences and distribute to the team.","Request the product owner to use email because using another tool will increase the workload.","Suggest to the team to start using the communication tool suggested by the product owner.","Contact the sponsor to request direction regarding the communication tool to be used by the project team."],
 "c":["A"],
 "e":"Accommodate the stakeholder's valid preference by updating the communications management plan with the approved tool and sharing it with the team. Insisting on email, informally suggesting a switch, or escalating to the sponsor are weaker than formally updating the plan."},

{"src":"PMP 1","n":97,"lang":"en",
 "q":"During the design phase of a project, the project manager notices that the schedule performance index (SPI) is 1.8 and the cost performance index (CPI) is 0.1. In a weekly meeting with top management, the project manager reports concerns about these indicators. However, the most concerned person was the project sponsor who said that if the trend continues, they will be forced to prematurely close the project. What should the project manager do?",
 "opts":["Stop crashing the schedule.","Monitor the project sponsor, but understand that it is normal to spend more money in the design phase of a project.","Use a resource-leveling technique.","Manage the project sponsor closely and revise the project's resource management plan to improve indicators."],
 "c":["A"],
 "e":"SPI 1.8 means well ahead of schedule and CPI 0.1 means severely over budget, a classic sign of over-spending to accelerate. Stopping schedule crashing eases the cost overrun since the project is already far ahead of schedule. The other options don't address the crash-driven cost blowout."},

{"src":"PMP 1","n":98,"lang":"en",
 "q":"While reviewing prototypes, the quality team found some deviations that do not affect the main functionality but have the potential to be an issue in the future. To avoid major problems in the future, the team recommends corrective actions that will require additional funds. The project is already struggling to stay on budget and has even used some of the contingency reserves. What should the project manager do?",
 "opts":["Report to the project sponsor that the product is working and the project is struggling with the budget.","Ask the quality team to discard the sample and test again with a new sample.","Ask the product designer why the deviation was not considered in the design phase.","Evaluate and initiate the change request process with the stakeholders."],
 "c":["D"],
 "e":"Corrective actions that require additional funds change the cost baseline, so they must go through the change request/change control process with stakeholders. Reporting, retesting, or assigning blame doesn't properly authorize the funding and change."},

{"src":"PMP 1","n":99,"lang":"en",
 "q":"Development team members are located in three different time zones. It is difficult to find a suitable time to hold the daily standup for all team members. What should the project manager do?",
 "opts":["Give up the daily standup and replace it with a daily report.","Let the team members decide themselves if they want to join the meeting.","Require all team members to join the daily standup regardless of the meeting time.","Encourage frequent, small team meetings with two or three team members."],
 "c":["C"],
 "e":"The daily standup is essential for synchronization, so all members should attend regardless of time zone (with a mutually agreed time). Dropping it, making it optional, or splitting into fragments breaks the whole-team coordination the standup provides."},

{"src":"PMP 1","n":100,"lang":"en",
 "q":"A project manager is leading a project that is in an advanced stage. All high-level risks identified in the risk management plan have been resolved or are no longer a risk for the project. Only low-level risks remain. What should the project manager do now?",
 "opts":["Reduce the priority of project risks as all remaining risks are low-level.","Reevaluate identified risks and update the risk register.","Notify stakeholders that all high-level risks have been resolved.","Reclassify low-level risks as high-level risks."],
 "c":["B"],
 "e":"Risk management is continuous: reassess the remaining risks (and watch for new ones) and update the risk register accordingly. Blanket-reducing priority, only notifying, or arbitrarily reclassifying risks ignores ongoing risk reassessment."},

{"src":"PMP 1","n":101,"lang":"en",
 "q":"The product team is progressing with a prototyping approach to deliver a multiyear business initiative. A few user stories are taking longer to be delivered. What should the project manager do?",
 "opts":["Determine the risks and identify a resolution during the retrospective meeting.","Determine cross-dependencies and plan a spike in the next sprint.","Inform stakeholders about the delay during project updates.","Discover the gaps in the communications management plan and address them accordingly."],
 "c":["A"],
 "e":"The retrospective is the agile ceremony for inspecting what's slowing delivery and agreeing on improvements, so use it to surface the risks/causes and identify resolutions. Jumping to a spike, just informing stakeholders, or blaming communications skips the team's own inspect-and-adapt step."},

{"src":"PMP 1","n":102,"lang":"en",
 "q":"During the execution of a project within an operations function, the project manager encounters scope changes and a delay in the delivery of critical tasks. How should the project manager address this situation?",
 "opts":["Initiate the change request to modify the scope and adjust the timelines.","Call for a team meeting to decide if the changes should be included.","Communicate the concerns to the project management office (PMO) during the weekly project reports.","Conduct an impact analysis and present the results to the steering committee for approval."],
 "c":["D"],
 "e":"Analyze the impact of the scope changes and delay, then present the findings to the steering committee/CCB for an approval decision. Initiating a change request before analyzing impact, or deciding informally in a team meeting, skips the proper evaluation and governance."},

{"src":"PMP 1","n":103,"lang":"en",
 "q":"A key stakeholder for a construction project has been spending a lot of time at the site and interrupting the team's efforts. What should the project manager do in this situation?",
 "opts":["Work to understand the key stakeholder's concerns and provide feedback in regular project status reports.","Arrange an ad hoc meeting to address the key stakeholder's concerns with team members.","Escalate the situation to the project sponsor because the key stakeholder's behavior is impacting progress.","Reinforce with the key stakeholder that the project is performing on schedule."],
 "c":["B"],
 "e":"A direct meeting to understand and address the stakeholder's concerns resolves the interruptions collaboratively. Escalating to the sponsor or just citing schedule performance avoids the direct engagement that actually addresses the concern."},

{"src":"PMP 1","n":104,"lang":"en",
 "q":"During an ongoing project, a key resource asks the project manager about the project purpose and if it is aligned with the organization's strategy. What should the project manager share with the key resource?",
 "opts":["The updated project management plan","The updated communications management plan","The updated benefits management plan","The updated scope management plan"],
 "c":["C"],
 "e":"The benefits management plan describes the project's intended benefits and how they align with organizational strategy, which is exactly what the resource is asking about. The other plans cover execution mechanics, not strategic value."},

{"src":"PMP 1","n":105,"lang":"en",
 "q":"As part of the strategic plan, a company decides to implement a new software platform to manage a centralized document repository. Some requirements are clear and some need more detail. What should the project manager do first to decide how to manage this project?",
 "opts":["Determine the most appropriate life cycle approach for the project.","Register all requirements into a backlog for further detailed analysis.","Select a predictive development life cycle based on the team's experience.","Split the requirements, put them into two separate projects, and apply different approaches."],
 "c":["C"],
 "e":"Per the source key, because part of the work is already clear, the team starts with a predictive life cycle. (Note: many PMI practitioners would instead choose 'determine the most appropriate life cycle approach' first—assess before committing—so use the Analyze-with-Claude button to compare reasoning.)"},

{"src":"PMP 1","n":106,"lang":"en",
 "q":"A project manager is leading a project which shows a trend to exceed the cost baseline. What should the project manager do first to manage the budget?",
 "opts":["Inform the stakeholders that the project will be finished over budget.","Ask the project sponsor for assistance in getting the budget back on track.","Meet with the project team to analyze the actual cost to determine deviations.","Issue a change request including the analysis to increase the budget."],
 "c":["C"],
 "e":"First analyze the actual costs with the team to understand the deviations and their causes before taking action. Informing stakeholders of an overrun, escalating, or requesting a budget increase all happen before the cost variance is understood."},

{"src":"PMP 1","n":107,"lang":"en",
 "q":"During project execution, the risk owner is monitoring the critical speed rate of a power engine to eventually trigger the defined risk-response strategy. It was defined that the power engine has the following characteristics: 1,600 rpm stops working and crashes the system; 1,500 rpm warns of max rate allowed; 1,400 rpm reduces the power and brakes the engine. Which of the following statements is correct?",
 "opts":["1600 rpm is a project issue; 1500 rpm is an event that triggers a response strategy; 1400 rpm is a project risk.","1600 rpm is a project issue; 1500 rpm is a project risk; 1400 rpm is an event that triggers a strategy.","1600 rpm is an event that triggers a response strategy; 1500 rpm is a project issue; 1400 rpm is a project risk.","1600 rpm is a project risk; 1500 rpm is an event that triggers a response strategy; 1400 rpm is a project issue."],
 "c":["A"],
 "e":"At 1,600 rpm the failure has already happened (system crash) = an issue; 1,500 rpm is the warning event that triggers the response strategy; 1,400 rpm is the potential future event (engine brakes) = a risk. An issue has occurred; a risk has not yet occurred; the trigger is the threshold event."},

{"src":"PMP 1","n":108,"lang":"en",
 "q":"A project manager is leading a project with several stakeholders in other functional areas of the company. One of these stakeholders has just been promoted to lead another functional area, and remains a stakeholder for this project. What should the project manager have the project team update to ensure proper communications to stakeholders?",
 "opts":["Project management plan","Communications management plan","Stakeholder engagement plan","Stakeholder register"],
 "c":["C"],
 "e":"The promotion changes the stakeholder's power and influence, so the stakeholder engagement plan (which governs the register and engagement/salience analysis) must be updated to reflect the new role. The other plans don't capture the changed stakeholder influence."},

{"src":"PMP 1","n":109,"lang":"en",
 "q":"From previous lessons learned working with a client, a project manager notes that the client's turnaround times on documents sent for approval were very slow and well outside of the agreed timelines. At the initial kick-off meeting with the same client, how should the project manager approach this issue for the new project?",
 "opts":["Update the project risk register with the potential for delays.","Inform the client that extra resources will be added to give the client more time for reviews.","Inform the client of their responsibility in relation to document approval.","Work with the client on an acceptable amount of time for document approval."],
 "c":["D"],
 "e":"Collaboratively agreeing on realistic approval turnaround times at kick-off sets shared expectations and prevents the past delays. Simply logging a risk, adding resources, or lecturing the client on their responsibility doesn't reach a workable agreement."},

{"src":"PMP 1","n":110,"lang":"en",
 "q":"A project manager is tracking a project, but a key stakeholder will not accept the project's key performance indicator (KPI) results. Which tool or technique should the project manager use?",
 "opts":["Expert judgment","Change control tools","Autocratic decision making","Context diagram"],
 "c":["D"],
 "e":"A context diagram visualizes the system and its stakeholders' needs/interactions, helping align the stakeholder on scope and what the KPIs represent. Expert judgment, change control, or autocratic decisions don't clarify the stakeholder's understanding of the results."},

{"src":"PMP 1","n":111,"lang":"en",
 "q":"A predictive organization has recently decided to switch to an agile delivery approach. The ongoing projects will still be led by project managers. Because of unexpected budget overruns on project A, senior management is not confident that the agile project teams are ready to self-manage the project budget. Which two actions should the project manager of project A undertake? (Choose two)",
 "opts":["Invite senior management to the standup meetings to give a status report on the expenditures.","Develop escalation levels with senior management regarding expenditures.","Have the agile coach produce a weekly report on the project expenditures for senior management.","Invite senior management to the retrospective for each sprint to evaluate the expenditures.","Establish information radiators with the help of the agile coach to visualize the expenditures."],
 "c":["B","E"],
 "e":"Agreed escalation thresholds give management confidence without intruding on the team, and information radiators make expenditures transparent to everyone (an agile-friendly practice). Inviting management into standups/retrospectives or producing separate reports undermines the team's self-management and the agile ceremonies."},

{"src":"PMP 1","n":112,"lang":"en",
 "q":"A marketing team is planning for a multiyear initiative that will include a lot of cross-functional stakeholders. The project manager has been assigned and is now in the project planning phase. What item(s) should the project manager develop first?",
 "opts":["A business case and scope document","A product backlog and identification of the features to be delivered","A risk management plan to incorporate known risks","A communications management plan that addresses the team's needs"],
 "c":["D"],
 "e":"With many cross-functional stakeholders, a communications management plan that addresses everyone's needs is the priority to keep the diverse group aligned. The business case precedes planning, and backlog/risk planning come after communications are structured."},

{"src":"PMP 1","n":113,"lang":"en",
 "q":"How should the project manager develop the project management plan to ensure that the project continually delivers the expected benefits?",
 "opts":["Perform integration testing of each release.","Include the entire project team in testing.","Clarify project benefits with the sponsor.","Identify the goal of each work product."],
 "c":["C"],
 "e":"Clarifying the expected benefits with the sponsor early (ideally while the charter is developed) anchors the plan to the intended value so it continually delivers benefits. Testing activities or work-product goals don't establish the benefit baseline the plan should serve."},

{"src":"PMP 1","n":114,"lang":"en",
 "q":"On an agile project during a retrospective, the team comments that issues have been increasing over the last few iterations. However, the team feels that most of the issues could have been prevented. What should the project manager do?",
 "opts":["Create a task board to ensure effective management of issue resolution.","Ensure risks are documented within the project meeting space and are reviewed regularly.","Ensure that the daily standups are more effectively managed.","Implement a reliable solution to prevent issues from occurring."],
 "c":["C"],
 "e":"The daily standup is where the team surfaces obstacles and intentions early; running it more effectively catches preventable issues before they grow. A task board, risk logging, or a one-off 'reliable solution' don't provide the daily impediment-surfacing the standup does."},

{"src":"PMP 1","n":115,"lang":"en",
 "q":"By the end of the first sprint, a project lead notices that one of the team members is not performing very well. Which form of communication should the project lead use to handle the situation?",
 "opts":["Informal verbal communication, such as a conversation","Formal written communication, such as an email with a copy sent to the manager","Formal verbal communication, such as a conversation at the end of the sprint retrospective","Informal written communication, such as a friendly reminder"],
 "c":["C"],
 "e":"A formal verbal conversation (e.g., tied to the retrospective) addresses the performance issue directly and respectfully while keeping it constructive. Informal notes are too casual for a performance matter, and a formal email cc'ing the manager escalates prematurely."},

{"src":"PMP 1","n":116,"lang":"en",
 "q":"A consultancy firm is finally selected and contracted for a project following an extensive and competitive procurement process. A few months into project execution, the client asks the project manager why the consultant is contracted for only 18 months instead of the full 32-month-long project. The project manager will need to carry out an unplanned second bidding process to ensure services are in place until the end of the project. What should the project manager have done to prevent this?",
 "opts":["Included the client in all negotiation phases with the consultancy to obtain their advanced approval before signing the contract.","Prepared and negotiated a second contract with the consultancy firm for the remaining 14 months after the first contract signature.","Agreed beforehand with the client on the frequency of reporting on the status of the procurement activities within the project.","Developed a detailed, phased procurement management plan including all activities required and had the client approve it."],
 "c":["D"],
 "e":"A detailed, phased procurement management plan covering the full project duration, approved by the client, would have aligned the contracting to the 32-month need and avoided the surprise gap. The other options don't plan procurement across the whole project lifecycle."},

{"src":"PMP 1","n":117,"lang":"en",
 "q":"A project manager receives a suggestion from a team member for improving the product attributes without impacting the schedule or cost. This will add value to the project and the customer. What should the project manager do?",
 "opts":["Ask another team member to validate the impacts of the suggestion.","Ask the team member to submit a formal change request.","Accept the suggestion as it has no impact on the project.","Reject the suggestion and ask the team member to focus on executing the plan."],
 "c":["B"],
 "e":"In a predictive project, any change to the product/scope—even a beneficial one—must go through the formal change request and change control process. Accepting it directly (gold-plating) or rejecting it outright both bypass proper change control."},

{"src":"PMP 1","n":118,"lang":"en",
 "q":"A project team has been working together for about 2 years. One of the team members has consistently shown high performance and stood out among their peers during the execution of the project. How should the project manager recognize high performance?",
 "opts":["Ask the sponsor to approve a bonus for the high-performing team member.","Acknowledge the high-performing team member in the next team meeting.","Thank the team member privately to avoid conflict among the team members.","Choose a reward for the team member that aligns with their personal goals."],
 "c":["D"],
 "e":"Rewards tailored to the individual's personal goals are the most motivating form of recognition. A generic bonus, public acknowledgment, or a private thank-you are less effective than a reward that genuinely matters to that person."},

{"src":"PMP 1","n":119,"lang":"en",
 "q":"A project manager is leading a hybrid project and is collaborating with the client to develop the project contract. Some delays have been experienced due to unforeseen technical difficulties and new dependencies. However, the project manager is optimistic about recovering the delays in the upcoming sprints. A key stakeholder asked the project manager if the contract deadlines can be met. What should the project manager do first?",
 "opts":["Ask the stakeholder to remain calm and confirm that they are participating in all team meetings.","Study the contract terms and deadlines to ensure they are not under risk and confirm them with the stakeholder.","Clarify the current work progress, causes for delays, existing risks, and planned corrective actions.","Submit a request for extra time and budget related to the dependencies and technical difficulties."],
 "c":["C"],
 "e":"Provide the stakeholder a transparent picture of progress, delay causes, risks, and corrective actions so they understand the real status. Reassuring them, studying terms in isolation, or jumping to a time/budget request comes before clarifying the actual situation."},

{"src":"PMP 1","n":120,"lang":"en",
 "q":"There is frequent conflict between two project team members working on a project. This has caused a negative impact on the project. How should the project manager handle this situation?",
 "opts":["Counsel both team members and caution them with a final warning.","Help cultivate self-awareness between the two team members through emotional intelligence (EI).","Relocate the two project team members into different project teams.","Escalate the issue to the human resources (HR) manager before a larger problem develops."],
 "c":["B"],
 "e":"Building emotional intelligence and self-awareness helps the members understand and manage their behavior, resolving the conflict at its source. Warnings, separating them, or escalating to HR avoid developing the interpersonal skills that fix the recurring conflict."},

{"src":"PMP 1","n":121,"lang":"en",
 "q":"A team is preparing the closing phase and building a plan for the next phase. One of the project sponsors is complaining that the last phase had some quality deliverable issues. However, the quality acceptance document was signed. What should the project manager do?",
 "opts":["Review best practices and lessons learned and apply them to the project plan.","Schedule a quality review meeting and include the sponsor's complaints.","Reopen the quality acceptance documentation to add the sponsor's complaints.","Postpone the next project phase until the impact of the quality issues is assessed."],
 "c":["B"],
 "e":"Hold a quality review meeting (with QA) that addresses the sponsor's complaints to investigate and resolve them properly. Reopening signed acceptance documents or postponing the next phase overreacts, and only updating lessons learned doesn't engage the sponsor's concern now."},

{"src":"PMP 1","n":122,"lang":"en",
 "q":"A team member who is working on a critical project deliverable reported that their computer is not working. The IT department says that due to a high workload, they will not be able to provide assistance for 1 week. How should the project manager address this situation?",
 "opts":["Schedule a meeting with the project sponsor to request a budget to buy or rent a new computer.","Ask the team member to find an innovative solution or workaround to overcome the issue and avoid project delays.","Use project reserves to buy new equipment to avoid an impact on the critical path of the project.","Explore options with the IT department to expedite the necessary repairs based on the criticality of the project."],
 "c":["D"],
 "e":"Work with IT to expedite the repair by explaining the project's criticality—engaging the responsible function to solve the actual problem. Escalating for budget, pushing the workaround onto the team member, or spending reserves are premature before exploring expedited repair."},

{"src":"PMP 1","n":123,"lang":"en",
 "q":"A project is comprised of team members who are not engaged and committed to project success. What should the project manager do to address this situation?",
 "opts":["Send each team member a direct email indicating that disciplinary action is likely if they do not change their attitude.","Contact the project sponsor to request approval to release the current team and hire replacement personnel.","Accept the situation knowing there is schedule slack available to release on an incremental basis when necessary.","Call each team member to gain their perspective on the problem, then bring the whole team together to discuss a solution."],
 "c":["D"],
 "e":"Understand each member's perspective through direct conversation, then facilitate a team discussion to solve the engagement problem collaboratively. Threatening discipline, replacing the team, or accepting it does not address why the team is disengaged."},

{"src":"PMP 1","n":124,"lang":"en",
 "q":"A large multinational company works on a project in an emerging-economy country. All business matters are conducted in local currency. During the course of the project, the budget becomes the primary concern due to currency instability in the country. What should the project manager do next?",
 "opts":["Ask for additional budget from the client","Evaluate the situation with the team","Escalate the risk to the project sponsor","Use the project's contingency reserve"],
 "c":["D"],
 "e":"Currency instability is a known/identifiable risk, and contingency reserves exist precisely to fund the impact of such identified risks. Asking the client for more money, escalating, or just evaluating doesn't use the reserve that was set aside for this."},

{"src":"PMP 1","n":125,"lang":"en",
 "q":"A project with a hard timeline is starting its first iteration out of six. The project team is lacking the necessary skills to execute. What should the project manager do?",
 "opts":["Allow the team to proceed as they attempt to meet the timeline.","Fill the skill gap personally to execute the project.","Add a subject matter expert (SME) to the project team.","Invite stakeholders to discuss a mitigation plan to finish on time."],
 "c":["B"],
 "e":"Per the source key, the PM closes the gap between the team's current and desired skills (the illustration frames this as coaching). (Note: adding an SME to the team is also a defensible PMI answer for a skill gap—use the Analyze-with-Claude button to weigh coaching vs. augmenting the team.)"},

{"src":"PMP 1","n":126,"lang":"en",
 "q":"A project manager is working with the same project team as in the last two construction projects. The project manager managed this team on a micro level for the last two projects. However, now the project manager would like to use a different leadership style to allow the team to make their own decisions. What kind of leadership style should the project manager utilize for the current project?",
 "opts":["Transformational","Charismatic","Interactional","Laissez-faire"],
 "c":["A"],
 "e":"Transformational leadership inspires and empowers the team to take ownership and make their own decisions, the shift the PM wants from micromanaging. Charismatic and interactional differ in focus, and laissez-faire is too hands-off (no guidance at all)."},

{"src":"PMP 1","n":127,"lang":"en",
 "q":"During the closing process, the project manager learns that one deliverable is not meeting customer expectations. How should the project manager proceed?",
 "opts":["Contact the project sponsor and ask for help in negotiating the closing of the project with the customer.","Ask the customer to issue a change request with the information to analyze and provide a solution.","Meet with the customer to reach agreement on the scope of the deliverable that will satisfy both parties.","Review the deliverable requirements, check the customer approval criteria, and proceed accordingly."],
 "c":["D"],
 "e":"Compare the deliverable against its documented requirements and the customer's acceptance criteria to objectively determine whether it conforms, then proceed accordingly. Escalating, requesting a change, or renegotiating scope comes before confirming against the agreed criteria."},

{"src":"PMP 1","n":128,"lang":"en",
 "q":"In the past year, a company paid US$60,000 to an external subcontractor for an ongoing project. The project manager evaluates delivering it in-house using PERT, with an optimistic term of 4 months, a pessimistic term of 6 months, and a most likely term of 5 months. In-house resources: two engineers (US$700/month each), one project manager (US$1,600/month), and additional monthly expenses of US$2,000. Using PERT for the duration, how much money will the project manager estimate the company can save?",
 "opts":["US$20,000","US$35,000","US$30,000","US$40,000"],
 "c":["B"],
 "e":"PERT duration = (O + 4M + P)/6 = (4 + 4×5 + 6)/6 = 5 months. Monthly cost = 2×700 + 1,600 + 2,000 = US$5,000, so in-house cost = 5 × 5,000 = US$25,000. Savings = 60,000 − 25,000 = US$35,000."},

{"src":"PMP 1","n":129,"lang":"en",
 "q":"A company has started developing a new product. During execution, the project team notices that another competitor has launched a product with very similar functionalities and a cheaper market price. What should have been done differently to avoid this scenario?",
 "opts":["A minimum viable product (MVP) should have been released to get feedback from the market.","An iterative life cycle approach should have been adopted.","The Kanban method should have been utilized to optimize the work in progress (WIP).","More backlog refinement meetings should have been scheduled."],
 "c":["A"],
 "e":"Releasing an MVP early would have delivered to market sooner, generated real customer feedback, and created urgency—reducing the risk of being beaten by a competitor. Iterative cycles, Kanban, or more refinement don't get a usable product to market as quickly as an MVP."},

{"src":"PMP 1","n":130,"lang":"en",
 "q":"A project manager has just been appointed to a project that has two major deliverables. Both deliverables have the same level of priority and are to be delivered at the same time. The members of the team are complaining about the lack of information regarding which deliverable needs to be finished first. What should the project manager do?",
 "opts":["Ask key stakeholders to cancel the project due to the difficulty of executing both deliverables at the same time.","Ask the team to start working on the easiest deliverable first so they gain experience to execute the second deliverable.","Require that the project team execute both deliverables at the same time since this was requested and approved in the project charter.","Meet with the project sponsor and key stakeholders to assess each deliverable's value to the organization and set execution priorities."],
 "c":["D"],
 "e":"Work with the sponsor and key stakeholders to assess each deliverable's business value and agree on execution priorities, giving the team clear direction. Canceling, picking the easiest first, or forcing simultaneous delivery ignores value-based prioritization."},

{"src":"PMP 1","n":131,"lang":"en",
 "q":"A project manager is assigned to a critical software development project. The team is using a hybrid approach and has divided development into four sprints. At the end of sprint three, the project manager discovers that the earned value (EV) is US$500,000 and the planned value (PV) is US$550,000. What should the project manager do?",
 "opts":["Fast-track the project.","Introduce a float.","Adjust the budget.","Adjust the project timeline."],
 "c":["A"],
 "e":"EV (500k) is less than PV (550k), so SV is negative—the project is behind schedule. Fast-tracking (running activities in parallel) compresses the schedule to recover. Adjusting budget or timeline doesn't recover the schedule variance the way schedule compression does."},

{"src":"PMP 1","n":132,"lang":"en",
 "q":"A team has had issues with behavior on an ongoing project, which is causing conflicts. As a result, the team is losing momentum and falling behind in its sprints. How should the project manager handle this situation?",
 "opts":["Engage a counselor to reconcile the situation between the team members affected.","Revisit the ground rules and team communication protocols, and discuss what is happening.","Require team members experiencing conflicts to take a day off from work.","Control backlog assignments and reduce direct communications between team members."],
 "c":["B"],
 "e":"Revisiting the team charter's ground rules and communication protocols (which define shared values, conflict resolution, and how the team communicates) and openly discussing the behavior addresses the conflict at its source. Counselors, days off, or reducing communication avoid the team-led reset."},

{"src":"PMP 1","n":133,"lang":"en",
 "q":"A project manager is starting a new project, and the project team is located in different countries. The team was going to use a specific tool to hold virtual meetings. However, that tool is not available in one team member's country. How should the project manager deal with this situation?",
 "opts":["Contact the project sponsor to have the team member removed from the project.","Ask the team member to move to another location where the tool is available.","Start the project and use email to communicate with the team member until the tool is available.","Investigate alternative tools for virtual meetings that are available to all team members."],
 "c":["D"],
 "e":"As a servant leader the PM removes the impediment by finding a virtual-meeting tool everyone can access, keeping the whole team connected. Removing or relocating the member, or sidelining them to email, fails to solve the access problem inclusively."},

{"src":"PMP 1","n":134,"lang":"en",
 "q":"A project team is conducting sprint planning. Based on the estimated effort, only 95% of the items included in the sprint may be delivered. The product owner asks the project manager for advice on how to prioritize the backlog items. What should be used to prioritize the backlog items?",
 "opts":["Technical complexity","Business value","Risk to delivery","Estimation accuracy"],
 "c":["B"],
 "e":"Backlog prioritization is driven by business value so the highest-value items are delivered first, especially when not everything will fit. Complexity, delivery risk, and estimation accuracy are secondary considerations, not the primary ordering criterion."},

{"src":"PMP 1","n":135,"lang":"en",
 "q":"A project manager is performing the scrum master role for a project team of developers. At the daily standup, a couple of developers complain about the noise from a neighboring team, the performance of their laptops, and the difficulty in booking meeting rooms. What should the project manager do?",
 "opts":["Acknowledge the impediments and facilitate their resolutions.","Note the impediments and escalate them to the product owner.","Assign actions to all stakeholders and oversee their implementation.","Note the impediments and work on addressing them later."],
 "c":["A"],
 "e":"Removing impediments is a core scrum master responsibility, so acknowledge them and facilitate their resolution promptly. Escalating to the PO, delaying, or pushing actions onto others doesn't fulfill the impediment-removal role."},

{"src":"PMP 1","n":136,"lang":"en",
 "q":"A state-of-the-art product was delivered at the end of a project life cycle. However, the customer claims the product was not designed to specifications. What should the project manager have done to avoid this issue?",
 "opts":["The customer's requirements should have been captured in order to meet the customer's standards.","The sprint retrospective meeting should have included necessary stakeholders.","The iteration review planning meeting should have been planned accordingly.","The customer's requirements should have been captured and modified to meet the supplier's standards."],
 "c":["A"],
 "e":"Delivered at the end of the life cycle signals a predictive approach, where capturing and baselining the customer's requirements up front is essential to meet their standards. Modifying requirements to the supplier's standards or relying on agile ceremonies misses the fixed-scope requirements step."},

{"src":"PMP 1","n":137,"lang":"en",
 "q":"After the merger of two large companies, a project manager is assigned to a project. The company hires a consultant to ensure best practices are being used in information security. During an iteration, the consultant requests changes to a software component, claiming that it is not meeting market requirements. What should the project manager do?",
 "opts":["Continue the iteration and review the change with the customer.","Cancel the iteration and meet with the product owner.","Cancel the iteration and add the requested changes.","Continue the iteration and add the requested changes."],
 "c":["A"],
 "e":"Protect the current sprint: continue the iteration and review the requested change with the customer (for a future sprint via the backlog). Canceling the iteration is disruptive, and adding changes mid-sprint violates sprint integrity."},

{"src":"PMP 1","n":138,"lang":"en",
 "q":"An agile project has a broad set of product features intended for different user profiles and usages. It is difficult to define common acceptance criteria that can apply to all the features. How can the project manager ensure that the appropriate acceptance criteria are applied to the features?",
 "opts":["Use the broadest set of acceptance criteria to ensure that all features have a common quality baseline and associated functional test cases.","Integrate the specific tests into the definition of ready (DoR) for each feature and the acceptance criteria into the feature's definition of done (DoD).","Integrate the acceptance criteria review into the definition of ready (DoR) for each feature and associated tests into the feature's definition of done (DoD).","Define the acceptance criteria and specific functional test cases only after analyzing the user feedback from testing the early feature release."],
 "c":["C"],
 "e":"Acceptance criteria are reviewed as part of each feature's Definition of Ready (so the team knows what 'good' means before starting), and the associated tests are part of the Definition of Done (to confirm completion). This tailors criteria per feature rather than forcing one broad baseline."},

{"src":"PMP 1","n":139,"lang":"en",
 "q":"A project manager is working on an enterprise resource planning (ERP) initiative that contains stakeholders spanning numerous business lines. The project manager begins to identify, evaluate, and categorize the stakeholder relationships to the program. What is the purpose of this process?",
 "opts":["Incorporate only primary stakeholders into the communications management plan.","Incorporate the needs of all stakeholders into the communications management plan.","Identify all stakeholders who can negatively affect the project.","Identify all stakeholders who can positively affect the project."],
 "c":["B"],
 "e":"Analyzing and categorizing stakeholders feeds the communications management plan with the needs of all stakeholders, ensuring the project delivers value to everyone involved. Focusing only on primary, negative, or positive stakeholders is incomplete."},

{"src":"PMP 1","n":140,"lang":"en",
 "q":"According to a burnup chart for the last 7-day sprint (Scope line rises from 10 to 13–14 story points during the sprint while the Completed line climbs from 1 toward 10), what should the project lead address with the product owner and team?",
 "opts":["The product owner should not ask the team to change their sprint commitments.","The team is accepting too much work in the sprint after it has started.","The team is not meeting the expectations of the product owner.","The team should have stopped on day 4 and started a new sprint."],
 "c":["B"],
 "e":"The scope line rising during the sprint (from 10 to ~14 points) shows work was added after the sprint started—scope creep into a committed sprint. The lead should address that the team is accepting additional work mid-sprint, which undermines the sprint commitment."},

{"src":"PMP 1","n":141,"lang":"en",
 "q":"A business transformation project has a four-phase delivery plan. The project team has successfully delivered one phase of the project. To further enhance team performance and empower team members, which two actions should the project manager take? (Choose two)",
 "opts":["Encourage team members to cross-check decisions with the project manager.","Vet all of the decisions before execution.","Allow team members to make limited decisions.","Organize and assign team members to tasks where they have strengths.","Support team members to make decisions in their areas of strength."],
 "c":["D","E"],
 "e":"Empowerment comes from playing to people's strengths—assigning work where they excel and letting them make decisions in those areas. Cross-checking, vetting all decisions, or only allowing 'limited' decisions keeps control with the PM and undercuts empowerment."},

{"src":"PMP 1","n":142,"lang":"en",
 "q":"A project team is working on an agile software development project. The project manager is concerned that the virtual team may not be as effective as a colocated team at headquarters. Which two actions should the project manager leverage to ensure the team is meeting performance expectations? (Choose two)",
 "opts":["Hold daily virtual meetings to review progress.","Implement time-keeping software for team members.","Task functional managers to drive accountability.","Utilize a web-based kanban board.","Require a monthly individual status report."],
 "c":["A","D"],
 "e":"Daily virtual standups keep a distributed team synchronized, and a shared web-based kanban board gives transparent, real-time visibility of work and flow. Time-keeping software, functional-manager accountability, and monthly reports are command-and-control measures that don't fit agile or build team performance."},

{"src":"PMP 1","n":143,"lang":"en",
 "q":"At the end of a meeting, a functional manager takes the project manager aside to raise some concerns on behalf of a project team member who is unclear about their role and responsibilities for the project. What should the project manager do in this situation?",
 "opts":["Tell the functional manager and the project team member to review the project charter.","Advise the functional manager and the project team member to review the resource management plan.","Ask the functional manager to create a specific responsible, accountable, consult, and inform (RACI) chart for the team member.","Develop a work plan for the project team member with the help of the resource manager."],
 "c":["B"],
 "e":"The resource management plan defines team roles and responsibilities, so directing them to review it clarifies the member's role. The project charter is too high-level, and creating a new RACI or work plan is more than needed when the information already exists in the resource management plan."},

{"src":"PMP 1","n":144,"lang":"en",
 "q":"The project manager just started leading a project team from which the previous project manager resigned. After a quick evaluation of the project, it is apparent that team morale is low due to continuous disagreement about how to complete tasks. Tasks are taking longer than forecasted and the project is off schedule. What should the project manager do?",
 "opts":["Inform the stakeholders that the project will be delayed due to previous team management.","Conduct a meeting with the project team to discuss and address the sources of disagreement.","Issue a change request to extend the project schedule based on identified delays.","Crash the project timeline to ensure the project is back on schedule."],
 "c":["B"],
 "e":"The continuous disagreement is the root cause of the delays, so meet the team to surface and resolve those sources of conflict. Announcing delays, extending the schedule, or crashing treats symptoms without fixing the team dysfunction."},

{"src":"PMP 1","n":145,"lang":"en",
 "q":"The steering committee has asked a project manager experienced in agile to provide an indication of the time remaining on a medium-sized project. What tools can be used to provide this information?",
 "opts":["Analysis of the business requirements document","A release burndown chart","Story points for the remaining user stories","Surrogate measurements"],
 "c":["B"],
 "e":"A release burndown chart shows the remaining work over time, making it the right tool to indicate how much time is left on the release. Raw story points or requirements analysis don't convey remaining time as clearly as the burndown trend."},

{"src":"PMP 1","n":146,"lang":"en",
 "q":"A project manager is managing a hybrid project. After attending an iteration review, a stakeholder tells the project manager that they are very busy and asks why these reviews are held so frequently. How should the project manager respond?",
 "opts":["Discuss with the stakeholder the benefits of the hybrid project and how frequent reviews lead to greater value and less rework.","Negotiate a reduced review attendance with the stakeholder and update the stakeholder engagement plan to reflect this reduced attendance.","Explain to the stakeholder that the stakeholder engagement plan requires their attendance at all reviews.","Record the issue in the issue log and escalate the conflict to the project sponsor for assistance."],
 "c":["A"],
 "e":"Educate the stakeholder on how frequent reviews deliver greater value and reduce rework, winning their continued engagement. Reducing their involvement, citing the plan as a mandate, or escalating doesn't address the value they're questioning."},

{"src":"PMP 1","n":147,"lang":"en",
 "q":"A team has been working for two months and has consistently missed planned user stories. How should the project manager address this issue?",
 "opts":["Promote changing the iteration duration in order to commit as planned.","Ensure the sprint size is appropriate for the iteration duration.","Engage in recruiting a highly skilled resource to reinforce the skills.","Consider using a reward system to encourage timely delivery."],
 "c":["B"],
 "e":"Consistently missing commitments usually means the team is taking on too much per sprint, so right-size the sprint backlog to the iteration length (with the team). Changing iteration length, adding people, or offering rewards doesn't fix over-commitment."},

{"src":"PMP 1","n":148,"lang":"en",
 "q":"A project manager was recently assigned to a banking IT project. The project team has been developing products using a predictive approach for more than 10 years, but management wants the team to change to an agile approach. The project manager met with the team to introduce the agile approach. Most of the team members complained about changing approaches because the performance of this team has always been above average among the IT teams in the bank. Which two actions should the project manager take? (Choose two)",
 "opts":["Interview the most resistant team members to persuade them to try to accept an agile approach.","Conduct training sessions with the team so that the team can understand what the agile approach and mindset are.","Ask the team to identify the product backlog and create a kanban board for the team to manage and follow the approach.","Meet with management to explain the team's resistance to the agile approach and request that they formally ask the team to implement agile.","Brainstorm with the team to understand the specifics of the team's project and to identify possible alternative approaches for the team."],
 "c":["B","E"],
 "e":"Training builds the team's understanding of the agile mindset, and brainstorming together surfaces the project's specifics and the best-fit approach—engaging the high-performing team collaboratively. Persuading resistors, forcing tools, or having management mandate it doesn't earn genuine buy-in."},

{"src":"PMP 1","n":149,"lang":"en",
 "q":"A project team is working remotely on a solution. The client wants the solution as soon as possible and is uncomfortable because the team members are not colocated. In addition, the client expressed concerns about the understanding of the project context and the team's decision-making process. What should the project manager do?",
 "opts":["Encourage the client to interact with the project collaboration platform","Update the communications management plan to satisfy the client's request","Request the client to attend the daily meetings with the project team","Ask the product owner to frequently communicate with the stakeholders"],
 "c":["A"],
 "e":"Inviting the client onto the project collaboration platform gives them transparent, real-time visibility into context and decisions, easing their discomfort. Updating a plan, pulling them into daily standups, or routing through the PO is less direct than giving them visibility themselves."},

{"src":"PMP 1","n":150,"lang":"en",
 "q":"A project manager who is managing a critical project in a multinational company is conducting a progress meeting with all team members. During the meeting, one of the team members states that they are facing a critical problem that will prevent them from completing their assigned task. What should the project manager do?",
 "opts":["Ask the team member to raise a change request to assess the issue in detail.","Schedule a meeting with the concerned team member to review and update the issue log together.","Ask the team member to follow the approved communications management plan to communicate their issue.","Review the risk register with the team member to find the proper response action for this issue."],
 "c":["B"],
 "e":"This is an issue (a problem that has occurred), so meet the team member to analyze it and update the issue log to track and resolve it. A change request or risk register applies to changes/future risks, not a current blocking issue, and deferring to a comms plan ignores the urgency."},

{"src":"PMP 1","n":151,"lang":"en",
 "q":"In a software development project, the product owner and development team agree on a sprint backlog. The highest priority in the backlog is to build a payment module. The team worked on the module during the first half of the sprint, but now two developers want to spend the rest of the sprint focusing on refactoring another feature that might create issues in the future. What should the project manager do?",
 "opts":["Restate to the developers the importance of focusing and meeting the sprint goal.","Praise the developers for their proactivity and focus on quality.","Let the developers focus on the refactoring work and discuss it in the retrospective.","Escalate the issue to the developers' functional managers."],
 "c":["C"],
 "e":"Per the source key, the team is allowed to do the valuable refactoring and reflect on it in the retrospective (refactoring improves code quality and prevents future issues). (Note: many practitioners would protect the sprint goal first—option A—so use the Analyze-with-Claude button to compare the trade-off.)"},

{"src":"PMP 1","n":152,"lang":"en",
 "q":"A client is structured as a matrix organization for an agile project. The project manager is working on-site with key stakeholders from different parts of the organization. How should the project manager handle the different stakeholders?",
 "opts":["The project manager is working on the client's premises, so follow the directions of all the key stakeholders.","This is an agile project, so listen only to the directions of the project manager's supervisor and the functional managers of the organization.","Because there are different perspectives, perform a stakeholder analysis and act based on the outcome.","All stakeholders are important, so the project manager should follow directions provided by management and key stakeholders."],
 "c":["C"],
 "e":"A stakeholder analysis clarifies the different stakeholders' interests, influence, and expectations so the PM can engage them appropriately. Blindly following everyone, only management, or only the supervisor ignores the structured analysis needed in a matrix environment."},

{"src":"PMP 1","n":153,"lang":"en",
 "q":"A project manager has been managing a highly complex project. The project manager has ensured that all of the required tasks have been completed. During a recent external audit, the project manager was informed of a heavy penalty being levied on the project for the latest module that was implemented. What should the project manager have done to ensure that there were no liabilities arising from the recent implementation?",
 "opts":["Ensured that all of the required tasks identified in the scope were completed successfully","Ensured that all defects identified during testing were resolved before implementation","Ensured that all documentation was completed post-implementation before closing the project","Ensured that all the required approvals were received per the organizational procedures"],
 "c":["D"],
 "e":"A penalty from an external audit points to a compliance/approval gap—obtaining all required approvals per organizational procedures would have ensured the implementation was authorized and compliant. Completing tasks, fixing defects, or finishing documentation doesn't cover the regulatory approvals that prevent liabilities."},

{"src":"PMP 1","n":154,"lang":"en",
 "q":"During a project's third iteration, two of the existing team members were replaced. As the project entered the next iteration, the team velocity dropped and the performance started to slip. What should the project manager do?",
 "opts":["Escalate the issue to the project sponsor.","Convene a team-building event to address key performance indicators (KPIs).","Guide the team to revert back to the forming and storming stages.","Discuss the decrease in productivity with human resources (HR)."],
 "c":["C"],
 "e":"Adding new members resets team development, so the PM guides the team back through forming and storming (Tuckman's model) to rebuild cohesion and recover velocity. Escalating, KPI events, or HR don't address the team re-development that the membership change requires."},

{"src":"PMP 1","n":155,"lang":"en",
 "q":"A project manager has been assigned to lead a project to deploy a new system. The stakeholders disagree on the project objectives and approach. What should the project manager do?",
 "opts":["Break down the situation to identify the root causes for the disagreement and then work with each stakeholder and participating entity on their exact roles and responsibilities.","Create a roles and responsibilities matrix based on the project charter and ask the sponsor to share it with all stakeholders to provide credible authority.","Conduct a general meeting with all stakeholders and review and address a list of activities for each of them.","Conduct scoping workshops with the project team to create a work breakdown structure (WBS), assign tasks to various entities, share this with all stakeholders, and ask the team to complete their tasks as scheduled."],
 "c":["A"],
 "e":"First understand the root causes of the disagreement, then work with each stakeholder on their exact roles and responsibilities to resolve it. Imposing a matrix via the sponsor, a generic meeting, or jumping to a WBS acts before the underlying disagreement is understood."},

{"src":"PMP 1","n":156,"lang":"en",
 "q":"A company is implementing a new system. The project manager has identified that it is best to complete this project using a mix of Scrum and Kanban methodologies. The product owner is confused as to how to start delivering the functionalities required. How should the project manager assist the product owner?",
 "opts":["Create a work breakdown structure (WBS) and explain the deliverables to the product owner.","Coach the product owner on how to create a product backlog.","Encourage the team to subdivide the project tasks.","Discuss the product owner's responsibilities within the project team."],
 "c":["B"],
 "e":"Delivery in Scrum/Kanban starts from a prioritized product backlog, so the PM (as coach) helps the product owner build it. A WBS is a predictive tool, and subdividing tasks or discussing responsibilities doesn't give the PO the backlog they need to start."},

{"src":"PMP 1","n":157,"lang":"en",
 "q":"The resource management plan is already done, and the team is located in different countries around the world. Team members are in multiple time zones, and they use different languages for communication. The project manager needs to keep the team organized and avoid misunderstandings and miscommunication that may produce a negative impact to the project. What should the project manager do to reduce negative impacts to the project?",
 "opts":["Conduct cultural awareness seminars.","Hold a virtual meeting to discuss the political environment.","Modify the communications management plan to account for regional differences.","Conduct a multigenerational management training session."],
 "c":["C"],
 "e":"Different time zones and languages are communication challenges, so update the communications management plan to account for those regional differences (timing, language, channels). Cultural seminars or other training don't structure the communications the way the plan does."},

{"src":"PMP 1","n":158,"lang":"en",
 "q":"The client has asked the project manager for a minor change to the product. As the project manager starts to implement the change, they determine that this is not a minor change. This change will have major implications on the budget and schedule. What should the project manager do next in order to reduce the impact of this situation?",
 "opts":["Check the change management plan and submit a change request for a scope change.","Check the change management plan and choose the most appropriate problem-solving method.","Apply expert judgment in order to estimate the budget and duration of the change implementation.","Inform the client that the impact of the change is too high and submit a change request for a scope change."],
 "c":["D"],
 "e":"Because the change has major budget/schedule implications, inform the client of the high impact (managing expectations) and submit a change request to the change control process. Simply submitting a request without informing the client, or estimating informally, manages the situation less transparently."},

{"src":"PMP 1","n":159,"lang":"en",
 "q":"To estimate the costs of a new project that is similar to a project that was implemented last year, the project manager meets with a group of experts from the previous project. The group uses a three-point estimating technique. The project manager submits the estimated budget to the project sponsor for approval. The project sponsor, who is new to the company, is concerned because the budget exceeded their expectations. What should the project manager do?",
 "opts":["Use soft skills to convince the project sponsor to approve the new budget estimate.","Review the historical information and lessons learned from last year's project to justify the new budget estimate.","Change the budgeting technique to a more accurate, bottom-up cost estimation.","Review the organizational process assessment to determine if a contingency reserve was considered in the budget estimate."],
 "c":["A"],
 "e":"Per the source key, the PM uses soft skills (e.g., negotiation/influence) to discuss and justify the well-founded estimate with the sponsor. (Note: justifying via historical data/lessons learned—option B—is also strongly defensible; use the Analyze-with-Claude button to compare.)"},

{"src":"PMP 1","n":160,"lang":"en",
 "q":"A team is ready to start working on a project with a customer who was very difficult to work with in the past because the customer was unable to describe exactly what they wanted. What approach should the project manager take to remove this impediment?",
 "opts":["Develop and monitor a plan with due dates to reduce the time of completing the product.","Work with the product owner to define the minimum viable product(s).","Follow a predictive approach in order to obtain formal acceptance of each deliverable.","Reinforce the negotiation and soft skills of the team through training."],
 "c":["B"],
 "e":"When the customer can't specify exact requirements, defining MVPs with the product owner delivers value incrementally and elicits feedback to clarify what they want. A predictive, fixed-scope approach is the wrong fit for poorly defined requirements."},

{"src":"PMP 1","n":161,"lang":"en",
 "q":"A project manager is assigned to a new project to deliver a product at the end of the year. The project manager was informed by the vendor that a core component could not be shipped on time, which will impact the schedule. What should the project manager do next to minimize the impact on the project?",
 "opts":["Review and update the issue log and determine if any alternative components can be offered.","Schedule a meeting with all stakeholders to extend the project deadline.","Replace the vendor with a new vendor who can offer the component on time.","Add an item to the next status review to make stakeholders aware of the vendor issue."],
 "c":["A"],
 "e":"Log the issue and first explore alternative components to keep the schedule, acting on the problem at hand. Extending the deadline, replacing the vendor, or just informing stakeholders are premature before checking for a workable alternative."},

{"src":"PMP 1","n":162,"lang":"en",
 "q":"The product owner has completed release planning for the project. The project manager is unable to obtain team members with the appropriate skills to meet the proposed timelines. What should the project manager do to ensure the deadline is met?",
 "opts":["Remove features from the release that require the skill set that is lacking on the team.","Provide skills training to the team and make up the lost time by asking team members to work on multiple stories in parallel.","Add team members with the required skill set on an ad-hoc basis to work on stories that existing team members cannot complete.","Send some of the team members to training for the proper skill set while the others work to meet the deadline."],
 "c":["C"],
 "e":"Bring in members with the required skills on an as-needed basis to handle the stories the current team can't, directly closing the skill gap to meet the deadline. Cutting features, parallel-loading work, or pulling people away for training risks scope or schedule."},

{"src":"PMP 1","n":163,"lang":"en",
 "q":"Project stakeholders are engaged in a discussion on how to achieve the best user experience in the design of a new application. The agile team is divided on the approach. The conflict is more of a collective disagreement than an outright conflict. What should the project manager do next?",
 "opts":["Apply appropriate conflict resolution techniques to solve the issue.","Engage in individual conversations with each team member.","Call a team meeting to have a formal discussion of the issue.","Allow the team to work through it on their own unless help is needed."],
 "c":["A"],
 "e":"Apply the appropriate conflict-resolution technique (ideally collaborate/problem-solve) to bring the divided group to consensus. Individual chats, a generic formal meeting, or staying hands-off are less targeted than selecting and applying the right resolution technique."},

{"src":"PMP 1","n":164,"lang":"en",
 "q":"A typically high-performing team member has started to demonstrate a performance drop as well as some aggressive behaviors toward other team members in meetings. The project manager has worked with this individual for many months on this project and has not seen this type of behavior before. What should the project manager do?",
 "opts":["Discuss the project manager's observations with the individual to determine why they are behaving this way.","Discuss the individual's behavior with the other team members to determine if they have noticed and know what is going on.","Attend more team meetings to observe the team more closely to determine the root cause.","Discuss their performance with the team member's functional manager as well as human resources (HR) and discuss the next steps."],
 "c":["A"],
 "e":"A sudden change in a normally strong performer signals an underlying issue, so talk directly with the individual to understand the root cause. Gossiping with others, just observing, or escalating to the functional manager/HR avoids the direct, respectful conversation that uncovers the cause."},

{"src":"PMP 1","n":165,"lang":"en",
 "q":"A senior project manager has included a junior project manager as part of the project team. During a coaching session, the junior project manager asks the senior project manager how to be efficient and reduce rework. How should the senior project manager respond?",
 "opts":["Schedule daily meetings with key stakeholders to assess the effectiveness of the project management plan.","Assign project tasks to the team regardless of their skills to accomplish the project schedule.","Monitor resource allocation to ensure that team members work 8 hours per day.","Use a work breakdown structure (WBS) to create a project schedule based on the project and resource requirements."],
 "c":["A"],
 "e":"Frequent (daily) collaboration meetings improve planning, surface impediments early, and build in quality—reducing rework through transparency. A WBS builds the schedule but doesn't drive efficiency, and the other options don't address rework."},

{"src":"PMP 1","n":166,"lang":"en",
 "q":"A project manager for a software development company faces a number of financial risks in their project. The project manager needs to frequently check the strength and efficiency of the risk management process. What should the project manager use to accomplish this?",
 "opts":["Stakeholder register","Brainstorming session","Audit meeting","Assumption log"],
 "c":["C"],
 "e":"A risk audit evaluates the effectiveness of the risk management process and the handling of risks, which is exactly the periodic check needed. The stakeholder register, brainstorming, and assumption log don't assess the risk process itself."},

{"src":"PMP 1","n":167,"lang":"en",
 "q":"A project team is implementing a hosted system for a third party. Just before going live, the customer asks the project team to include additional functionality. The project team identified risks to the delivery date and informed the project manager that some of the requested functions are in conflict with the agreed scope. What should the project manager do?",
 "opts":["Discuss with the team, estimate the effort, and raise a change request.","Discuss with the customer the risks identified and team's concerns.","Ask the team to deliver the functionality on the agreed go-live date.","Ask the customer to go live and add the new functionality in the backlog."],
 "c":["A"],
 "e":"The added functionality is a scope change, so discuss it with the team, estimate the effort/impact, and raise a change request through change control. Just discussing risks, refusing, or deferring to a backlog skips the formal change request the change requires."},

{"src":"PMP 1","n":168,"lang":"en",
 "q":"A company must implement a new regulation. The government has specified the date when the regulation will be enforced but has only provided high-level information on the regulation's requirements. Therefore, changes in definitions are to be expected due to uncertainty. Which life cycle should the project manager use for this project?",
 "opts":["A predictive execution strategy","A hybrid execution strategy","An iterative execution strategy","An Agile Execution Strategy"],
 "c":["D"],
 "e":"High uncertainty with frequently changing requirements is best handled by an agile life cycle, which embraces change through short, adaptive iterations. A predictive approach would struggle with the expected definition changes."},

{"src":"PMP 1","n":169,"lang":"en",
 "q":"Following the initial project assessment, the project manager finds that, due to organizational restructuring, the client stakeholders have changed. This has resulted in receiving conflicting information from the new stakeholders. What should the project manager update first?",
 "opts":["Risk register","Project management plan","Project charter","Project scope statement"],
 "c":["A"],
 "e":"New stakeholders bringing conflicting information introduces new risks, so the risk register is updated first to capture and plan responses for them. The charter, plan, and scope statement are updated later as the situation is analyzed."},

{"src":"PMP 1","n":170,"lang":"en",
 "q":"A project for a new product launch is in a very initial stage and the requirements are evolving. Due to stiff competition in the market, the customer would like to launch the product keeping the scope flexible. The project team intends to start the work based on the forecast of a similar project delivered last year. Which project approach suits the scenario?",
 "opts":["Execute it as a project with stage gates.","Execute it as a Scrum project.","Execute it using the same approach as the reference project.","Execute it as a predictive project."],
 "c":["B"],
 "e":"Evolving requirements and a deliberately flexible scope point to Scrum, which delivers iteratively and adapts to change. Stage gates and predictive approaches assume a more fixed scope, and blindly copying the reference project's approach ignores this project's flexibility need."},

{"src":"PMP 1","n":171,"lang":"en",
 "q":"An agile project is running activities to define the minimum viable product (MVP). During the session, the project manager identifies some mandatory regulations, but there is no consensus to include these regulations in the MVP because it may extend the duration of the project. What should the project manager do?",
 "opts":["Get commitment from the team to include all of the required regulations.","Share with the participants the need to focus only on product functionality.","Train the team on the new regulations as requested by management.","Ask the project sponsor to add more time to the project."],
 "c":["A"],
 "e":"Mandatory regulations are non-negotiable compliance requirements, so the PM must get the team's commitment to include them in the MVP regardless of schedule pressure. Focusing only on functionality, training, or just extending time doesn't ensure the required regulations are built in."},

{"src":"PMP 1","n":172,"lang":"en",
 "q":"The programming activities of a project were planned to last 35 days per module, but the programming of the first module has taken 45 days. What should the project manager do?",
 "opts":["Ask the team to work overtime to complete the deliverable on time.","Submit a change request to the project sponsor to change the schedule.","Check the scope to verify if there is scope creep and get the project on schedule.","Evaluate the situation and identify ways to compress the schedule without impacting baselines."],
 "c":["D"],
 "e":"Analyze the overrun and look for schedule-compression options (fast-tracking/crashing) that recover time without changing the baselines. Overtime, a schedule-change request, or assuming scope creep act before evaluating the best recovery approach."},

{"src":"PMP 1","n":173,"lang":"en",
 "q":"During an iteration of a project, a planned activity becomes more complex. The work should be delivered in the shortest time possible. What approach should be used in this situation?",
 "opts":["The product owner moves it to the product backlog.","The team members bring in a specialist to help them.","The cross-functional team members work together to complete the activity.","The team facilitator gets support from external team members."],
 "c":["B"],
 "e":"Bringing in a specialist (expert judgment) is the fastest way to handle the newly complex activity and deliver it in the shortest time. Moving it to the backlog delays it, and general team collaboration may be slower than focused expertise."},

{"src":"PMP 1","n":174,"lang":"en",
 "q":"A company wants to increase the commercial value of one of its products in a highly competitive market. In order to do this, the company commissions a project to create a prototype. The team constructs the prototype incrementally. Which action should the project manager take as a priority?",
 "opts":["Ensure the project sponsor is aware of the go-to-market date.","Deliver business value as soon as possible.","Emphasize the use of Kanban methodology.","Implement a plan-do-check-act (PDCA) cycle."],
 "c":["B"],
 "e":"Incremental development prioritizes delivering business value as early as possible, which is critical in a competitive market. Awareness of dates, a specific method like Kanban, or PDCA are supporting practices, not the priority outcome."},

{"src":"PMP 1","n":175,"lang":"en",
 "q":"A project manager for a strategic initiative realizes a new stakeholder is interested in the project and wants to ensure the stakeholder is fully aware of the goals, objectives, benefits, and risks of the project. What should the project manager do to enhance the success of the project?",
 "opts":["Schedule a meeting with the new stakeholder.","Add the new stakeholder to the communications management plan.","Share the project status reports with the new stakeholder.","Update the stakeholder engagement plan."],
 "c":["D"],
 "e":"Update the stakeholder engagement plan to add the new stakeholder to the register and analyze their power/interest and engagement needs, which then drives appropriate communication. A single meeting, comms-plan entry, or sending reports is narrower than the engagement plan update that governs all of it."},

{"src":"PMP 1","n":176,"lang":"en",
 "q":"A large corporation is transforming itself from a predictive to an agile approach. A project team with knowledge of agile practices is experiencing significant conflicts with the executives regarding the processes to be followed. How should these conflicts be resolved?",
 "opts":["Negotiate with the executives and agree on a process.","Train the executives on agile practices.","Include the executives in team retrospectives.","Request that the executives use agile practices."],
 "c":["B"],
 "e":"The conflict stems from the executives' unfamiliarity with agile, so training them builds the shared understanding that resolves the process disagreements. Negotiating a hybrid process, including them in retrospectives, or simply requesting compliance doesn't fix the underlying knowledge gap."},

{"src":"PMP 1","n":177,"lang":"en",
 "q":"An organization is embarking on the deployment of a new digital solution that will revolutionize purchasing services. The project manager has created all the necessary plans and activities to ensure a successful integration and solution launch. There is a risk of a partner's system being unavailable which could impact the project negatively. What key activity is needed to ensure the project's success?",
 "opts":["Continuously monitor the risk of the partner system's unavailability, and put the proper resolution plans in place in case the risk materializes.","Mitigate the risk by developing alternative plans to make the solution ready and available without full integration capabilities.","Communicate to the project stakeholders that full integration may not be possible and that the project will potentially be delayed as a result.","Eliminate the risk by mandating the partner to change the system settings to facilitate an easier integration with the new solution."],
 "c":["A"],
 "e":"Continuously monitor the identified risk and have response (contingency) plans ready if it materializes—appropriate management of a known external risk. Prematurely descoping integration, announcing delays, or mandating partner changes overreact before the risk actually occurs."},

{"src":"PMP 1","n":178,"lang":"en",
 "q":"A project's business case was just approved and the project manager is creating the benefits management plan. It is unclear how the benefits from the project align with the organization's vision. What should the project manager do first to verify alignment?",
 "opts":["Meet with the project sponsor to obtain more information","Consult with the project team members to gain their opinions","Request a copy of the project assumptions log for more insight","Ask the project team to delay the kick-off while resolving the concern"],
 "c":["A"],
 "e":"The sponsor has the deepest understanding of the organization's vision and strategy, so meeting them clarifies how the project's benefits align. The team, assumptions log, or delaying kick-off won't provide the strategic alignment insight the sponsor can."},

{"src":"PMP 1","n":179,"lang":"en",
 "q":"During the execution phase of a project using a hybrid approach, the project manager feels that the project does not have the right level of stakeholder engagement. What should the project manager do in this situation?",
 "opts":["Contact the stakeholders directly and invite them to project meetings","Create a power/interest grid to assess the level of each stakeholder","Escalate this situation to the project sponsor","Capture this within the risk register and perform a qualitative analysis"],
 "c":["B"],
 "e":"A power/interest grid assesses each stakeholder's influence and interest so the PM can determine and apply the right engagement approach. Inviting everyone to meetings, escalating, or logging a risk doesn't analyze who needs what level of engagement."},

{"src":"PMP 1","n":180,"lang":"en",
 "q":"A project manager is leading an in-house company project in its very early stages. This current project is similar to another project that ended a year ago. What should the project manager do to analyze the involved stakeholders?",
 "opts":["Refer to the stakeholder register from the previous project as it was similar to the current project.","Lower the priority of stakeholder engagement as the stakeholders already have knowledge of this kind of project.","Use lessons learned from the previous project as a guide for the current project's stakeholder register.","Document in the risk register that the current project may have different stakeholders than the previous project."],
 "c":["C"],
 "e":"Lessons learned from the similar prior project guide building this project's own stakeholder register (preferences, actions, involvement) while accounting for differences. Simply reusing the old register assumes identical stakeholders, and lowering engagement priority is risky."},

{"src":"PMP 2","n":1,"lang":"en",
 "q":"For an iterative development project, the project manager selected team members from locations across multiple time zones. This presents an opportunity to achieve over 18-hour development days but introduces a greater need for communication methods to coordinate activities. What three actions should the project manager do next? (Choose three)",
 "opts":["Evaluate the risk of lower-quality deliverables.","Confirm approval for the potential of additional cost for rework.","Direct the team to be very clear in their communications with each other.","Support everyone during their own working hours.","Establish peer reviews for every deliverable."],
 "c":["C","D","E"],
 "e":"To make a multi-time-zone team work, verify clear communication channels, support members in their own working hours, and use peer reviews to maintain quality across handoffs. Pre-assuming lower quality or rework cost isn't a proactive next action."},

{"src":"PMP 2","n":2,"lang":"en",
 "q":"During the execution of a project, the finance team identified that they were not involved in building the business case for the project and will not approve the estimated return on investment (ROI). What should the project manager do?",
 "opts":["Continue developing based on the project management plan and address the issue post-delivery.","Revisit the scope management plan and note this as a risk.","Schedule a meeting with the product owner and finance team to agree on course corrections.","Revisit the communications management plan and make necessary adjustments."],
 "c":["C"],
 "e":"Bring the product owner and finance team together to resolve the disagreement and agree on course corrections—direct collaboration resolves the conflict. Continuing regardless, just logging a risk, or tweaking the comms plan doesn't address finance's objection."},

{"src":"PMP 2","n":3,"lang":"en",
 "q":"A new project manager is assigned to a project midway through its implementation. During the first risk review meeting, the project manager notices that the version of the risk register used by the project team members is different from the one the project manager is using. What should the project manager have done to avoid this issue?",
 "opts":["Performed expert judgment","Referred to the communications management plan","Validated the issue log","Validated the project artifacts"],
 "c":["D"],
 "e":"Validating the project artifacts (ensuring everyone uses the current, controlled versions) would have prevented the team working from an outdated risk register. Expert judgment, the comms plan, or the issue log don't address version control of artifacts."},

{"src":"PMP 2","n":4,"lang":"en",
 "q":"In the beginning of project execution, the project manager finds out that a key resource will be unavailable for two weeks, which could cause a delay to the schedule. What should the project manager do first?",
 "opts":["Update the resource management plan and resource allocation chart accordingly.","Assign the tasks to another resource, and ask the resource to work double shifts.","Ask human resources (HR) for a replacement, and update the risk register.","Update the schedule to reflect the project's changes in duration."],
 "c":["A"],
 "e":"This is a resource problem, so update the resource management plan and allocation chart to arrange alternative resourcing for the unavailable key resource. Overloading another person, escalating to HR, or just extending the schedule are weaker first responses."},

{"src":"PMP 2","n":5,"lang":"en",
 "q":"A project manager has been assigned to an education project with the objective of training a workforce in application development. One of the deliverables is to provide internships to the best students in the customer's active projects. However, the customer does not have enough projects in progress right now. The project manager decides to create several internal projects in order to ensure the internships. Which three actions should the project manager take to fulfill this scenario without adding costs for the customer? (Choose three)",
 "opts":["Manage the quality of the delivery.","Use the project's contingency budget.","Create a change request with the scope of the new internal projects.","Create a work breakdown structure (WBS) of the new scope with the internal projects.","Influence the client to initiate a new project"],
 "c":["A","B","D"],
 "e":"Fund the new internal projects from the existing contingency budget (no added customer cost), define the new scope with a WBS, and manage the delivery quality. A change request or pressuring the client would add cost or shift the burden to the customer."},

{"src":"PMP 2","n":6,"lang":"en",
 "q":"A project manager is leading a software development project that will assist in maintenance management for a power plant. The project manager is analyzing project performance data and trying to forecast if the project will have any slippage in the future. What should the project manager do?",
 "opts":["Perform quality control analysis.","Perform schedule analysis.","Perform trend analysis.","Perform root cause analysis (RCA)."],
 "c":["C"],
 "e":"Trend analysis uses historical performance data to forecast future outcomes (like potential slippage), which is exactly the forward-looking question. Quality control, schedule, or root-cause analysis examine current/past conditions rather than projecting the future trend."},

{"src":"PMP 2","n":7,"lang":"en",
 "q":"A project manager is part of an organization that is assessing the use of agile delivery approaches. A new project management office (PMO) manager who had a bad experience with a Scrum approach recommended that the project board replace Scrum with a predictive approach. What should the project manager do?",
 "opts":["Define a risk management plan for the new approach, as needed.","Review the benefits realization plan to include the cost of the change.","Define a project management plan for the new approach, as needed.","Review the communications management plan to identify new stakeholders."],
 "c":["B"],
 "e":"Switching methodologies has costs and benefit implications, so review the benefits realization plan to include the cost of the change before committing. Jumping to redefine plans assumes the change is justified without weighing its cost against the benefits."},

{"src":"PMP 2","n":8,"lang":"en",
 "q":"A project manager is working on an agile project that is within the scope of a recently announced corporate audit. The project manager was appointed to attend the first meeting with the auditors scheduled for the following week. What should the project manager do to prepare for the audit?",
 "opts":["Reserve story points for the next iteration if needed.","Review the project information.","Ask the development team to provide compliance information.","Ask for a waiver during the audit."],
 "c":["B"],
 "e":"Review the project information so you fully understand the project's status and can represent it accurately to the auditors. Reserving story points, offloading to the team, or seeking a waiver doesn't prepare the PM to engage the audit knowledgeably."},

{"src":"PMP 2","n":9,"lang":"en",
 "q":"A project manager is leading a hybrid project funded by a time and materials (T&M) agreement. The project was completed, and the client accepted the delivery of the product. One week later, the client asked the project manager to fix two defects they found in the product. The project manager responded with an estimate of time and money and asked the client to confirm this. The client disagreed and insisted that the work should be done for free because it is a defect in the product. What should the project manager have done to prevent this?",
 "opts":["Trained client engineers to support the product after its delivery.","Reviewed the project quality control plan with the client.","Suggested a fixed-cost basis and included free bug fixes for 1 month.","Planned how the delivered product will be supported after its delivery."],
 "c":["D"],
 "e":"Planning post-delivery support (warranty/maintenance terms) up front sets clear expectations about who handles defects after acceptance, preventing the dispute. Training, reviewing QC, or a different contract type don't define the after-delivery support that caused the conflict."},

{"src":"PMP 2","n":10,"lang":"en",
 "q":"A project manager joins a team that is providing a customized enterprise resource planning (ERP) system to a client. A potential design problem is identified by the development team. After reviewing this issue with the project owner and team members, all agreed to revise the design to avoid a system defect when deployed. The design is already approved. What should be the project manager's next steps?",
 "opts":["Make a change request and get formal approval from the project owner.","Inform the client of the problem and follow the change request process.","Amend the design specifications and pass them to the development team for reprogramming.","Conduct a proof of concept to make sure there are no problems in the revised design."],
 "c":["D"],
 "e":"Validate the revised design with a proof of concept to confirm it actually resolves the defect before changing the approved baseline. Submitting a change request or amending specs before proving the new design risks formalizing an unverified solution."},

{"src":"PMP 2","n":11,"lang":"en",
 "q":"The project velocity has recently slipped, and a decision was made to skip testing to increase the momentum and bring the project back on track. What will the project manager need to focus on as a result of this decision?",
 "opts":["Increased cost","Decreased efficiency","Increased technical debt","Increased burn rate"],
 "c":["C"],
 "e":"Skipping testing and quality practices accumulates technical debt—the backlog of cleanup, maintenance, and fixes deferred while building. The PM must track and manage that growing debt, which is the direct consequence of cutting testing."},

{"src":"PMP 2","n":12,"lang":"en",
 "q":"A project scope statement has been approved, and the project manager and core team have been assigned to the project. Suddenly, the project manager takes a temporary leave of absence due to a medical emergency. While the project manager is on leave, the team develops the project schedule. When the project manager returns, a detailed schedule is almost complete. What should the project manager do?",
 "opts":["Congratulate the team and move on to the execution phase.","Ensure that the schedule is aligned with the vision and objectives.","Develop a new detailed schedule with the team and project sponsor.","Escalate the compliance issue to the project management office (PMO)."],
 "c":["B"],
 "e":"Verify the team-built schedule aligns with the project's vision and objectives before relying on it. Simply accepting it, rebuilding it from scratch, or escalating overlooks the validation step that confirms the schedule supports the goals."},

{"src":"PMP 2","n":13,"lang":"en",
 "q":"A project manager notices a conflict in the team on both technical and interpersonal levels during the execution phase of a project. What should the project manager do?",
 "opts":["Hire an external consultant to lead an organizational intervention","Address the conflicts during the lessons learned session","Hold a team meeting to understand and address the conflict.","Schedule a monthly meeting to handle all project conflicts."],
 "c":["C"],
 "e":"A team meeting to understand and address the conflict directly is the most effective, timely way to resolve it. Outsourcing to a consultant, deferring to lessons learned, or batching conflicts monthly delays resolution of an active problem."},

{"src":"PMP 2","n":14,"lang":"en",
 "q":"The team lead on a project has left the organization and management wants to promote engineer A to this position. The project manager is confident that selecting engineer A will add problems to the project and believes that engineer B would better fit this role. The project manager discussed all of the arguments with management but their decision remains unchanged. What should the project manager do?",
 "opts":["Help engineer A to become familiar with this new role and ensure engineer B stays motivated.","Coach engineer B to be prepared to perform this role if and when engineer A fails","Request management approval that engineer B will be promoted if engineer A fails in this role.","Promote engineer B instead since the project manager has the authority to make this decision"],
 "c":["A"],
 "e":"Once management has decided, the PM respects the decision and supports engineer A's success in the role while keeping engineer B motivated and engaged. Planning for A to fail or overriding management undermines the decision and the team."},

{"src":"PMP 2","n":15,"lang":"en",
 "q":"A project manager is managing a project that requires specialized architecture services. This project was identified as a high risk for the organization. The project manager works with an external architect who is highly specialized. However, the architect will soon resign because they accepted an offer from another firm. What should the project manager do next?",
 "opts":["Escalate the issue to the procurement department and ask them to discuss this with the vendor firm.","Discuss this with the project team, assess the impact, and decide on the appropriate actions to follow.","Call the account manager and ask them to retain the expert by offering them an incentive.","Ask the architect for the contact details for their new employer and call their account manager."],
 "c":["B"],
 "e":"Discuss with the team, assess the impact of losing the key specialist, and decide on appropriate actions—understanding before acting. Escalating to procurement or trying to retain the individual jumps to a solution before the impact is understood."},

{"src":"PMP 2","n":16,"lang":"en",
 "q":"During a kick-off meeting, the project manager notices that one of the stakeholders responsible for providing key information to the team has a low level of participation. This stakeholder believes that there will be no benefit from implementing the project. The project manager realizes that this could be a high risk for the project. Which action should the project manager take?",
 "opts":["Facilitate a supportive level of engagement for this stakeholder.","Ask the project sponsor for advice as it might be possible to work with another stakeholder.","Ensure the key information is accurate and monitor the situation as the project progresses.","Perform a qualitative risk analysis using a probability and impact matrix."],
 "c":["A"],
 "e":"Move this key but resistant stakeholder toward a supportive level of engagement, since their participation is critical and their disengagement is a real risk. Replacing them, only monitoring, or just analyzing the risk doesn't actively raise their engagement."},

{"src":"PMP 2","n":17,"lang":"en",
 "q":"A team has been working for some time on a project with incremental deliveries. The project manager noticed that one of the team members is less engaged in team meetings, yet delivers promptly. While discussing the source of demotivation, the team member informed the project manager that this is due to a feeling of lack of skills in dealing with new tasks. How should the project manager address the issue?",
 "opts":["Replace the team member with a resource from another team who already has the requisite training and skills needed.","Review which tasks the team member would feel more motivated to work on then assign those tasks to the team member","Create a self-paced learning plan with the team member during working hours, with checkpoints on learning progress.","Discuss growth and development needs with the team member and provide learning opportunities accordingly"],
 "c":["D"],
 "e":"Address the root cause—the skills gap—by discussing the member's growth and development needs and providing appropriate learning opportunities. Replacing them or only reassigning comfortable tasks avoids developing the member; a self-paced plan is good only when set with them, not imposed."},

{"src":"PMP 2","n":18,"lang":"en",
 "q":"A project to build a new energy plant was just completed. However, a few days before operation of the plant, the local community protested and closed access to the plant. They argue that an important natural reserve was impacted and prior consultation was not done by the project team. What should the project manager verify first?",
 "opts":["Risk management plan","Cost-benefit analysis","Stakeholder engagement plan","Business case"],
 "c":["A"],
 "e":"Community protest over an unconsidered environmental impact is a realized risk, so check the risk management plan first to see whether this risk was identified and what response was planned. The other documents don't capture risk identification and response."},

{"src":"PMP 2","n":19,"lang":"en",
 "q":"During a project meeting, the project manager expressed the importance of delivering reports on time. These individual reports will be consolidated into a single report. One of the team member's reports was not delivered on time and therefore the report could not be integrated with the other reports. This demonstrates a lack of teamwork and impacts project success. What should the project manager do to resolve this issue?",
 "opts":["Include this item in the risk register.","Discuss this matter during the project meeting.","Assign the task to someone else on the team.","Discuss it with the team member in confidence."],
 "c":["D"],
 "e":"A private, confidential conversation lets the PM understand why the member missed the deadline and offer support without publicly singling them out (which would harm morale). Logging a risk, airing it in the meeting, or reassigning the task doesn't address the individual constructively."},

{"src":"PMP 2","n":20,"lang":"en",
 "q":"During project implementation, one team finds out that a key project deliverable was not budgeted. What should the project manager do first?",
 "opts":["Escalate the issue to the project sponsor for resolution","Meet with the team to discuss alternatives.","Hold the team accountable and ask for an immediate solution","Remove the deliverable from the project scope"],
 "c":["B"],
 "e":"Meet with the team to analyze the situation and discuss alternatives before deciding on a course of action. Escalating immediately, blaming the team, or dropping the deliverable acts before the options are understood."},

{"src":"PMP 2","n":21,"lang":"en",
 "q":"A project manager assigned to an education project needs to train a workforce in application development. The customer is responsible for providing the training center, and the deadline to start the first training is in 2 weeks. The customer calls to say that the center will not be available in 2 weeks due to some unforeseen problems. What should the project manager do next?",
 "opts":["Update the project schedule with the new date for training","Apply the terms and conditions of the contract with penalties.","Help the customer find an alternative location to start the training.","Issue a change request to reflect the anticipated date for training"],
 "c":["C"],
 "e":"Collaborate with the customer to find an alternative location so training can start on time, keeping the project on track. Applying penalties damages the relationship, and updating the schedule or issuing a change request gives up on the deadline before exploring alternatives."},

{"src":"PMP 2","n":22,"lang":"en",
 "q":"A project development team is located in different areas around the world. Last week, a very important release was delayed because one of the developers did not respond on time to an email that was sent to everyone. The developer seems to have missed reading the email. What should the project manager do to avoid these situations?",
 "opts":["Review the resource management plan.","Review the stakeholder engagement plan.","Review the communications management plan","Review the risk management plan."],
 "c":["C"],
 "e":"The problem is a communication-method failure (a critical message missed in mass email), so review and improve the communications management plan (channels, acknowledgments, escalation). The other plans don't govern how the team communicates."},

{"src":"PMP 2","n":23,"lang":"en",
 "q":"An oil and gas project started without having acquired full funding for the project. The remaining funds were to be acquired during project execution. The acquisition of the remaining funds was delayed several months, resulting in a suspension of work by all contractors. What should the project manager have done to prevent this from happening?",
 "opts":["Ensured the project team monitored and reviewed the project risk register periodically.","Ensured the risk was adequately assessed and mitigated by the appropriate stakeholders.","Ensured the stakeholder who was providing additional funds remained interested in the project","Ensured the stakeholder anticipated obstacles to achieving financial closure on the remaining funds"],
 "c":["B"],
 "e":"The funding gap was a known risk that should have been properly assessed and mitigated by the appropriate stakeholders during planning. Periodic monitoring or keeping a funder interested doesn't substitute for assessing and mitigating the funding risk with a response plan."},

{"src":"PMP 2","n":24,"lang":"en",
 "q":"A project is starting its second iteration out of six. During the daily meeting, a team member asks for help. In order to finish a deliverable, an approval from the design department will be needed. What should the project manager do?",
 "opts":["Update the issue log and escalate it to the project sponsor","Meet with the design manager to ask for the necessary approval","Invite a design team member to the next daily meeting.","Replace this iteration deliverable for one without any blockers."],
 "c":["B"],
 "e":"As a servant leader removing impediments, the PM meets the design manager directly to obtain the needed approval. Escalating to the sponsor is premature, and the daily standup is for the team—you don't pull in an external department just for an approval."},

{"src":"PMP 2","n":25,"lang":"en",
 "q":"In an agile team, some of the team members are coming forward to ask for more autonomy on the project. What should the project manager do in this situation?",
 "opts":["Discourage them as conflicts may be created","Encourage the team members to make decisions","Discourage this request, as it is for the management team.","Encourage the team lead to make decisions"],
 "c":["B"],
 "e":"Agile teams thrive on self-organization, so encourage the members to make decisions—this empowerment is exactly what's wanted. Discouraging autonomy or funneling decisions to a lead or management contradicts the self-managing principle."},

{"src":"PMP 2","n":26,"lang":"en",
 "q":"The project team is experiencing schedule delays due to issues arising with suppliers. Some of the tasks are on the critical path. What should the project manager do in this situation?",
 "opts":["Review the critical path with the stakeholders to define the next steps","Escalate the supplier-related issues to the sponsor for the resolution.","Discuss the critical path issues with the purchasing department.","Review the work breakdown structure (WBS) with the suppliers"],
 "c":["A"],
 "e":"Because the delays hit the critical path (directly affecting the end date), review the critical path with stakeholders to understand the impact and decide next steps, then engage procurement once a direction is set. Escalating or going straight to purchasing skips understanding the schedule impact first."},

{"src":"PMP 2","n":27,"lang":"en",
 "q":"A team shared a complaint regarding the scrum master during the last sprint review. The team expressed that they were delayed because the scrum master did not remove impediments, such as scheduling some meetings with external teams and obtaining a room to work collaboratively, among other critical blockers. What should the project manager do?",
 "opts":["Push back, explaining that a self-organized team should address all of the problems to get the work done.","Determine and agree when a situation becomes an impediment to be solved by the scrum master.","Request the team review and prioritize the open impediments to resolve all of them as soon as possible.","Request an additional resource to assist in solving all of the impediments listed by the team."],
 "c":["B"],
 "e":"What counts as an impediment for the scrum master to remove varies by team, so define and agree on which situations are scrum-master impediments. Pushing back, just prioritizing the list, or adding resources doesn't establish the shared understanding of the scrum master's role."},

{"src":"PMP 2","n":28,"lang":"en",
 "q":"A government agency is developing a new connectivity policy. The assigned project manager is a famous economist with international recognition. The team is eager to work with the project manager but feels intimidated. The project manager realizes that a junior economist, whose tasks are behind schedule, is especially intimidated by the situation. What should the project manager do first?",
 "opts":["Request to replace the junior economist with a more experienced economist","Evaluate the performance of the junior economist in the upcoming months.","Consider organizing paired work sessions for the junior and senior economists","Reassign some of the junior economist's tasks to other economists."],
 "c":["C"],
 "e":"Pairing the intimidated junior with senior economists builds confidence and transfers skills through collaboration, addressing the root cause. Replacing, waiting to evaluate, or reassigning tasks abandons the junior's development."},

{"src":"PMP 2","n":29,"lang":"en",
 "q":"A project manager received a request to add features and functions to the project after the scope had already been established. What should the project manager do?",
 "opts":["Follow the risk management process","Follow the change management process","Follow the requirements management process.","Follow the stakeholder engagement process"],
 "c":["B"],
 "e":"Any change to an established scope must go through the change management (integrated change control) process for evaluation and approval. The risk, requirements, and stakeholder processes don't authorize scope changes."},

{"src":"PMP 2","n":30,"lang":"en",
 "q":"In the middle of a software development project, the lead design programmer accepted an offer from a competitor. The project manager needs to find a replacement to meet an upcoming milestone. What should the project manager do?",
 "opts":["Assign the tasks to another team member","Consult the risk response plan","Issue a request to the change control board (CCB).","Update the resource calendar"],
 "c":["B"],
 "e":"Losing a key expert is a foreseeable resource risk, so consult the risk response plan for the pre-planned contingency. Reassigning ad hoc, going to the CCB, or just updating the calendar ignores the planned response that should already exist."},

{"src":"PMP 2","n":31,"lang":"en",
 "q":"A project manager discovers that the project team is spending a large amount of time delivering several reporting formats for the same project updates to multiple stakeholders. What should the project manager do to reduce the time spent on these activities?",
 "opts":["Delegate all of the stakeholder communications activities to a project team member","Segment the types of communications to be delivered to different stakeholders.","Hold biweekly status meetings to deliver information to all stakeholders at once","Update the communications management plan to remove less influential stakeholders."],
 "c":["B"],
 "e":"Segment communications by each stakeholder's interest and information needs (not everyone needs the same detail/frequency), reducing redundant reporting while keeping everyone appropriately informed. Delegating, batching everyone into one meeting, or dropping stakeholders are weaker fixes."},

{"src":"PMP 2","n":32,"lang":"en",
 "q":"A project is halfway through its execution phase. The quality specifications for the project deliverables are formally accepted. However, a key stakeholder is dissatisfied, stating that the deliverables do not meet the quality specifications. What should the project manager do in this situation?",
 "opts":["Inform the project sponsor of the stakeholder's dissatisfaction","Review the requirements traceability matrix and conduct inspections","Notify the stakeholder that arbitration procedures will be followed.","Document the issue in the issue log and send a report to management"],
 "c":["B"],
 "e":"Review the requirements traceability matrix and inspect the deliverables to objectively determine whether they meet the agreed specifications before deciding on action. Escalating, threatening arbitration, or just logging the issue acts before verifying the facts."},

{"src":"PMP 2","n":33,"lang":"en",
 "q":"A new engineer was assigned to a project. Although the engineer has adequate skills for the job, they always wait for authorization from the project manager before acting. What should the project manager do?",
 "opts":["Replace the resource with an experienced engineer.","Obtain related training for the new team member.","Determine the appropriate level of decision-making authority and empower the engineer.","Make all of the decisions for the engineer in order to keep the workflow on track"],
 "c":["C"],
 "e":"Clarify the engineer's decision-making authority and empower them to act, since they already have the skills—they just need permission. Replacing or retraining a capable engineer, or making all decisions for them, reinforces the dependence instead of removing it."},

{"src":"PMP 2","n":34,"lang":"en",
 "q":"A project manager is newly appointed to lead a project team that will be working remotely. The project manager decided to use a set of tools that worked well for previous projects. However, a few team members are not happy with this plan. What should the project manager have done to avoid this?",
 "opts":["Asked the project sponsor for approval to purchase the newest collaboration tool on the market.","Delegated the responsibility of selecting the collaboration tools to the project's technical lead.","Allowed the project team members to use the tools that will work best for them for this project.","Met with the project team to determine their collaboration needs and identified tools that will work best"],
 "c":["D"],
 "e":"Involving the team to determine their collaboration needs and jointly selecting suitable tools secures buy-in and fits the project. Imposing previous tools, buying the newest, delegating to one lead, or letting everyone pick their own all skip the collaborative needs assessment."},

{"src":"PMP 2","n":35,"lang":"en",
 "q":"A client wants to create an innovative software for the travel industry using an agile approach. A project manager from the company was assigned to work on the details and estimates as requested by the client. What should the project manager do before sending the information to the client?",
 "opts":["Put the tasks with estimates in the backlog and clarify that the project will not need any reserves.","Sequence the tasks with estimates and add standard risk reserves to use for software development","Analyze the tasks, provide estimates, include a prototype demo, and send to the client directly.","Work with the team to understand their views and add the appropriate dependencies and risks."],
 "c":["D"],
 "e":"Collaborate with the team to refine the estimates and identify dependencies and risks, producing realistic numbers before sharing with the client. Estimating alone or applying generic reserves without the team's input yields less accurate, less credible estimates."},

{"src":"PMP 2","n":36,"lang":"en",
 "q":"A project manager is leading a project that is in the execution phase. Due to a company reorganization, some key project team members are likely to leave the project. What should the project manager do next?",
 "opts":["Engage with the project sponsor to ensure that this change does not affect the project execution.","Determine the impact to the project and implement mitigations per the project management plan.","Discuss this issue with the affected team members and support them during the transition.","Start looking for replacements as soon as possible to help prevent any schedule slippage."],
 "c":["B"],
 "e":"Members 'likely to leave' is a risk, so determine the impact and apply mitigations per the plan. Escalating to the sponsor, just supporting members, or immediately recruiting acts before the impact is assessed."},

{"src":"PMP 2","n":37,"lang":"en",
 "q":"During daily team standup meetings, team members constantly ask about which tasks they should focus on first. How should the project manager ensure the team is fully productive?",
 "opts":["Review the project backlog during the team's standup meeting.","Encourage project team members to prioritize the product backlog.","Ensure team members are working according to the project plan","Work with the product owner to ensure the project backlog is prioritized"],
 "c":["D"],
 "e":"The product owner owns backlog priority, so work with them to keep it properly prioritized—then the team always knows what to pull next. Reviewing the backlog in standup or letting the team self-prioritize doesn't establish the PO-owned priority that guides the work."},

{"src":"PMP 2","n":38,"lang":"en",
 "q":"Knowledge sharing has been a key priority for a complex technological project with a large number of team members. During the project execution phase, the project manager receives numerous complaints from all team members that they are overloaded with knowledge-sharing emails, which contain useful information but are unmanageable due to the high volume. What should the project manager do to solve this problem?",
 "opts":["Establish a pull communications method to access knowledge repositories and lessons learned.","Establish an interactive communications method between stakeholders to share knowledge and lessons learned","Establish a push communications method for the team to share knowledge and lessons learned.","Establish an unofficial communication method for the team to share knowledge and lessons learned."],
 "c":["A"],
 "e":"Pull communication lets team members access knowledge repositories and lessons learned at their convenience, eliminating inbox overload from pushed emails. Push or interactive methods would add to the volume rather than relieve it."},

{"src":"PMP 2","n":39,"lang":"en",
 "q":"An agile team has completed five sprints. The final product is expected to be delivered in another three sprints. The project manager receives a call from the technical resource manager that one of the developers must take leave for the next 4 weeks, with no temporary replacement available. This is going to significantly impact the deliverable. What should the project manager do?",
 "opts":["Inform the customer of the impact immediately and prepare for escalation","Request additional funding from the sponsor to obtain a new resource to fill in the gap.","Perform an impact analysis, readjust the schedule as needed, and communicate the changes to the customer.","Ask the product owner to stretch the delivery team further to complete the final product in the original timeline."],
 "c":["C"],
 "e":"Analyze the impact of the developer's absence, adjust the schedule accordingly, and transparently communicate the changes to the customer. Alarming the customer immediately, seeking funding, or overloading the team all act before understanding the actual impact."},

{"src":"PMP 2","n":40,"lang":"en",
 "q":"A project manager has been assigned to lead an IT project to store information on sold lottery tickets. During the project execution, a failure in the system caused 10 numbers to be sold two times. This happened during the month of greatest demand because the prize was the highest of the year. The project team is concerned that one of those tickets could be the winner. What should the project manager do first?",
 "opts":["Carry out tests to verify that the system does not fail again due to the high occurrence of events.","Inform the sponsor about the issue and make a request for sufficient funds to be made available.","Review the list of risks to verify that this issue was considered and execute the detailed action plan.","Reassure the sponsor by explaining the probability of this risk materializing is extremely low."],
 "c":["C"],
 "e":"Check the risk register to see whether this risk was identified and execute its planned response. Testing, requesting funds, or downplaying probability act before consulting the planned risk response that should address exactly this scenario."},

{"src":"PMP 2","n":41,"lang":"en",
 "q":"During the final stage of the launch for a new design, team members are having a conflict about some features of the design. What should the project manager do?",
 "opts":["Ask the project sponsor to make the decision about the design features.","Ask stakeholders to reduce the scope in order to meet project deadlines.","Review the design with the team members and propose a new launch date.","Call a stakeholder meeting along with team members and seek consensus."],
 "c":["D"],
 "e":"Bring the team and stakeholders together in a meeting to discuss the design conflict and reach consensus (collaborate). Escalating to the sponsor, cutting scope, or pushing the launch date doesn't resolve the disagreement collaboratively."},

{"src":"PMP 2","n":42,"lang":"en",
 "q":"An agile project started 6 months ago. Since the beginning of the project, there have been disagreements among team members regarding daily meetings since some of the team members are not available at the same time. The project manager is convinced an agile team should be self-organizing, and that meeting times should be worked out by the team. What should the project manager do?",
 "opts":["Establish a team charter.","Assign a team leader.","Recognize the team's contributions.","Establish a project schedule."],
 "c":["A"],
 "e":"A team charter captures the team's own ground rules and working agreements (including meeting norms), aligning the self-organizing team on how to handle availability. Assigning a leader, recognition, or a schedule doesn't set the shared agreements that resolve the recurring disagreement."},

{"src":"PMP 2","n":43,"lang":"en",
 "q":"In a geographically distributed team, a developer continues to delay a coding activity. This activity is on the critical path. The project manager found out that the delay is due to the developer's lack of understanding of the customer requirements shared by the off-site team. What should the project manager do?",
 "opts":["Replace the developer with a skilled resource","Facilitate communication to ensure clarification of the requirements.","Perform a root cause analysis (RCA) with the team members","Conduct frequent performance reviews."],
 "c":["B"],
 "e":"The root cause is already known—a requirements misunderstanding—so facilitate communication to clarify the requirements for the developer. Replacing them, redoing RCA (cause already found), or performance reviews don't address the clarity gap."},

{"src":"PMP 2","n":44,"lang":"en",
 "q":"A project manager is working on a multinational project that has more than 100 stakeholders. The project manager is concerned about the appropriate stakeholder participation and involvement level required for successful project delivery. What should the project manager do?",
 "opts":["Evaluate the stakeholder engagement assessment matrix","Perform a stakeholder assumption and constraint analysis.","Review the issue log with the project stakeholders","Review the risk log with the project stakeholders"],
 "c":["A"],
 "e":"The stakeholder engagement assessment matrix compares each stakeholder's current vs. desired engagement level, helping target the right participation for so many stakeholders. Assumption analysis, the issue log, or the risk log don't measure engagement levels."},

{"src":"PMP 2","n":45,"lang":"en",
 "q":"Several components of an expensive manufacturing project have been returned by the customer. The customer is complaining that the quality of the components is poor and is concerned that, as a result, their products will be returned due to quality issues. The project manager is certain that quality has been maintained. How should the project manager communicate with the customer to help assure them that the project has produced quality products?",
 "opts":["Show the customer a sample history of quality.","Show the customer the tolerance and control limits","Show the customer the quality control measurements","Show the customer sample results from the current batch."],
 "c":["C"],
 "e":"Quality control measurements document the actual results of inspecting/testing the deliverables against the standards, providing objective evidence that quality was met. A sample history, control limits, or one current batch is less complete proof than the documented QC measurements."},

{"src":"PMP 2","n":46,"lang":"en",
 "q":"During the sprint planning of a project, the team members have a disagreement about the comparative size of the backlog items and if they would fit into the next sprint. Considering a hybrid approach, what should the project manager do?",
 "opts":["Make the decision about the backlog items based on what makes more sense","Facilitate the discussion so that the team members can reach a consensus","Ask an external technical leader to provide input and plan the sprint.","Remove the items from the product backlog since the size couldn't be estimated."],
 "c":["B"],
 "e":"The team owns its estimates, so the PM facilitates the discussion to help them reach consensus on sizing (e.g., via planning poker). Deciding for them, bringing in an outsider, or dropping the items overrides the team's collaborative estimation."},

{"src":"PMP 2","n":47,"lang":"en",
 "q":"A team has individual daily start times for each team member. Sometimes the overlap between team members' working hours is short. During those times, the interactions and work as a team are reduced and ineffective. What should the project manager do?",
 "opts":["Allocate the overlap time for each team member and ask them to adhere to the schedule.","Ask the team to define a team charter and agree on core working hours","Ask the team to start at the same time to obtain the maximum number of team working hours.","Inform the team members who are joining late to join early and work together."],
 "c":["B"],
 "e":"Have the team create a team charter and agree on core working hours themselves, building ownership and overlap for collaboration. Imposing a schedule or forcing identical start times overrides the self-organizing team's input."},

{"src":"PMP 2","n":48,"lang":"en",
 "q":"During an agile project, a project manager lost one of the resources, and at the same time, uncovered new requirements, which were added to the backlog. What should the project manager do next?",
 "opts":["Reevaluate the project schedule as the velocity will be impacted by the loss of resources.","Reevaluate the velocity as the iteration duration will be impacted by new requirements.","Reevaluate the scope of the current iteration to keep the same velocity and timeline.","Reevaluate the backlog priority with the product owner since the velocity has been impacted."],
 "c":["D"],
 "e":"With reduced velocity and new requirements, re-prioritize the backlog with the product owner so the most valuable work is done within the new capacity. Each new requirement is assessed for business value and re-prioritized—that's the agile response, not just reworking schedules."},

{"src":"PMP 2","n":49,"lang":"en",
 "q":"A project manager is leading a large public project that will have a high impact on the town's citizens. How should the project manager define the different requirements?",
 "opts":["Analyze the interests and influence of stakeholders and evaluate their requirements.","Include only the common requirements of each stakeholder group in the project goals.","Add only the opinions of the team, because they know the objectives of the project.","Include only the key requirements and keep the stakeholders informed about scope decisions."],
 "c":["A"],
 "e":"In a high-impact public project, analyze all stakeholders' interests and influence (including affected citizens) and evaluate their requirements to meet community needs. Limiting input to common requirements, the team, or only key ones risks missing important stakeholder needs."},

{"src":"PMP 2","n":50,"lang":"en",
 "q":"When a project is in its final stage, the project manager will request from the team to prepare a delivery acceptance document. What is the importance of this document to the project manager?",
 "opts":["Needed to release the team at the end of the project.","Needed to close the work breakdown structure (WBS).","Needed to share it with the stakeholders.","Needed to close out the project or a phase"],
 "c":["D"],
 "e":"Formal delivery acceptance is the basis for closing out the project or phase—without documented customer acceptance, the project cannot be formally closed. Releasing the team and archiving follow from closure, but the document's core purpose is enabling that close-out."},

{"src":"PMP 2","n":51,"lang":"en",
 "q":"A company is conducting a digital transformation project that has a hard deadline. The marketing team wants to combine this implementation with a new product, and the newly assigned project manager is not aware of all the dependencies. Using a hybrid approach, what should the project manager do to ensure the dependencies are taken into consideration?",
 "opts":["Combine all of the small projects into one big project so the team can collaborate better as one big team.","Facilitate communication promoting a Scrum of Scrums so the different teams are aware of the dependencies and progress.","Make sure that each individual project schedule is aligned with the common deadlines.","Implement a project newsletter so the different team members can be informed about the progress of each project."],
 "c":["B"],
 "e":"A Scrum of Scrums coordinates multiple teams working on interdependent work, surfacing cross-team dependencies and integration points. Merging into one project, aligning schedules, or a newsletter don't provide the ongoing coordination of dependencies that a Scrum of Scrums does."},

{"src":"PMP 2","n":52,"lang":"en",
 "q":"Close to the end of a project, a customer claims that some deliverables have not been met and starts initiating change requests. What should the project manager do next?",
 "opts":["Revise the project scope baseline.","Review the approved project charter.","Escalate the change request to the project sponsor.","Refer to the requirements traceability matrix."],
 "c":["D"],
 "e":"The requirements traceability matrix links requirements to deliverables, so use it to verify whether the customer's claim of unmet deliverables is valid before acting. Revising the baseline, checking the charter, or escalating jumps ahead of confirming the facts."},

{"src":"PMP 2","n":53,"lang":"en",
 "q":"The change control board (CCB) has approved a change request to add a new component within a product. The operations manager discovers the change after its approval and complains that the implementation of the change will cause significant disruption to the production line. What should the project manager do first?",
 "opts":["Meet with the operations manager to explain the background for the change and understand the operation manager's concerns about the change.","Formally acknowledge the operation manager's concerns and raise a new risk that the project may deliver late and over budget due to manufacturing difficulties.","Lead a root cause analysis workshop with the design team to identify the failures that led to the decision to add a component that was not compatible with the production line.","Escalate the issue to the project sponsor, as the decision to add the component brings many benefits even though it will be disruptive to the production line."],
 "c":["A"],
 "e":"Meet the operations manager to explain the approved change and understand their concerns—direct, two-way communication addresses the disruption worry. Logging a risk, running an RCA, or escalating acts before understanding the operations manager's actual concerns."},

{"src":"PMP 2","n":54,"lang":"en",
 "q":"A project is behind schedule because of a delayed equipment installation. This is affecting many other activities. The manager responsible for the equipment delivery notified the project manager that there is a capacity issue for the team doing the installation. What should the project manager have done to prevent this situation from happening?",
 "opts":["Conducted periodic reviews with the manager on resource availability.","Consulted with the installation team on a regular basis to check for conflicts","Asked the manager to follow the agreed-upon resource management plan.","Defined the skill requirements more clearly in the scope statement"],
 "c":["A"],
 "e":"Periodic reviews of resource availability with the responsible manager would have surfaced the capacity issue early enough to mitigate it (alternative teams or schedule adjustment). Checking with the install team or relying on the plan/scope wording wouldn't catch the manager's capacity constraint proactively."},

{"src":"PMP 2","n":55,"lang":"en",
 "q":"During a project, the project manager finds an opportunity to reduce the project cost by completing a deliverable ahead of schedule with a minimal impact on project resources. However, the client has only been focused on the next milestone. How should the project manager approach this situation?",
 "opts":["Focus on the deliverable of the next project milestone as per the client's strict instructions.","Review the change management plan to discuss early benefits realization with the client","Evaluate whether the client's request will add extra features to the project deliverable","Apply additional resources to realize the early deliverable and ensure the next milestone is achieved."],
 "c":["B"],
 "e":"Present the cost-saving opportunity to the client through the change management process so they can make an informed decision about early benefits realization. Ignoring the opportunity or unilaterally applying resources bypasses the client's decision and proper change control."},

{"src":"PMP 2","n":56,"lang":"en",
 "q":"A startup company has just appointed a CEO that has no prior project experience. The CEO wants to execute projects with their own resources and has appointed a project manager. What should the project manager recommend that the CEO do first?",
 "opts":["Create a framework to support project success.","Utilize an agile approach for all upcoming projects.","Utilize a predictive approach for all upcoming projects.","Create a framework to justify project execution."],
 "c":["A"],
 "e":"For an inexperienced CEO and a new project capability, first establish a framework (governance, best practices, roles, processes, metrics) to support project success. Committing to one delivery approach for everything, or a framework just to 'justify' projects, is premature or misdirected."},

{"src":"PMP 2","n":57,"lang":"en",
 "q":"A project is ready to start, and resources are allocated. The development manager then informs the project manager that a critical resource is no longer available due to a family emergency. The development manager indicates that all other developers have been assigned to other projects, and there is no capacity for any developer to take on additional work. What should the project manager do next to fulfill this resource need?",
 "opts":["Determine resource allocation options based on project priority.","Meet with the sponsor to determine how to rebaseline the schedule","Inform the sponsor of the situation and request an extension of the target completion date.","Apply changes to the existing resource allocations and inform the development manager."],
 "c":["A"],
 "e":"Assess the situation and identify resource options based on project priorities (e.g., reallocating from lower-priority work). Rebaselining, requesting an extension, or unilaterally changing allocations act before evaluating priority-based options."},

{"src":"PMP 2","n":58,"lang":"en",
 "q":"A project is in the start-up phase of the project life cycle. The project team consists of 20 people from five different countries. The project team is scheduled to have quarterly face-to-face meetings. However, due to unforeseen circumstances, no face-to-face meetings can occur. The project manager is concerned that the team may not be able to fulfill their individual roles without face-to-face meetings. What should the project manager do in this situation?",
 "opts":["Review the risk register for this unforeseen circumstance.","Ask for an extension of time on the project deliverables.","Use the contingency reserve and subcontract the work.","Utilize virtual teams for meetings and deliverables"],
 "c":["D"],
 "e":"Establish virtual team collaboration (tools and meetings) so the distributed team can still communicate and deliver despite no face-to-face meetings. Reviewing risks, extending time, or subcontracting don't solve the immediate need to keep the team working together."},

{"src":"PMP 2","n":59,"lang":"en",
 "q":"Some of the team members who are working on a new software tool are in a public space, arguing over a new feature being coded instead of completing the coding. How should the project manager handle this situation and get the team to complete the task?",
 "opts":["Contact the human resources (HR) department to tell them there is an argument in a public space and that they need to intervene immediately.","Bring the team members to the meeting room to discuss the new feature and to reach a common understanding of the necessary coding.","Engage in the conversation in the public space by telling them the expectations of the feature and advise them to return to their workspace","Listen to the argument from a nearby location to understand the situation, then offer insight into the features when the team is calm"],
 "c":["B"],
 "e":"Move the team to a private meeting room and facilitate a collaborative discussion to reach a shared understanding and resolve the disagreement. Escalating to HR, intervening publicly, or eavesdropping doesn't constructively resolve the technical conflict."},

{"src":"PMP 2","n":60,"lang":"en",
 "q":"An agile project's sponsor has indicated that a particular stakeholder group has not been engaged in the project. What should the project manager do?",
 "opts":["Call a meeting with the project team to determine why the stakeholder has not been engaged and who is accountable","Engage the product owner, indicating the sponsor's concerns and request the appropriate next steps.","Review the communications management plan to determine how the stakeholders on the project were planning to be involved.","Meet with the stakeholder group to capture their interests and project requirements, and add these to the product backlog."],
 "c":["C"],
 "e":"First review the communications management plan to see how that stakeholder group was supposed to be engaged, then identify the gap. Blaming the team, deflecting to the product owner, or jumping to capture requirements acts before understanding the planned engagement."},

{"src":"PMP 2","n":61,"lang":"en",
 "q":"A team member has experienced personal conflicts on past projects, making them nervous about working on the current project. The project manager knows this team member could add value to the project. What should the project manager do to get the most value from this team member?",
 "opts":["Consult with human resources (HR) and check company policies on what is allowed to advise the team member.","Advise the team member to focus on the current project and consider the past conflicts as lessons learned.","Advise the team member not to share personal information from past projects with the entire team.","Consult with the project sponsor to find out if it is allowed to share personal information with the entire team."],
 "c":["B"],
 "e":"Help the team member treat the current project as a fresh opportunity and view past conflicts as lessons learned, setting clear expectations and offering support. Involving HR or the sponsor is unnecessary for a personal concern the PM can address within the team."},

{"src":"PMP 2","n":62,"lang":"en",
 "q":"A development team is struggling to find design documentation for the upcoming sprint. Prior to leaving for vacation, the product owner communicated that the documentation had been uploaded to the project file repository. What should the project manager do to address this issue?",
 "opts":["Audit the effectiveness of the management of project artifacts.","Investigate new software tools used to manage project artifacts","Improve email communication protocols to manage project artifacts","Assign a team member to manage project artifacts"],
 "c":["A"],
 "e":"Audit how project artifacts are managed to find why the team can't locate documentation that was supposedly uploaded, then fix the process. New tools, email protocols, or assigning an owner are premature before understanding the artifact-management gap."},

{"src":"PMP 2","n":63,"lang":"en",
 "q":"In a daily standup meeting, a project manager notices that a team member's reports include incomplete tasks with no impediments. A colleague informs the project manager that this team member is experiencing a personal issue. What should the project manager do?",
 "opts":["Ask the human resources (HR) manager to speak with this team member.","Coach the team member on how to keep work and personal issues separate","Understand the team member's personal issue and offer them support","Reallocate this team member's tasks to others for the immediate future"],
 "c":["C"],
 "e":"Approach the team member privately with empathy to understand the personal issue and offer support, prioritizing their wellbeing while keeping the project on track. Sending HR, lecturing about separating work/life, or immediately reassigning tasks isn't the supportive first step."},

{"src":"PMP 2","n":64,"lang":"en",
 "q":"A company is interested in reducing production costs in one of its production lines. When the project charter was about to be approved, one of the key stakeholders questioned the project's value and caused a delay in the approval. What should the project manager have done to avoid this setback?",
 "opts":["Analyzed stakeholders","Detailed the project charter","Developed benchmarking","Assessed feasibility and impacts"],
 "c":["A"],
 "e":"Analyzing stakeholders early would have identified the key stakeholder and their concerns about the project's value, so they could be addressed before charter approval. A more detailed charter, benchmarking, or feasibility analysis wouldn't surface the stakeholder's objection in advance."},

{"src":"PMP 2","n":65,"lang":"en",
 "q":"A project manager at a large corporation has been assigned to oversee a customer experience project. Within the project, customer satisfaction is a big issue and there are many unresolved customer queries on a daily basis. The project manager has been informed that the customer service team is overwhelmed with these unresolved queries. Which two actions should the project manager take to correct this problem? (Choose two)",
 "opts":["Ask senior management to replace the customer service manager with a more qualified one.","Confirm the issues truly exist by conducting a survey and other analyses that may detect the real issue.","Request that the customer service team prioritizes work on queries that are received this week.","Deploy solutions that will track, prioritize, and resolve queries as soon as possible.","Contact the customers in batches and inform them that the issues are being worked on currently."],
 "c":["B","D"],
 "e":"First confirm the real issue through a survey/analysis (understand before acting), then deploy a solution to track, prioritize, and resolve queries. Replacing the manager, only prioritizing this week, or just messaging customers don't fix the underlying query backlog."},

{"src":"PMP 2","n":66,"lang":"en",
 "q":"A project manager starts to receive emails from different stakeholders requesting information about the project status. What should the project manager do to avoid this in the future?",
 "opts":["Include the stakeholders' emails in all of the project status communications.","Schedule weekly project status meetings with all stakeholders including the customer and internal resources","Include the project stakeholders' needs while planning the project communications strategy","Designate a team member to respond to the emails from these stakeholders"],
 "c":["C"],
 "e":"Proactively capturing each stakeholder's communication needs and preferences in the communications strategy/plan ensures they're informed in a timely way and don't need to chase status. CC'ing everyone, blanket meetings, or delegating replies are reactive workarounds."},

{"src":"PMP 2","n":67,"lang":"en",
 "q":"In the daily check-in meeting, a team member informs the project team that the licenses for one of the components that will be used will expire in a couple of months. The licensing cost is significant. What should the project manager do next?",
 "opts":["Add a spike to the backlog for an alternative design","Raise a change request to secure the renewal.","Escalate the licensing issue to the product owner","Evaluate the impact of nonrenewal of the license"],
 "c":["D"],
 "e":"First evaluate the impact of not renewing the license (risks, costs, alternatives) to inform the decision before acting. Adding a spike, raising a change request, or escalating all act before the impact is understood."},

{"src":"PMP 2","n":68,"lang":"en",
 "q":"During the execution phase of a project, a new department has been created in the company. The manager of this department has requested to be involved in the regular project communications and reporting cycles. What should the project manager do?",
 "opts":["Inform the manager that only stakeholders identified in the communications management plan should be involved.","Perform a stakeholder analysis and update the communications management plan to reflect the new situation.","Suggest the manager contact the steering committee and project management office (PMO) to be included","Ask the project team members to include the manager in all of their communications regarding this project."],
 "c":["B"],
 "e":"The new department is a change in the stakeholder landscape, so perform a stakeholder analysis and update the communications management plan to engage the new manager appropriately. Refusing them, deflecting to the PMO, or ad-hoc CC'ing bypasses proper stakeholder analysis."},

{"src":"PMP 2","n":69,"lang":"en",
 "q":"A project manager has been assigned to deliver a challenging project on time and within budget. The project manager needs to empower the team members by removing organizational impediments and facilitating collaboration. What should the project manager do to lead the project?",
 "opts":["Act as the project owner","Assume the functional manager role","Perform the project sponsor activities","Use a servant-leader approach"],
 "c":["D"],
 "e":"Servant leadership focuses on serving the team—removing impediments and facilitating collaboration so members can perform at their best, which is exactly what's described. Acting as owner, functional manager, or sponsor are different roles that don't capture this empowering style."},

{"src":"PMP 2","n":70,"lang":"en",
 "q":"The scope of a project is to replace the street signs within a 10-km radius. The client does not want to sign the project closure report if the radius is not extended by 1 km, to be aligned with the council's development plan. What should the project manager do?",
 "opts":["Initiate a new project and discuss the request because it came in too late.","Delay the approval and ask the project sponsor for additional funding","Obtain formal acceptance for the completed work and submit a change request","Contact the council and ask for their approval to extend the scope of the project"],
 "c":["C"],
 "e":"Obtain formal acceptance for the work already completed (per the agreed 10-km scope), then handle the additional 1 km as a change request through change control. Starting a new project, delaying for funding, or extending scope without a change request bypasses proper process."},

{"src":"PMP 2","n":71,"lang":"en",
 "q":"The deliverables of a project have been completed. However, the stakeholders did not approve the deliverables, because they did not cover the needs of the business. Which actions should the project manager have taken to avoid this situation?",
 "opts":["Defined the project management plan and ensured it met the project objectives and business needs","Provided and explained the sprint backlog at the beginning of the project to the stakeholders and project team","Ensured that the stakeholders participated in the daily meetings to confirm that the project was developing correctly","Requested that the team change the frequency of the deliverables in the development phase to obtain earlier feedback"],
 "c":["A"],
 "e":"Defining a project management plan aligned to the project objectives and business needs would have ensured the deliverables met what the business required. Sharing a sprint backlog, having stakeholders join daily standups (they normally don't), or changing delivery frequency wouldn't guarantee alignment to business needs."},

{"src":"PMP 2","n":72,"lang":"en",
 "q":"A project has met the agreed-upon product requirements and is closing. There is time remaining in the schedule and excess budget. The resources are not yet scheduled to move to other projects. Several product champions want to continue adding functionality to the product. What should the project manager do?",
 "opts":["Acknowledge the project closure criteria has been met and release the remaining budget and resources","Use the remaining time and resources to add additional scope to the project without spending the project budget","Ensure product quality by spending the remaining resources and time on additional quality checks for the product.","Obtain approval from the sponsor to add additional functionality to the project deliverables with the leftover budget"],
 "c":["A"],
 "e":"The agreed requirements (closure criteria) are met, so close the project and release the remaining budget and resources—adding functionality now would be gold-plating/scope creep. Using leftover time/budget for extra scope or quality checks isn't justified once closure criteria are satisfied."},

{"src":"PMP 2","n":73,"lang":"en",
 "q":"A subject matter expert (SME) external to the project team provides a suggestion that will save the project delivery time. This SME has available time to support the project but was not planned to be involved in the current agile iteration. What should the project manager do?",
 "opts":["Ask the SME to complete all of the necessary changes, which will save time","Direct the team member to ignore the SME's changes as they were not planned","Ask the existing team members to collaborate with the SME and complete the activity.","Bring the SME on board immediately and discuss the implications later"],
 "c":["C"],
 "e":"Have the existing team collaborate with the SME so the team understands and can maintain the changes while leveraging the SME's expertise. Letting the SME work alone, ignoring the suggestion, or onboarding without discussing implications either loses team ownership or skips analysis."},

{"src":"PMP 2","n":74,"lang":"en",
 "q":"A project team member, who is inexperienced in system development work, is not performing well. Currently, there are no other staff members available who are qualified to complete the work. What should the project manager do?",
 "opts":["Work with the functional manager to determine an incentive for the team member to complete the project","Request a substitute for the team member with a new resource who is more skilled in system development work","Assess the team member's development requirements and arrange for the team member to receive training","Seek approval from the project sponsor to use some of the project budget reserves to hire a new resource"],
 "c":["C"],
 "e":"Develop the existing team member by assessing their training needs and providing training—developing people is a core PM responsibility, especially when no substitutes exist. Incentives, substitutes (none available), or hiring don't build the needed capability in the person already on the team."},

{"src":"PMP 2","n":75,"lang":"en",
 "q":"On a project with multiple international locations, a trend for delays has been flagged. When looking into the issue, the project manager realized that some of the key activities were scheduled over the holiday periods in the overseas offices. What should the project manager do next?",
 "opts":["Request that the offices in overseas locations work overtime to mitigate schedule slippages","Update the schedule to reflect the holidays and notify the project sponsor of the delayed completion","Hire additional personnel on a temporary basis to cover the extra workload during the holiday seasons","Update the schedule to reflect the holidays and share the workload among offices to mitigate schedule slippage."],
 "c":["D"],
 "e":"Update the schedule for the real holiday calendars and redistribute work across offices to recover the slippage proactively. Forcing overtime, just notifying the sponsor of a delay, or hiring temporary staff are less feasible than rebalancing the existing cross-office capacity."},

{"src":"PMP 2","n":76,"lang":"en",
 "q":"A project manager is leading a global project with resources spread out in different locations. The stakeholders have different interpretations of the requirements. The project manager is concerned about scope creep. Which action should the project manager take to control the scope?",
 "opts":["Ensure that all key stakeholders agree to the project deliverables and there will be no need for changes","Accept only those changes that are approved by the change control board (CCB) prior to being implemented","Accept only those changes to requirements that provide additional value to the project","Reject all changes that will increase the budget or increase the timeline of the project"],
 "c":["B"],
 "e":"Controlling scope means routing every change through the change control board for review and approval before implementation, preventing uncontrolled scope creep. Assuming no changes, accepting 'valuable' changes informally, or blanket-rejecting changes all bypass proper change control."},

{"src":"PMP 2","n":77,"lang":"en",
 "q":"An organization is struggling to start an important project. The project manager has identified that the scope definition is the main item preventing the project from starting. Although most of the scope items are defined and agreed upon among the stakeholders, there are a few items that are hard to grasp and very complex to define at this stage. What should the project manager do?",
 "opts":["Propose that the well-defined scope items be delivered using a predictive approach and use an agile approach to deal with the complex items","Maintain stakeholder momentum in working on the scope definition until a full detailed scope is achieved prior to starting the project","Update the risk register and escalate the issue to the project management office (PMO), requesting more resources be added to help define the project scope","Recommend splitting the project into two smaller projects in order to work exclusively in an agile environment without interference from a predictive approach."],
 "c":["A"],
 "e":"A hybrid approach fits: deliver the clear scope predictively while handling the complex, uncertain items with agile/iterative methods. Waiting for full scope, escalating for resources, or splitting into two projects are less efficient than tailoring the approach to each part."},

{"src":"PMP 2","n":78,"lang":"en",
 "q":"A project manager is leading a strategic and competitive advantage project for a healthcare organization that is in its testing phase. The team has been working on this project for 6 months, and the morale of the team is low due to the schedule. What can the project manager do to motivate the team?",
 "opts":["Talk to the business team about the project phase and the team's commitment, and request a monetary reward for the team","Continue to perform the last phase of the project with the project team as this will benefit the organization and provide a competitive edge.","Speak with the project management office (PMO) and request a 2-week extension of the schedule so the team can have rotational time off","Meet with the horizontal team managers to recognize the team members and give rewards and promotions upon completion of the project"],
 "c":["D"],
 "e":"Recognition and rewards/promotions (coordinated with the relevant managers) tied to completion boost morale and motivation. A blanket monetary request, just pushing on, or a schedule extension don't directly recognize and incentivize the individuals."},

{"src":"PMP 2","n":79,"lang":"en",
 "q":"A project that is using an incremental approach has collocated team members and is using a Kanban board to visualize the work in progress (WIP). A key external stakeholder requests that all project activities be placed on hold until a status report is distributed. What should the project manager have done to avoid this situation?",
 "opts":["Invited all of the stakeholders to daily standup meetings","Sent weekly status update reports to all of the stakeholders","Stored project documentation in a shared folder","Organized monthly meetings with the external stakeholders"],
 "c":["B"],
 "e":"Regular weekly status reports would have kept the external stakeholder informed of progress, preventing them from feeling out of the loop and halting work. Inviting outsiders to standups, a shared folder, or monthly meetings wouldn't proactively deliver the status they needed."},

{"src":"PMP 2","n":80,"lang":"en",
 "q":"A project manager is working on a complex project with a high degree of change. What strategy should the project manager use to ensure a successful project completion?",
 "opts":["Increase the active engagement and participation of key stakeholders","Schedule a regular risk review with the project management team","Update the project management plan to reduce the possibility of scope creep","Create a project change roadmap to keep track of all unforeseen changes"],
 "c":["A"],
 "e":"In a complex, high-change project, actively engaging key stakeholders helps gain support, surface concerns early, and keep the work aligned to their evolving needs. Risk reviews, plan updates, or a change roadmap are useful but secondary to strong stakeholder engagement."},

{"src":"PMP 2","n":81,"lang":"en",
 "q":"A project manager leads a development project for a mobile chatting application. One of the experienced developers constantly creates conflicts with the other team members. As a result, the other team members are refusing to work with the developer. Since the developer is a high performer, the project goals may be negatively impacted if they leave the team. What should the project manager do in this situation?",
 "opts":["Meet with the team members and developer individually to understand the situation and build an action plan accordingly","Meet with the developer and ask them to be respectful with the other team members and work together as a team","Arrange a team-building training session and require all of the team members to attend","Collect the team's feedback and send it to human resources (HR) to resolve the conflict"],
 "c":["A"],
 "e":"Meet the developer and team members individually to understand the root of the conflict, then build a targeted action plan. Simply telling the developer to be respectful, a generic team-building event, or escalating to HR won't get to the root cause."},

{"src":"PMP 2","n":82,"lang":"en",
 "q":"An agile project manager notices that one of the team members is developing extensive knowledge in a specific matter. Other team members are likely to require this knowledge in the future. What should the agile project manager do?",
 "opts":["Ask the team member to produce a lessons-learned document and share it with the team","Ask the team member to provide a training session for the rest of the team","Ask the team member to develop more expertise and become a subject matter expert (SME)","Ask the team member to record highlights the team may want to know"],
 "c":["B"],
 "e":"Extensive, specialized knowledge is best transferred through a proper training session, which lets the team learn and ask questions interactively. A lessons-learned document or recorded highlights can't convey deep specialized expertise as effectively as training."},

{"src":"PMP 2","n":83,"lang":"en",
 "q":"A high-performing team is involved in a project with a new domain. The team is unable to deliver as expected. What should the project manager do?",
 "opts":["Dissolve the team and include the team members in other existing teams","Set up a clear expectation that the team is to deliver as in their previous projects","Support the team in identifying the area of development and addressing the gap","Identify the struggling team members and move them out of the team"],
 "c":["C"],
 "e":"Even a strong team can struggle in an unfamiliar domain, so support them in identifying the development area and closing the gap (training, resources). Dissolving the team, demanding past performance, or removing members ignores the real cause—the new domain learning curve."},

{"src":"PMP 2","n":84,"lang":"en",
 "q":"At a project meeting, a business analyst requires input from a marketing consultant to continue working on a task. The business analyst has tried to contact the consultant but has been told that the consultant does not have the time to discuss the project. What should the project manager do to move the project forward?",
 "opts":["Discuss the request with the consultant and set up time with the business analyst and consultant to discuss the project requirements","Escalate the issues about the consultant to the functional manager so the consultant dedicates the necessary time to the project","Urge the business analyst to continue contacting the consultant to discuss the project since this is the responsibility of the business analyst","Include a new project risk into the risk register and allocate the consultant as the risk owner as it is their responsibility to provide input"],
 "c":["A"],
 "e":"As a servant leader removing the impediment, the PM engages the consultant directly and arranges a meeting between them and the business analyst. Escalating, pushing it back on the analyst, or logging a risk don't actively clear the blocker."},

{"src":"PMP 2","n":85,"lang":"en",
 "q":"A project manager has expertise in the technical domain. The project manager frequently demonstrates unacceptable behavior toward other team members, however, when confronted, the project manager denies this behavior. What is the reason for the project manager's behavior?",
 "opts":["The project manager lacks the necessary empathy","The project manager was not properly trained","The project manager lacks emotional intelligence (EI)","The project manager is in the wrong project role"],
 "c":["C"],
 "e":"Behaving poorly toward others and being unaware of it (denying it when confronted) is a hallmark of low emotional intelligence—the inability to recognize and regulate one's own and others' emotions. Empathy is one component of EI; the broader root cause here is lacking EI."},

{"src":"PMP 2","n":86,"lang":"en",
 "q":"A project manager has been assigned with initiating a large project to deliver complex equipment. Part of the project will be the result of a long research process, but once this is completed, the other part of the project must be incrementally delivered to the customer. Which approach should the project manager choose for this project?",
 "opts":["Agile","Adaptive","Predictive","Hybrid"],
 "c":["D"],
 "e":"One part is a long, plan-driven research effort (predictive) and the other is incremental delivery (agile), so combining them is a hybrid approach. Pure agile, adaptive, or predictive wouldn't suit both parts of the work."},

{"src":"PMP 2","n":87,"lang":"en",
 "q":"A project team strongly encourages the project manager to purchase an application that will significantly reduce work time. Without the application, the deadline might be missed. However, the purchase will impact the project cost. The sponsor would like to explore a less expensive application that will save the team's time. What should the project manager do?",
 "opts":["Purchase the application","Create a decision tree","Analyze assumptions and constraints","Raise a change request"],
 "c":["B"],
 "e":"A decision tree analyzes the options (expensive app, cheaper app, status quo) and their cost/schedule outcomes to make an informed, data-based choice. Buying immediately, analyzing assumptions, or raising a change request comes before objectively comparing the alternatives."},

{"src":"PMP 2","n":88,"lang":"en",
 "q":"When trying to track project benefits, a project manager realizes that some key performance indicators (KPIs) are not yet defined. What should the project manager do?",
 "opts":["Forward the issue to the benefit owner and ask them to collect the missing data and build the KPIs","Ask the program manager to track and collect the missing data and work to build the KPIs","Coordinate with the benefit owner to track and collect the missing data and build the KPIs","Work with the business analysts to track and collect the missing data and build the KPIs"],
 "c":["C"],
 "e":"Collaborate with the benefit owner—who has a vested interest in the benefits—to gather the data and define the KPIs together. Simply forwarding it to them, or going to the program manager or analysts instead, is less effective than working jointly with the benefit owner."},

{"src":"PMP 2","n":89,"lang":"en",
 "q":"A facility extension project is in the execution stage. There is a project activity to connect the power of all new equipment to an existing facility, Point A, which requires a significant cable length. The construction team found that the power could be connected to Point B and save money. What should the project manager do next?",
 "opts":["Perform a cost-benefit analysis to evaluate using the Point B option","Seek a decision from the construction team after explaining the project budget and schedule constraint","Add the issue to the change log and request additional budget through the change control board (CCB)","Use the Point B option because it will save the project cost and time"],
 "c":["A"],
 "e":"Perform a cost-benefit analysis to objectively evaluate whether the Point B option truly provides the best value before acting. Letting the construction team decide, raising a change request, or switching to Point B immediately all act before the option is properly evaluated."},

{"src":"PMP 2","n":90,"lang":"en",
 "q":"A project team consisting of various professionals of different nationalities is struggling to achieve their project deliverables. What should the project manager do?",
 "opts":["Inform the project sponsor and train the entire team","Ask the human resources (HR) department to conduct performance training","Provide the team with support to solve their own problems","Ask the functional department manager to replace the team members"],
 "c":["C"],
 "e":"A servant-leader PM supports the team in solving their own problems, empowering them rather than taking over or replacing them. Escalating, outsourcing training to HR, or replacing members doesn't develop the team's own capability."},

{"src":"PMP 2","n":91,"lang":"en",
 "q":"A company wants to be a pioneer in its industry and has announced the launch date of a new innovative product. After the first sprint planning, the team realizes that it is not possible to deliver all the features in the required time. What should the project manager do to ensure success?",
 "opts":["Ask the customer to reduce the backlog to meet the launch date","Increase the capacity of the team to deliver the completed backlog on time","Assist the team in defining a minimum viable product (MVP) by the launch date","Schedule a meeting with the stakeholders to review the contingency plan"],
 "c":["C"],
 "e":"Help the team define an MVP—the highest-value subset of features deliverable by the launch date. Asking the customer to cut the backlog, adding capacity, or reviewing a contingency plan doesn't focus delivery on the most valuable product for the deadline like an MVP does."},

{"src":"PMP 2","n":92,"lang":"en",
 "q":"In a project, one team member has been asking for directions and what actions to take on many tasks. As a result, the team member has performed slowly in project delivery. What should the project manager do to help this team member?",
 "opts":["Mentor the team member on a regular basis","Empower the team member to make decisions","Assign another senior team member to coach the team member","Address the team member's performance accordingly"],
 "c":["A"],
 "e":"Regular mentoring by the PM builds the member's confidence and decision-making ability so they need less direction over time. Empowering them outright may be premature, delegating coaching to others shifts the PM's responsibility, and just addressing performance doesn't provide the support to improve."},

{"src":"PMP 2","n":93,"lang":"en",
 "q":"A project manager has been assigned to a new project that will be delivered using an agile framework. The sponsor is asking for a cost estimation to build the business case. What should the project manager do?",
 "opts":["Review the completion costs of similar projects","Ensure the client representative is involved in the estimation","Ensure that a 20% contingency is included in the estimation","Review the lessons learned from similar projects"],
 "c":["A"],
 "e":"Analogous estimating—using the actual completion costs of similar past projects—provides a quick, credible cost baseline for the business case in an agile context. Lessons learned, a fixed 20% contingency, or client involvement don't give the cost figure the sponsor needs."},

{"src":"PMP 2","n":94,"lang":"en",
 "q":"A project manager is managing a government project that targets the development of small businesses in remote towns. The project team is having difficulty conducting entrepreneurship training in some of the targeted towns because the local municipal governments will not authorize the training. As a result of the delay, project implementation is at risk. What is a probable reason for this?",
 "opts":["The project did not fully engage the project sponsor","The project should have constructed training centers in the remote towns","The project should have representatives in all the remote towns","The project did not identify and engage the correct stakeholders"],
 "c":["D"],
 "e":"The local municipal governments are key stakeholders whose authorization is required; the difficulty signals they weren't properly identified and engaged. Sponsor engagement, training centers, or local representatives don't explain the missing authorization the way unengaged stakeholders do."},

{"src":"PMP 2","n":95,"lang":"en",
 "q":"During project initiation meetings, the project manager of a 9-month project states that a specific task would be out of schedule. At the project kick-off meeting, a key project stakeholder strongly objects, stating that this task is the key value of the project and should be completed on time. What should the project manager do to reduce the chances of having a strong rejection from stakeholders during the project initiation stage?",
 "opts":["Develop a stakeholder-by-stakeholder engagement assessment matrix","Schedule meetings with key stakeholders in advance to gather feedback on the project scope","Send a letter to the stakeholders to inform them about the project scope and start date","Identify stakeholder rejection as a risk in the project risk register"],
 "c":["B"],
 "e":"Meeting key stakeholders in advance to gather their feedback on scope lets them raise concerns early, preventing surprises and strong rejections at kick-off. An assessment matrix, a one-way letter, or logging a risk don't proactively surface concerns beforehand."},

{"src":"PMP 2","n":96,"lang":"en",
 "q":"A laboratory is launching a new product, defined to be executed in eight iterations with a fixed release date. At the beginning of the second iteration, the project manager realizes that a significant regulatory feature is missing in the project backlog. What should the project manager do?",
 "opts":["Estimate the related cost and ask for a project extension","Increase the capacity of the team to include the missed feature","Invite stakeholders to discuss the impact of adding the regulatory feature","Ask the product owner to add the missing feature in the backlog"],
 "c":["C"],
 "e":"Engage stakeholders (including the product owner, business, and customer) to assess the impact of adding the mandatory regulatory feature before deciding how to proceed. Simply adding it to the backlog, extending the project, or adding capacity acts before the impact and trade-offs are evaluated."},

{"src":"PMP 2","n":97,"lang":"en",
 "q":"An agile team is developing a new content management system. Some of the team members are struggling to deliver certain features since they have never worked on this type of solution. What should the project manager do?",
 "opts":["Replace the members with new members who understand the system","Encourage the team to hold a knowledge-sharing session in each iteration","Ask the team to read through all of the available system documents","Encourage the team members to make changes directly in the system"],
 "c":["B"],
 "e":"Knowledge-sharing sessions each iteration let team members share expertise, learn from each other, and close the knowledge gap on the unfamiliar solution. Replacing members, reading docs alone, or experimenting directly in the system are less effective at building shared capability."},

{"src":"PMP 2","n":98,"lang":"en",
 "q":"A project manager is assigned to work on a complex and innovative project. The technology being used and developed is new and complicated, and most of the stakeholders are unfamiliar with it. Which approach should the project manager use as a servant leader?",
 "opts":["Allocate resources to provide training about the new technology to the stakeholders","Escalate the issue with upper management to find a novel solution for this situation.","Ask stakeholders to find and self-fund education about this new technology","Contact the project sponsor to get those stakeholders removed from the project"],
 "c":["A"],
 "e":"As a servant leader, provide the resources/training to bring stakeholders up to speed on the new technology, enabling their understanding and engagement. Escalating, making stakeholders self-fund education, or removing them fails to serve and develop the stakeholders."},

{"src":"PMP 2","n":99,"lang":"en",
 "q":"A project is ready to deliver the first batch of the product when a quality control check rejects the product as unfit for delivery. The root cause analysis (RCA) shows that a component added as part of the latest change request was not tested throughout the development cycle. What should the project manager do next?",
 "opts":["Review the quality management process and perform a process audit as part of ongoing quality assurance","Review the change control process to ensure quality management artifacts are updated as part of the change","Review the quality management process with the project management office (PMO) to ensure compliance with best practices","Review the communications management plan to ensure change requests are communicated to the affected team members"],
 "c":["A"],
 "e":"A component slipping through untested points to a quality assurance gap, so review the quality management process and perform a process audit to find and fix why testing was missed. Reviewing change control, the PMO, or communications doesn't directly address the QA process that let untested work through."},

{"src":"PMP 2","n":100,"lang":"en",
 "q":"A key project stakeholder does not attend any of the project meetings and is providing false information that the project team is not delivering on time. What should the project manager do to address this situation?",
 "opts":["Email the latest status report to the key stakeholder","Share the meeting recordings with the key stakeholder","Discuss communication needs with the key stakeholder","Create a new communication artifact for the key stakeholder"],
 "c":["C"],
 "e":"Since the stakeholder doesn't attend meetings, the current communication method isn't working—sit down and discuss their communication needs to find an approach that works for them. Emailing reports, sharing recordings, or creating an artifact assumes a method without first understanding their needs."},

{"src":"PMP 2","n":101,"lang":"en",
 "q":"A project team is preparing a report for the stakeholders. The team gathers and collates a large amount of data to be included in the status report. What should the project manager do next?",
 "opts":["Instruct the team to analyze and interpret the data before including it in the report","Instruct the team to make only a graphical representation of all the data for the report","Instruct the team to conduct a meeting with the stakeholders before finalizing the report","Ensure the team follows standard operating procedures (SOP) for creating a stakeholder report"],
 "c":["A"],
 "e":"Raw data must be analyzed and interpreted into work performance information before it's useful to stakeholders. Just graphing all the data, meeting first, or following an SOP doesn't guarantee the report contains meaningful, relevant insights."},

{"src":"PMP 2","n":102,"lang":"en",
 "q":"A senior vice president requested the creation of more agile teams to run projects of varying lengths and complexities in parallel with each other. This will impact the amount of time the project managers will be able to dedicate to each project. The project managers typically dedicate 50% of their time to projects regardless of their complexity. What should the project manager do as a servant leader to help achieve this transformation?",
 "opts":["Review the organizational process assets (OPAs) to evaluate mitigation plans for similar risks","Create a risk entry regarding the project manager's capacity and develop a mitigation plan","Empower the team to develop the necessary skills to move the project forward independently","Issue a change request to hire additional project managers based on the increased workload"],
 "c":["C"],
 "e":"With PMs spread across parallel projects, the servant leader empowers teams to build the skills to self-manage and progress independently. Logging a capacity risk or hiring more PMs doesn't build the team autonomy that scaling agile requires."},

{"src":"PMP 2","n":103,"lang":"en",
 "q":"During an iteration review session, some product features are at risk of being canceled due to high operational costs when the product is launched into a production environment. What type of analysis should the project lead have conducted to avoid this situation?",
 "opts":["Gap analysis","Capacity analysis","Benefits analysis","Risk analysis"],
 "c":["C"],
 "e":"A benefits analysis weighs each feature's business value against its costs (including operational costs), so low-value/high-cost features are deprioritized early rather than risking cancellation late. Gap, capacity, or risk analysis don't drive value-vs-cost prioritization."},

{"src":"PMP 2","n":104,"lang":"en",
 "q":"A project manager is working as a servant leader for a pilot project to adopt agile practices. After three sprints, the team demos the product to the main stakeholders but receives negative feedback about the product's features. The team feels that all the features met the acceptance criteria defined by the product owner and does not understand what went wrong. How should the project manager mitigate product rejection?",
 "opts":["Create dedicated sprints to implement feature fixes for the rejected ones","Engage the main stakeholders in the upcoming sprint review sessions","Ask the project sponsor to evaluate the assignment of a new product owner","Share the product backlog for the main stakeholders to review in advance"],
 "c":["B"],
 "e":"Engaging the main stakeholders in the sprint reviews gives them a platform for direct, frequent feedback so the product adapts to their expectations early—preventing late rejection. Fix-sprints, replacing the PO, or sharing the backlog don't provide the ongoing inspect-and-adapt feedback loop."},

{"src":"PMP 2","n":105,"lang":"en",
 "q":"A company plans to initiate a project involving a new technology. Approval for the project is required. What should the project manager do?",
 "opts":["Identify the risks in implementing the new technology","Define change management for the new technology","Analyze the cost and identify the project benefits","Review the cost and schedule baselines for execution"],
 "c":["C"],
 "e":"To gain approval, build the business case by analyzing the cost and identifying the project's benefits (the value justification). Risk identification, change management, or baselines come later, during planning/execution, not for the initial go decision."},

{"src":"PMP 2","n":106,"lang":"en",
 "q":"An agile project will be delivered to a client based on a fixed price in cryptocurrency. Due to the volatility of cryptocurrency, both parties have agreed to adjust the scope to the value of the cryptocurrency at the end of every iteration and halt the project when the agreed price is reached. Which approach should be used in this situation?",
 "opts":["A flow-based approach starting with the smallest stories","A predictive approach with short phases","An iterative approach starting with the highest value epic","An incremental approach with a minimum viable product (MVP)"],
 "c":["C"],
 "e":"Since scope is re-evaluated 'at the end of every iteration,' an iterative approach that tackles the highest-value epic first ensures the most value is delivered before the project may halt. The phrasing (iterations, refining) points to iterative, value-first sequencing."},

{"src":"PMP 2","n":107,"lang":"en",
 "q":"A project manager is working on a financial system implementation project for a government company. One of the key stakeholders, who uses predictive approaches, does not like virtual tools such as chats, task trackers, and so forth. They prefer email, telephone calls, and face-to-face meetings. How should the project manager approach this situation?",
 "opts":["Ask the manager to use virtual communication tools and online dashboards","Align and agree with the stakeholder on the communication approach","Create a digital dashboard to meet the manager's information needs","Use regular screen-sharing sessions to show the progress to the stakeholder"],
 "c":["B"],
 "e":"Understand the stakeholder's preferences and agree together on a communication approach that works for them, respecting their preferred methods. Forcing virtual tools or dashboards ignores their stated preference and risks disengagement."},

{"src":"PMP 2","n":108,"lang":"en",
 "q":"A project manager is leading an electrical project that spans five towns. The project manager is informed by the team that one of the towns is not allowing the team to work there even though the town was included in stakeholder engagements before the start of the project. What should the project manager do?",
 "opts":["Instruct the team to move to the next town and continue the project to avoid further delays","Change the project scope and seek approval to exclude this town from the overall project","Retrieve all minutes of stakeholder engagements and send to the town as a reminder of their commitment to the project","Engage with the town's authorities to discuss the reason for the change in commitment and agree on a solution"],
 "c":["D"],
 "e":"Engage the town's authorities directly to understand why their commitment changed and collaboratively find a solution. Skipping the town, excluding it from scope, or just sending past minutes doesn't address the underlying reason for the resistance."},

{"src":"PMP 2","n":109,"lang":"en",
 "q":"A vendor has manufactured a complex product. During a factory acceptance test, several deficiencies were identified. Product delivery is on the critical path, and any delay would impact project completion. What should the project manager do?",
 "opts":["Ship the package as is, complete carryover work on-site, and back charge the vendor for the rework","Hold the shipment until the rework is completed at the vendor's facility to eliminate extra work on-site","Assess the schedule impact and evaluate the most feasible solution to keep the project on track","Initiate regular conference calls with the vendor for status updates to keep this item in focus"],
 "c":["C"],
 "e":"Assess the schedule impact of the deficiencies and evaluate the most feasible option to keep the critical-path delivery on track. Shipping as-is, holding the shipment, or just adding status calls commit to a path before the impact and options are analyzed."},

{"src":"PMP 2","n":110,"lang":"en",
 "q":"A company has started developing a new product. During execution, the project team notices that another competitor has launched a product with very similar functionalities and a cheaper market price. What should have been done differently to avoid this scenario?",
 "opts":["A minimum viable product (MVP) should have been released to get feedback from the market","An iterative life cycle approach should have been adopted","The Kanban method should have been utilized to optimize the work in progress (WIP)","More backlog refinement meetings should have been scheduled"],
 "c":["A"],
 "e":"Releasing an MVP early would have gotten a viable product to market sooner, generated feedback, and reduced the risk of being beaten by a competitor. Iterative cycles, Kanban, or more refinement don't deliver to market as quickly as an MVP."},

{"src":"PMP 2","n":111,"lang":"en",
 "q":"A project manager entered into a contract with a vendor to build a new system. During the acceptance test stage, the project manager noticed that the system quality was not satisfactory. How should the project manager support their claim to the vendor?",
 "opts":["Show that the acceptance criteria were changed by the vendor without approval","Document in the risk log that the issues in the system were already identified","Provide the failed test results along with the agreed-upon acceptance criteria","Share unsuccessful results from previous projects with a similar scope"],
 "c":["C"],
 "e":"Present the failed test results against the agreed-upon acceptance criteria to objectively prove the deliverable doesn't conform. Claims of changed criteria, risk-log entries, or results from other projects aren't the contractual evidence that the agreed criteria weren't met."},

{"src":"PMP 2","n":112,"lang":"en",
 "q":"A mid-size capital project has shared resources from the company's contract department. During the execution phase, the project team observed a delay in contracting. After communicating with the contract department, the project manager found the contract team is prioritizing other contracting jobs because they are not part of the project team. What should the project manager do first in this situation?",
 "opts":["Report the issue to higher management and seek their direct involvement to complete the job from the contract department","Arrange a meeting with the contract department to build trust and commitment by recognizing their critical support to the project.","Communicate with the contract manager to commit a budget for the resources required for project execution","Arrange several meetings with the contract department and ask them to contract the project as it is critical for project success"],
 "c":["B"],
 "e":"Meet the contract department to build trust and commitment by recognizing their critical role and clarifying the project's importance and the delay's impact. Escalating, committing budget, or repeatedly demanding work doesn't earn the engagement of this shared resource."},

{"src":"PMP 2","n":113,"lang":"en",
 "q":"During sprint planning, the product owner wants to prioritize items with high business value. However, the project team is concerned about technical debt and infrastructure dependencies to deliver the expected value. How should the project manager handle this issue?",
 "opts":["Support the product owner so only items with high business value are included in the next sprint","Support the project team so technical debt and infrastructure dependencies are included in the next sprint","Support the project sponsor so the decision is made top-down and the team follows the provided direction","Support a voting exercise so all team members can express their opinion and the reason behind their vote"],
 "c":["D"],
 "e":"Facilitate a collaborative discussion/voting where the team and PO surface the reasons (the PO may not know the technical dependencies needed to realize value). Blindly backing the PO or the team, or imposing a top-down decision, ignores the shared understanding needed to balance value and technical debt."},

{"src":"PMP 2","n":114,"lang":"en",
 "q":"A project manager has been asked to participate in the procurement process for a software application that allows customers to buy online e-learning courses. The project manager will take over the project once the supplier has been sourced. What should the project manager do before the project starts?",
 "opts":["Ensure that the requirements for the project are understood and that the objectives are verified before approval of an agreement","Notify the project sponsor that the project manager cannot be involved in the agreement negotiation process","Ask the project sponsor to involve the legal department in the negotiation for the project agreement","Inform the project sponsor that once the agreement has been signed, there cannot be any changes to the scope of the project"],
 "c":["A"],
 "e":"Before the agreement is approved, ensure the requirements are understood and objectives verified so the procurement aligns with the project's goals and it can be executed successfully. Declining involvement, deferring to legal, or declaring no future changes don't ensure that critical alignment up front."},

{"src":"PMP 2","n":115,"lang":"en",
 "q":"A project manager is assigned to a regulatory project for their country. The deadline for delivering results in compliance with the new laws is very tight, and the project team members have not yet been assigned. One of the project manager's peers informs the project manager that a project with the same scope for a different country was completed 1 year ago. What should the project manager do first?",
 "opts":["Register a new risk in the risk management plan","Complete a bottom-up estimation for the project activities","Ask for additional resources and review the project estimate","Review organizational process assets (OPAs) and perform analogous estimating"],
 "c":["D"],
 "e":"Leverage the similar completed project: review OPAs (including its lessons learned and data) and perform analogous estimating for a quick initial estimate. Bottom-up estimating is slower and premature with no team assigned, and registering a risk or asking for resources comes after estimating."},

{"src":"PMP 2","n":116,"lang":"en",
 "q":"A project lead is working on an agile project to build the team's vision, including the decision-making process. It was decided that the team will vote on items, and if 90% of the team agrees on a decision, then it will be carried forward. At the very first technical design meeting, the team makes a decision that the project lead strongly disagrees with. How should the project lead proceed?",
 "opts":["Refuse to let the team proceed with the work based on their decision","Note the reasons for the disagreement and let the team proceed","Take the technical team lead aside and try to persuade them to change their vote","Set up a meeting with the project champion and ask them to intervene"],
 "c":["B"],
 "e":"The lead must respect the team's agreed decision-making process, documenting their concerns in case issues arise later while letting the team proceed—preserving autonomy and trust. Overriding, lobbying a member, or escalating undermines the agreed process the lead helped establish."},

{"src":"PMP 2","n":117,"lang":"en",
 "q":"A project manager is leading a project that has a dependency on the client's vendor management system for critical requirements. The vendor management team will not derive any benefits from the project. However, the project requires support from the vendor management head. What should the project manager do next?",
 "opts":["Work with the sponsor on a communications management plan to obtain support from the vendor management head","Review the terms of reference of the steering committee to include the vendor management head","Obtain help from the sponsor to highlight any nonfinancial benefits for the vendor management team","Get support from the sponsor to hire a subject matter expert (SME) from the vendor management team to work on the project"],
 "c":["A"],
 "e":"Work with the sponsor on a communications plan to convey the project's value and gain the vendor management head's commitment, since they don't see a direct benefit. Adding them to a committee, inventing benefits, or hiring an SME doesn't address the need to communicate and win their support."},

{"src":"PMP 2","n":118,"lang":"en",
 "q":"An enthusiastic, newly hired vice-president has been assigned as the product owner for a digital transformation project. The project manager advises the product owner that some of the functional requirements are not aligned with the chief information technology officer's (CTO) strategy. The product owner tells the project manager that the CTO is not part of the project and should not be engaged at this moment. What should the project manager do?",
 "opts":["Continue building the backlog since they are functional requirements","Review the organizational process assets (OPAs) for historical information from similar projects","Influence the product owner to review the engagement needed from other stakeholders","Advise the CTO about the possible impacts caused by the project"],
 "c":["C"],
 "e":"Influence the product owner to reconsider which stakeholders need to be engaged—the CTO's strategy alignment is important even if the new PO dismisses it. Continuing the backlog, reviewing OPAs, or going around the PO to the CTO directly aren't the right next step."},

{"src":"PMP 2","n":119,"lang":"en",
 "q":"A project manager is working on multiple building construction projects using a predictive project management approach. Senior leadership within the organization is promoting the inclusion of more agile practices into the current project methodologies. The project sponsor now wants the projects to use more agile methodologies. Before mandating any agile methods to be used in a project, what should the project manager do first?",
 "opts":["Shift all existing projects to agile methodologies with a strict timeline","Pause the current projects to align them to the new agile cadence","Assess the organizational culture and readiness for the transformation","Provide agile training to those who will be part of the project team"],
 "c":["D"],
 "e":"Per the source key, provide agile training to the team members first so they understand and can apply the new methods before any mandate. (Note: assessing organizational readiness—option C—is also a common PMI answer for a transformation; use the Analyze-with-Claude button to weigh training vs. readiness assessment.)"},

{"src":"PMP 2","n":120,"lang":"en",
 "q":"A project team was hired to perform on-site duties, but there were unexpected circumstances that required the entire team to work remotely. Some team members returned to their hometowns, and they are now requesting reimbursement for operational expenditures. What should the project manager do?",
 "opts":["Follow the Perform Integrated Change Control process and submit a change request","Review the budget and allocate funds for the reimbursements","Ask the team members to draft a justification before processing the reimbursements","Update the project management plan with the incurred costs"],
 "c":["A"],
 "e":"The shift to remote work and the new reimbursement costs affect the budget, so follow Perform Integrated Change Control and submit a change request for evaluation and approval. Allocating funds or updating the plan without change control bypasses governance."},

{"src":"PMP 2","n":121,"lang":"en",
 "q":"In the middle of the execution phase for a big project, a new stakeholder who was unfamiliar with the project joined the board of directors and is influencing the decisions made in the project. This change is impacting the project's progress. What should the project manager do first?",
 "opts":["Update the risk register with a new risk","Revise the project schedule","Present the project to the stakeholder","Inform the client about the change"],
 "c":["C"],
 "e":"Present the project to the new, unfamiliar stakeholder so they understand its goals, status, and the impact of their decisions—aligning their influence with the project. Logging a risk, revising the schedule, or informing the client doesn't address the stakeholder's lack of understanding driving the problem."},

{"src":"PMP 2","n":122,"lang":"en",
 "q":"The project sponsor reports that a key stakeholder is complaining about performance differences in the metrics reported by the project manager, which gives the impression that the project is out of control. The project manager has been providing the project's status according to the agreed-upon communications management plan. This key stakeholder, however, seldom attends the status report meetings. What should the project manager do first to address this situation?",
 "opts":["Provide evidence to the project sponsor that the project is on track and the communications have been sent","Review the resource breakdown structure (RBS) to request the main stakeholder's manager to replace the stakeholder","Evaluate the communication needs to discover the gaps and adjust the original plan if needed","Use the contingency reserves to implement fast tracking and improve the schedule key performance indicators (KPIs)"],
 "c":["C"],
 "e":"Since the stakeholder isn't getting the message (they skip meetings), evaluate the communication needs to find the gap and adjust the plan so they receive and understand the status. Defending to the sponsor, trying to replace the stakeholder, or fast-tracking the schedule don't fix the communication gap."},

{"src":"PMP 2","n":123,"lang":"en",
 "q":"An experienced team member has joined a project during the execution phase. What should the project manager do to ensure the new team member engages with the team quickly?",
 "opts":["Send an email to the new team member with the status of the project and assigned tasks","Ask a team member to set up a meeting to communicate the project's detailed scope and task assignments to the new member","Set up a meeting with the new team member to go through all of the project documents and assigned tasks","Allow the new team member to meet with the team and start their assigned tasks independently"],
 "c":["C"],
 "e":"A new member is in the forming stage and needs the PM's guidance, so meet them to walk through the project documents and their assignments for proper onboarding. An email, delegating onboarding to a teammate, or leaving them to start alone don't provide that guidance."},

{"src":"PMP 2","n":124,"lang":"en",
 "q":"Two members of a new project team disagree on the best approach to solve a problem. The conflict has been going on for 1 week. The two members are tense around each other and are taking everything personally. What should the project manager do?",
 "opts":["Help the team members to resolve the conflict in a way that results in the best team performance.","Send both team members to the human resources (HR) department for an acceptable resolution","Understand each team member's argument and select the one that results in less risk to the project","Offer a compromise that is acceptable to both team members, even if it may not be the best solution"],
 "c":["A"],
 "e":"Facilitate a collaborate/problem-solve resolution that considers both viewpoints and yields the best team performance and project outcome (win-win). Escalating to HR, the PM picking a side, or settling for a lose-lose compromise are inferior to genuine collaboration."},

{"src":"PMP 2","n":125,"lang":"en",
 "q":"A project manager is assigned to a new project team that has several stakeholders who can have significant influence over the project outcomes. The project manager categorized the stakeholders into four different groups and wants to define the communication strategy. How should the project manager handle communication?",
 "opts":["Utilize a uniform communication structure across all groups","Describe the four groups in the project stakeholder matrix","Define and direct communication to influential stakeholders","Develop and define a communication strategy for stakeholders."],
 "c":["D"],
 "e":"Develop a communication strategy tailored to the different stakeholder groups so each group's needs are met effectively. A uniform structure ignores the differences, only describing groups or focusing solely on influential stakeholders doesn't define how to communicate with all of them."},

{"src":"PMP 2","n":126,"lang":"en",
 "q":"A project manager is leading a project to develop accounting software for a trading firm. As the outcomes are being delivered, a new risk is identified regarding an indirect tax regulation that is being changed by the government in the next 6 months. This risk will have a significant impact on the project outcomes. What should the project manager do next?",
 "opts":["Determine the impact of the risk and prioritize outcomes that do not affect the tax module","Assess the impact of the risk with an expert and prioritize further outcomes with the client","Analyze the impact of the risk and discuss with the team to determine how to separate the tax module","Delay developing the indirect tax module until the tax regulation comes into effect"],
 "c":["B"],
 "e":"Assess the regulatory risk's impact with a tax expert (for an accurate assessment) and re-prioritize outcomes with the client. Prioritizing around the module, separating it with the team, or delaying it act before the impact is properly understood with the right expertise and the client."},

{"src":"PMP 2","n":127,"lang":"en",
 "q":"A project manager is working on an agile project in a changing business environment. The project sponsor is worried about benefits realization and asks the project manager to pay attention to changes that can affect the project. Which action should the project manager take to identify changes that may affect the product?",
 "opts":["Apply a pair programming technique to react to changes immediately, even in the middle of the iteration","Implement face-to-face communication between the team and product owner to change the iteration objectives as needed","Take action to reprioritize the backlog after every iteration to evaluate if user stories are still valuable to the business","Stop the iteration as soon as possible in case an external change that makes the product invaluable is identified"],
 "c":["C"],
 "e":"Re-prioritizing the backlog after every iteration (with the product owner) lets the team continually re-evaluate whether stories still deliver business value as the environment changes. Pair programming, mid-iteration objective changes, or stopping iterations don't provide that regular value reassessment."},

{"src":"PMP 2","n":128,"lang":"en",
 "q":"An organization is adopting an agile mindset. On the first agile project, the project manager faced an issue because the team was unable to make decisions on time. What should the project manager do to address the problem?",
 "opts":["Invite senior leadership to a team-building workshop and restate the importance of accountability and leadership under the new company direction","Adopt an autocratic leadership style and dictate all decisions in order to speed up the team's velocity","Assess the situation to understand how the organization's culture impacts the decision-making process, and coach the team toward a new model","Define clear guidelines on how decisions should be made under the new organizational policy and reinforce them with the team on every occasion"],
 "c":["C"],
 "e":"Understand how the organization's culture is hindering decision-making, then coach the team toward the new self-organizing model—a transition that takes adaptation. Autocratic control contradicts agile, and inviting leadership or just dictating guidelines doesn't build the team's decision-making capability."},

{"src":"PMP 2","n":129,"lang":"en",
 "q":"A project is approaching successful completion, and all stakeholders are really proud of the achieved results. The project management office (PMO) recommends the project manager to lead the next similar project. What should the project manager do next?",
 "opts":["Engage key stakeholders to complete and deliver lessons learned","Select the same stakeholders for the next project to avoid doing a knowledge transfer","Request the PMO to manage the lessons learned repository","Present the project achievements and delegate knowledge transfer to the steering committee"],
 "c":["A"],
 "e":"Before leaving, engage key stakeholders to complete and capture lessons learned—the PM knows the details and this is part of proper closing. Reusing the same stakeholders, offloading lessons learned to the PMO, or delegating knowledge transfer skips the PM's closing responsibility."},

{"src":"PMP 2","n":130,"lang":"en",
 "q":"A project manager is using a subcontractor for mobile application development. When the project team tests the application, they realize it does not comply with the new company user-interface standards. Even though these new standards were not included in the statement of work (SOW), the subcontractor agrees to the additional scope as long as payment for this additional work is paid immediately. What should the project manager do?",
 "opts":["Negotiate new pricing based on the revised estimations from the subcontractor that reflect the new work","Initiate a change to incorporate an immediate payment milestone in the contract as per the subcontractor's request","Initiate a change request and review the contract to evaluate the payment options before responding","Deny the subcontractor's payment request, because the product does not comply with the project needs and close the project"],
 "c":["C"],
 "e":"The new UI standards are additional scope not in the SOW, so initiate a change request and review the contract terms to evaluate payment options before agreeing. Negotiating pricing or accepting an immediate-payment milestone without analysis, or denying and closing, skip proper change control and contract review."},

{"src":"PMP 2","n":131,"lang":"en",
 "q":"An external project manager is managing the construction of new corporate offices for a large company. The project management plan states that it is necessary to contract a highly trained external resource to revise and validate an important project component. The client has rejected hiring the external resource because of the high cost, even though it is within the project budget. What should the project manager do to resolve this issue?",
 "opts":["Hire the resource in order to achieve the project objectives","Evaluate the consequences and meet with the client to explain the possible scenarios","Try to accomplish the task internally by leveling resources from other projects","Ask the client for a meeting and explain that the task will not be performed"],
 "c":["B"],
 "e":"Evaluate the consequences of not hiring the specialist, then meet the client to explain the scenarios and impact so they can make an informed decision. Overriding the client, improvising internally, or refusing the task bypass understanding and transparent dialogue."},

{"src":"PMP 2","n":132,"lang":"en",
 "q":"A project to construct an electricity generation plant is on-site near a nature reserve. An environmental activist group is questioning the need for the project. The customer has informed the project manager to disregard the group as they are not in support of the project. What should the project manager do next?",
 "opts":["Obey the directive from the customer since they are funding the project and are the most important stakeholder","Add the activist group to the list of stakeholders and analyze the impact they may have on project delivery","Request the project team disregard all communication from the activist group and continue with their project work","Ask the customer to contact the activist group and let them know the project status and completion date"],
 "c":["B"],
 "e":"Even opposing groups are stakeholders who can impact delivery (delays, legal issues), so add the activist group to the stakeholder list and analyze their potential impact. Disregarding them as the customer directs, or just informing them of dates, ignores a real project risk."},

{"src":"PMP 2","n":133,"lang":"en",
 "q":"A project had scheduling delays, which necessitated the team to work overtime to meet critical milestones. This led to some misunderstandings among team members. The project sponsor has allocated a monetary award. How should the project manager utilize the award?",
 "opts":["Draft a points system to allocate a portion of the money for each resource","Arrange a luncheon for all team members and include team-building sessions","Allocate the reward to the team leaders to spend on the team as they see fit","Meet with the team members and ask them how to spend the reward"],
 "c":["D"],
 "e":"Engaging the team in deciding how to spend the reward values their input, boosts morale and cohesion, and helps mend the misunderstandings. A points system, a pre-decided luncheon, or letting leaders decide doesn't include the whole team in the decision."},

{"src":"PMP 2","n":134,"lang":"en",
 "q":"During iteration two of a project with two teams (A and B), the project manager is concerned that there are two critical activities from team A that must be started in iteration four. Additionally, these two activities are dependent on an activity from team B that has not yet been prioritized. What should the project manager do?",
 "opts":["Include the dependent activity in the current iteration for Team B","Ensure team B prioritizes the dependent activity in iteration three","Cancel the current iteration and reschedule it with the dependent activity","Assign the two critical activities to team B in iteration two"],
 "c":["B"],
 "e":"Have team B prioritize the dependent activity in iteration three so it's complete before team A's critical activities start in iteration four, keeping the project on schedule. Forcing it into the current iteration, canceling, or reassigning activities are disruptive or don't resolve the dependency timing."},

{"src":"PMP 2","n":135,"lang":"en",
 "q":"A project is near completion but it is behind schedule. The contingency budget has already being used, although the remaining budget should be enough to complete the project. The only subject matter expert (SME) assigned to the project has been asked by their manager to solve a problem in the operations department. What should the project manager do?",
 "opts":["Free the resource to handle the operations priority","Design a negotiation strategy for this situation","Ask the manager to submit a change request","Escalate the situation to the project sponsor"],
 "c":["B"],
 "e":"The sole SME is critical to finishing the at-risk project, so develop a negotiation strategy to discuss with the manager and reach a resolution that protects the project. Simply releasing the SME, demanding a change request, or escalating doesn't negotiate a workable solution."},

{"src":"PMP 2","n":136,"lang":"en",
 "q":"A project manager is working on a major construction project. Part of the agreed-upon plan was to use a cloud-based system with a server that runs on high-speed internet service since most of the team members work virtually. The company needs to reduce operating costs as much as possible and management insists that the project will now use a system running on a conventional dial-up service. What should the project manager do next?",
 "opts":["Meet with management about their decision and ask them to get another project manager","Replace some of the team members that work virtually to help the company save money","Evaluate the impact of this decision and communicate with management","Agree with management and continue working the project management plan"],
 "c":["C"],
 "e":"Any change to the agreed, approved plan should be analyzed first: evaluate the impact of switching to dial-up and communicate the findings to management. Refusing, replacing team members, or blindly agreeing all skip the impact analysis management needs to make an informed decision."},

{"src":"PMP 2","n":137,"lang":"en",
 "q":"A senior project manager has included a junior project manager as part of the project team. During a coaching session, the junior project manager asks the senior project manager how to be efficient and reduce rework. How should the senior project manager respond?",
 "opts":["Schedule daily meetings with key stakeholders to assess the effectiveness of the project management plan.","Assign project tasks to the team regardless of their skills to accomplish the project schedule","Monitor resource allocation to ensure that team members work 8 hours per day","Use a work breakdown structure (WBS) to create a project schedule based on the project and resource requirements"],
 "c":["A"],
 "e":"Frequent (daily) collaboration meetings improve planning, surface impediments early, and build in quality—reducing rework through transparency. A WBS builds the schedule but doesn't drive efficiency, and the other options don't address rework."},

{"src":"PMP 2","n":138,"lang":"en",
 "q":"A company is working on a project to develop an integration of their existing systems. There are known issues in production. What should the project manager do?",
 "opts":["Include acceptance criteria in the definition of done (DoD)","Review the quality control processes and procedures","Ensure the product owner reviews requirements with the team.","Identify and remove impediments and mitigate the risks"],
 "c":["D"],
 "e":"Known production issues are impediments and risks, so the PM identifies and removes the impediments and mitigates the risks to prevent them from recurring. Adjusting the DoD, reviewing QC, or having the PO review requirements don't directly clear the existing impediments."},

{"src":"PMP 2","n":139,"lang":"en",
 "q":"A healthcare company is in the process of deploying an innovative healthcare solution globally. The project team has completed a limited release. The stakeholders have raised concerns about global healthcare guidelines and risks. What should the project manager do next?",
 "opts":["Review compliance and regulatory requirements with stakeholders","Perform a qualitative risk analysis on the identified project risks","Evaluate lessons learned from similar projects prior to deployment","Conduct a feasibility analysis of deploying the solution globally"],
 "c":["A"],
 "e":"The concern is about global healthcare guidelines (compliance), so review the compliance and regulatory requirements with stakeholders. A feasibility analysis belongs before the project (it's already deploying), and qualitative risk or lessons learned don't directly address the compliance concern."},

{"src":"PMP 2","n":140,"lang":"en",
 "q":"A project team has established contracts for purchasing vehicles and construction services. In the middle of project execution, the team learns that the vehicle supplier cannot deliver the vehicles. There is not enough time to find a new supplier without delaying the project. Which action should the project manager take to procure the vehicles?",
 "opts":["Arrange a meeting with the client to request additional time to find a new supplier","Procure the vehicles from another supplier and report the change to the change control board (CCB) later","Review the existing service contracts to find an option to help procure the vehicles","Ask the technical team to revise the requirements, even if it requires extra budget"],
 "c":["C"],
 "e":"Quickly review the existing service contracts for an option (e.g., a clause or alternate provision) to procure the vehicles without delay. Requesting more time, procuring outside change control, or revising requirements are slower or bypass proper process."},

{"src":"PMP 2","n":141,"lang":"en",
 "q":"In an agile project, the team decided to have a rotating group of facilitators for the daily scrum. Overall, most of the facilitators have grown and done well in this role; however, a few of them tend to run inefficient daily scrums. What should the project manager do next?",
 "opts":["Return to using the scrum master as the facilitator for the daily scrums now that the trial period is over","Ask the facilitators who are doing well to continue and recommend that the others stop facilitating","Retrain all team members on daily scrum facilitation to ensure there is consistency in the approach","Praise the team for self-organizing and growing, then work one-on-one with the facilitators, as needed"],
 "c":["D"],
 "e":"Recognize the team's self-organization and growth, then coach the struggling facilitators one-on-one—preserving team autonomy while improving. Reverting to the scrum master, stopping some facilitators, or retraining everyone undercuts the self-organizing approach that's mostly working."},

{"src":"PMP 2","n":142,"lang":"en",
 "q":"A local company is developing a new product and, for the first time, using a remote team for the programming tasks of features. The design for the product comes from the local team. During the third sprint review, the product owner has concerns about the project's outcomes. The remote development team is complaining that they do not clearly understand the requirements conveyed in the daily standup meetings. How should the project manager address this situation?",
 "opts":["Evaluate and reassign the developing tasks to a local vendor who has worked on previous projects.","Determine the communication needs, environment, and tools to get the message across","Review lessons learned from previous projects and organizational process assets (OPAs)","Document the risk in the risk management plan and use contingency reserves to hire a local vendor"],
 "c":["B"],
 "e":"The issue is communication with the virtual team, so determine their communication needs, environment, and tools to ensure requirements get across clearly. Reassigning to a local vendor, reviewing OPAs, or hiring don't fix the underlying communication gap."},

{"src":"PMP 2","n":143,"lang":"en",
 "q":"After releasing a new product, the team compiles reports for several weeks on how the product is received by the customers. Which two steps should be taken next? (Choose 2)",
 "opts":["Refine and prioritize the backlog based on the reports","Validate the reports for accuracy with a small subset of customers","Review the reports to ensure that they are easily collectible","Develop new features based on the customer reports","Ask key stakeholders to prioritize the reports based on business goals"],
 "c":["A","E"],
 "e":"Have key stakeholders (including the product owner) prioritize the feedback against business goals, then refine and re-prioritize the product backlog accordingly. Validating with a subset, checking collectibility, or building features directly skip the value-based prioritization of the feedback."},

{"src":"PMP 2","n":144,"lang":"en",
 "q":"An agile team is required to address a technical, complex requirement that has no visual deliverable to show the customer. What guidance should the product manager provide to improve the requirement development?",
 "opts":["Allow the team to focus and complete the complex work because it will reduce the risk of finding issues later in the project","Reprioritize the product backlog by lowering the priority of the complex activity, so it will be worked on later in the project","Discuss this with the team and reprioritize the backlog to deliver maximum value by including a portion of the complex work","Inform the customer that there will not be any deliverables due to the complexity involved in the requirement"],
 "c":["C"],
 "e":"Discuss with the team and re-prioritize the backlog so each iteration delivers maximum value while including a portion of the complex work (and find ways to show it, e.g., diagrams). Focusing only on the complex work, deferring it, or telling the customer there's nothing to show all reduce value or satisfaction."},

{"src":"PMP 2","n":145,"lang":"en",
 "q":"A project team is working on the design of a solution for a complex project. Due to a lack of clarity of the customer's requirements, daily meetings were agreed upon between the team and the customer. There are a few external contractors in the project who do not agree with this idea as they feel it is a waste of time. What should the project manager do?",
 "opts":["Ask the customer to develop a document with the requirements","Warn and penalize the contractors who do not attend the meeting","Welcome and involve the contractors as part of the project team","Ask the contractors to meet with the customer independently"],
 "c":["C"],
 "e":"Agile values collaboration, so welcome and involve the contractors as part of the team—participating in the standups will expose them to the value of the agile approach. A requirements document, penalties, or separate meetings work against collaboration."},

{"src":"PMP 2","n":146,"lang":"en",
 "q":"In order to increase a project's profit margin, the project manager and subject matter experts (SMEs) agreed to select the oldest model of a specific machine available in the market. When the machine reached its destination country, it was blocked by customs authorities who restricted the import of this machine model. What should the project manager have done to prevent this from happening?",
 "opts":["Ensured that the technical team researched and selected the latest model of the machine","Ensured that the SMEs correctly defined the project scope and the machine model","Ensured that regulatory compliance was considered in the quality management plan","Ensured the project sponsor provided enough funding to purchase the latest version of the machine"],
 "c":["C"],
 "e":"Import restrictions are a compliance/regulatory constraint, so compliance requirements should have been identified, considered in the quality management plan, and managed throughout. Choosing the latest model or more funding doesn't address the missed compliance check that caused the customs block."},

{"src":"PMP 2","n":147,"lang":"en",
 "q":"A project is about to start. The project manager has found that the client does not have an appropriate knowledge management system and does not have a requirement for it. What should the project manager do?",
 "opts":["Utilize the cloud wiki to collect the necessary knowledge","Coordinate with the team and the project stakeholders to collect the knowledge","Provide just-in-time knowledge to the client, upon request","Ask the team to collect the knowledge locally and share it at the end of the project"],
 "c":["B"],
 "e":"Manage project knowledge by coordinating with the team and stakeholders to collect and share knowledge throughout (not just at the end), regardless of the client's system. A single tool, on-request-only, or end-of-project sharing don't manage knowledge continuously and collaboratively."},

{"src":"PMP 2","n":148,"lang":"en",
 "q":"After the merger of two large companies, a project manager is assigned to a project. The company hires a consultant to ensure best practices are being used in information security. During an iteration, the consultant requests changes to a software component, claiming that it is not meeting market requirements. What should the project manager do?",
 "opts":["Cancel the iteration and add the requested changes","Cancel the iteration and meet with the product owner","Continue the iteration and review the change with the customer","Continue the iteration and add the requested changes"],
 "c":["C"],
 "e":"Protect the sprint: continue the current iteration and review the requested change with the customer for a future sprint (via the backlog). The consultant's opinion isn't binding mid-sprint; canceling the iteration or injecting changes violates sprint integrity."},

{"src":"PMP 2","n":149,"lang":"en",
 "q":"A new team member on a self-organizing team is finding it difficult to deliver due to the new technology being used in the project. What should the project manager do in this situation?",
 "opts":["Delegate other team members to complete this team member's tasks","Request the team member to move to another team","Mentor the team member and provide the necessary training","Include the team member in an improvement program through a human resources (HR) request"],
 "c":["C"],
 "e":"Mentor the new member and provide training on the new technology—developing people is a core PM responsibility. Reassigning their tasks, moving them off the team, or an HR improvement program don't help them gain the needed skills."},

{"src":"PMP 2","n":150,"lang":"en",
 "q":"A project manager manages a bridge construction project. The project manager has received a major change request from a regulatory stakeholder to add one branch of the bridge to the engineering design. The project manager prepared a change request, which was reviewed and accepted by the change control board (CCB). What should the project manager do now?",
 "opts":["Include the new branch of the bridge in the design","Evaluate the adjustments to the project management plan","Notify the project team about the change request's approval","Communicate the decision to the stakeholder who requested the change"],
 "c":["B"],
 "e":"After a change is approved, evaluate and make the needed adjustments to the project management plan (scope, schedule, cost, quality, risk) before implementing. Implementing the design change, or just notifying people, comes after the plan is updated to reflect the change."},

{"src":"PMP 2","n":151,"lang":"en",
 "q":"A project team has installed software for the client, passed the knowledge to operations, sent the final report, and celebrated. Then 1 week later, the client sends a list of requirements that have not been met. The client is requesting to fix the issues quickly and within the initial budget. What should the project manager have done to prevent this from happening?",
 "opts":["Reviewed the acceptance criteria and obtained client approval","Established meetings with the client throughout the project phases","Conducted a pilot to understand the client's performance parameters","Allocated a risk budget for any post-release improvements"],
 "c":["B"],
 "e":"Regular meetings with the client throughout the phases would have surfaced and validated requirements continuously, preventing unmet requirements from appearing after closure. The lack of client engagement during implementation was the root cause; a risk budget or pilot doesn't fix that."},

{"src":"PMP 2","n":152,"lang":"en",
 "q":"A project manager is leading an innovation project that is facing a lot of risks due to a high level of uncertainty. What strategy should the project manager use to manage these risks using an agile approach?",
 "opts":["Evaluate the risks constantly and reprioritize work as the project progresses","Assign an agile risk manager to better understand and track the project risks","Develop an agile risk matrix to track, assess, and evaluate the uncertainty of the project.","Implement a scrum risk retrospective to analyze the risks at the end of the project"],
 "c":["A"],
 "e":"Agile manages uncertainty by continuously assessing risks and re-prioritizing work as the project progresses (with stakeholders). A dedicated risk manager, a static matrix, or end-of-project risk analysis don't provide the continuous, adaptive risk handling agile requires."},

{"src":"PMP 2","n":153,"lang":"en",
 "q":"A project manager is in a cross-functional agile team. The project manager has been assigned a software project with multiple features that will take the team about a year to complete. The CEO is insisting that the solution needs to meet business goals for the year-end, which is due in 6 months. What should the project manager do to align stakeholders' needs and expectations?",
 "opts":["Modify the requirements to those that can be completed in 6 months","Include all stakeholders in daily scrum meetings to keep them informed","Work with the stakeholders to create a prioritized backlog and release a roadmap.","Implement fast-tracking and crashing activities to complete the project in 6 months"],
 "c":["C"],
 "e":"Work with stakeholders to prioritize the backlog and produce a release roadmap, giving transparency and delivering the highest-value features that meet business goals by year-end. Unilaterally modifying requirements, cramming stakeholders into standups, or crashing don't align expectations through value-based planning."},

{"src":"PMP 2","n":154,"lang":"en",
 "q":"A project manager is leading an automation project. The project team is colocated and includes people from both the technical and business units. The project manager discovers that personal differences and work styles are affecting the project negatively. How should the project manager address this?",
 "opts":["Develop a schedule that ensures people with personal differences work separately","Invite the functional managers of the different units to address their personal differences","Use an approach to the project that does not involve colocation","Meet with the team and agree on expectations from team members"],
 "c":["D"],
 "e":"Meet with the team to understand the issues and agree on shared expectations/ground rules that resolve the work-style clashes. Separating people, escalating to functional managers, or removing colocation avoid building the team agreements that fix the problem."},

{"src":"PMP 2","n":155,"lang":"en",
 "q":"An agile team is working on a 2-week sprint to deliver business value. About 1 week after the sprint starts, the team members do not feel confident about the product increment they are building. They do not want to wait until the end of the sprint and want to validate the product increment with the product owner now. What should the project manager do to mitigate this risk?",
 "opts":["Address the issue as part of the retrospective","Schedule an additional interim demo with the product owner","Ask the team to work overtime toward the end of the sprint to meet the original sprint end date","Extend the sprint end date in anticipation of feedback from the product owner that will require extra work"],
 "c":["B"],
 "e":"An interim demo lets the team validate the increment with the product owner mid-sprint and get early feedback, reducing the risk of building the wrong thing. Waiting for the retrospective, working overtime, or extending the sprint don't provide the early validation the team needs."},

{"src":"PMP 2","n":156,"lang":"en",
 "q":"A project manager has been transferred to another country to work on a key project. The project manager successfully completed the planning phase and has started the execution phase, reporting good progress. During the last meeting with the project sponsor, the project manager was told that some stakeholders have complained that the project manager's emails are very harsh. What should the project manager do to solve this situation?",
 "opts":["Explain to the sponsor that the communication model being used is from company headquarters","Change the communication mode from emails to meetings to reduce stakeholders' complaints","Determine the level of influence of the stakeholders who are complaining","Adjust the communications management plan to gain stakeholder trust"],
 "c":["D"],
 "e":"Adjust the communications management plan (tone, method, frequency, cultural fit) to rebuild stakeholder trust—communication should account for the audience and context. Defending the HQ model, blanket-switching to meetings, or gauging influence don't address the communication approach that's causing the complaints."},

{"src":"PMP 2","n":157,"lang":"en","q":"A project manager is assigned to a technical research project. The project team has already been assigned, and the main subject matter expert (SME) sends the project manager a list of the technical skills required for the execution of this project. What should the project manager do next?","opts":["Discuss the list with the project sponsor to confirm that the resources have the required skills","Meet with the project team to understand their skills and identify potential gaps and training requirements","Ask the functional manager to review the resource pool and recommend the right individuals to join the team","Send the list to the project team and ask them to get training on the required skills"],"c":["B"],"e":"Meet with the project team to understand their skills and identify potential gaps and training requirements. As PM it is important to have a clear understanding of the team members' skills and to identify any gaps that may affect successful execution. The PM can then take appropriate actions (training/resources) to ensure the team can deliver successfully."},
{"src":"PMP 2","n":158,"lang":"en","q":"A team has had issues with behavior on an ongoing project, which is causing conflicts. As a result, the team is losing momentum and falling behind in its sprints. How should the project manager handle this situation?","opts":["Control backlog assignments and reduce direct communications between team members","Engage a counselor to reconcile the situation between the team members affected","Require team members experiencing the conflicts to take a day off from work","Revisit the ground rules and team communication protocols, and discuss what is happening"],"c":["D"],"e":"Revisiting the ground rules and team communication protocols helps the team identify root causes of the conflicts and find ways to address them. The PM facilitates a discussion where members express concerns respectfully and constructively, so the team can find solutions and regain momentum."},
{"src":"PMP 2","n":159,"lang":"en","q":"An organization is about to start a multiphased project. Due to strict regulations, some of the phases must be completely planned in advance. However, other phases allow for more flexibility and experimentation on scope and schedule. The sponsor wants to pursue the project objectives as planned, but also wants to accommodate new information and changes as the project progresses. Which project management approach should the project manager select for this project?","opts":["Agile","Phased","Hybrid","Incremental"],"c":["C"],"e":"The stem mixes 'some phases must be fully planned in advance' (predictive) with 'other phases allow more flexibility and experimentation in scope and schedule' (agile). Combining both calls for a hybrid approach."},
{"src":"PMP 2","n":160,"lang":"en","q":"A project manager is assigned to a new IT project. The sales manager directs the project manager to proceed with the project kick-off without waiting for stakeholder approval. What should the project manager do next?","opts":["Contact the customer for confirmation and obtain the attendee list","Obtain the same directive in writing before proceeding","Confirm the approach with the project management office (PMO)","Check the project charter and verify the approach"],"c":["D"],"e":"The project charter is the document that authorizes the project, defines the main stakeholders, and determines how the project is started and its constraints. The PM should review this key document to verify the correct approach rather than acting on an informal directive."},
{"src":"PMP 2","n":161,"lang":"en","q":"In a newly formed project team, one experienced team member is overwhelmed by the complexity of the tasks and is not happy with the project team. What should the project manager do?","opts":["Set up team-building activities with the project team members to reduce tension","Ask the team member to enter this potential schedule delay in the risk register","Actively listen to the team member and identify ways to support their needs in completing the tasks","Explain that the tasks are unavoidable and ask the team member to find a solution to overcome the challenges"],"c":["C"],"e":"Active listening is a key skill of the professional project manager. The PM should actively listen to the team member and identify ways to support their needs in completing the tasks (servant leadership / removing impediments)."},
{"src":"PMP 2","n":162,"lang":"en","q":"A project manager is managing a project with team members who are working all over the globe. The project manager notices that each team member's deliverables do not meet the requirements. What should the project manager do first?","opts":["Meet with the project team to explain that noncompliant deliverables must be reworked and submitted immediately to avoid project delays","Assemble the project team via conference call to tell them that disciplinary action will be taken if any more noncompliant deliverables are submitted","Schedule time with the project sponsor to inform them that the project schedule is slipping due to the time it will take to rework the deliverables","Discuss the process used to create the noncompliant deliverables with the project team to see if there was a potential misunderstanding"],"c":["D"],"e":"When deliverables don't meet requirements, identify the root cause first. Discussing the process used to create them helps the PM spot misunderstandings and work with the team to close gaps in their understanding of the requirements — improving performance and preventing recurrence."},
{"src":"PMP 2","n":163,"lang":"en","q":"A project is ending its fifth out of six iterations. During the iteration review, a customer adds a new regulatory feature that exceeds the capacity of the remaining iteration. What should the project lead do?","opts":["Register an issue and escalate it to the project sponsor","Invite the stakeholders to discuss a new prioritization","Estimate the impact and consult with the product owner","Increase the team's capacity to finish the project on time"],"c":["C"],"e":"The project lead should first estimate the impact of the new regulatory feature on the remaining iteration (time, resources, cost), then consult with the product owner to discuss options such as extending the iteration, cutting other features, or deferring the feature to a later release. Collaborate with the PO to choose the course that aligns with project goals and priorities."},
{"src":"PMP 2","n":164,"lang":"en","q":"A project manager is assigned to a major construction project. The project is critical to the city's population, and especially to the local government official who committed to delivering the project on time as an election promise. A new project sponsor has been assigned and wants to include aspects that the previous project sponsor did not support. What should the project manager do next?","opts":["Inform the new project sponsor that the new aspects will not be included in this project","Meet with the new project sponsor to review the current project scope and the requested changes","Invite the project sponsor to a briefing with the project team to demonstrate progress and seek further support to meet project-committed deliverables","Rebaseline the project as there is now a new project sponsor, which is presenting an opportunity to review and recalibrate the project objectives"],"c":["B"],"e":"Meet with the new project sponsor to review the current scope and the requested changes. This ensures both the PM and the new sponsor are aligned on the scope and objectives before any change is evaluated through change control."},
{"src":"PMP 2","n":165,"lang":"en","q":"A project manager has noticed that a critical team member is consistently arguing with a client. This has started to have a negative effect on the meetings. How should the project manager address this?","opts":["Talk to the client privately to highlight the situation","Remove the project team member from all future meetings","Recommend a performance improvement plan to the project team member","Explain the impact of the scenario to the project team member"],"c":["D"],"e":"The PM should have a conversation with the critical team member, explaining the impact their behavior is having on the meetings and the overall project, and work with them to identify the underlying reasons for the arguing and explore solutions."},
{"src":"PMP 2","n":166,"lang":"en","q":"A project is in progress with a team that is new to agile approaches. After the first sprint review, the team performance begins slipping, and the conclusion is that there is not an adequate flow of work for the team. What should the project manager do?","opts":["Provide the product owner with training","Discuss the issue with the stakeholders","Change the project approach to hybrid","Submit a change request to rebaseline the project"],"c":["A"],"e":"Since the team has no flow of work (which stems from the product backlog), the product owner is not adequately trained to do backlog refinement per business value. Provide the PO with training. Discussing it with stakeholders won't be solution-oriented."},
{"src":"PMP 2","n":167,"lang":"en","q":"Companies A and B are codeveloping a cutting-edge technology product. Based on customer feedback, the marketing department urgently requests a new feature in 2 weeks. Company A's front end is ready, but Company B requires 4 weeks to deliver the complete back end and is busy with the current release. What should the project manager do to obtain agreement from both companies and acceptance from marketing?","opts":["Plan the delivery of a minimum viable feature in 2 weeks to enable early showcasing of business value","Create a special release 4 weeks after the current release to deliver the new feature in its entirety","Stop the current release work for 4 weeks in order to work and deliver the new feature completely","Add the new feature to the list for the next release and continue working on the current release"],"c":["A"],"e":"Planning a minimum viable feature helps customers organize their thinking about which capabilities are true must-haves and enables identification of a Minimum Viable Product (MVP), showcasing business value early."},
{"src":"PMP 2","n":168,"lang":"en","q":"A project manager is leading an engineering project. The project manager must obtain some certifications before mass production, which is scheduled in 3 months. The certification process will require at least 6 months. What should the project manager do next?","opts":["Assess and evaluate the certification process and understand the best and worst-case scenarios","Engage a consultant to shorten the certification process to meet the schedule requirement","Meet with senior management to discuss the possibility of an extension to the project timeline","Discuss extending the mass production timeline by 3 months with the project sponsor"],"c":["A"],"e":"Assess and evaluate the certification process and understand the best and worst-case scenarios. This lets the PM make a correct, informed decision in favor of both the project and obtaining the certificates before escalating or committing to a fixed change."},
{"src":"PMP 2","n":169,"lang":"en","q":"A team is finishing the third release to present to the stakeholder as part of a demonstration. The company that subcontracts the developers has communicated that there is a cash flow problem and the company will not be able to provide the same resources for the next iterations. What should the project manager do next?","opts":["Escalate the situation to the project sponsor for a possible resolution","Create a risk register to track the risks and request additional budget","Analyze the impact against the release plan if the blocker is not addressed","Work with the project owner to prioritize the product backlog"],"c":["C"],"e":"Analyze the impact against the release plan if the blocker is not addressed. Understanding the impact of the resource shortfall on the release plan must come before escalating or requesting budget."},
{"src":"PMP 2","n":170,"lang":"en","q":"A newly commissioned project has had trouble recruiting team members because the turnover rate is high. The human resource (HR) manager has conducted several exit interviews. Many team members have expressed that the project manager has inadequate relationship management competencies. The project manager explains that the exiting team members were lazy. What should the project manager do to prevent this high turnover rate from continuing?","opts":["Explain to the recruiting manager that a project is temporary in nature, making it different than an operational environment","Avoid micromanaging the team, enforce stricter rules, and monitor employees","Be more involved in the team member recruitment process to ensure qualified team members are chosen","Develop relationship skills as a leader and also develop the teams' emotional intelligence skills"],"c":["D"],"e":"Don't fall for being more involved in hiring — it doesn't address the root cause. The exit interviews point to the PM's relationship management. The answer is to develop relationship skills as a leader and develop emotional intelligence in the team."},
{"src":"PMP 2","n":171,"lang":"en","q":"A project manager is leading a fast-moving project with strict deadlines and minimal time to deliver each milestone. The project manager discovers that one of the remote team members, who puts a lot of effort into delivering the project, has confided in an on-site team member. The remote team member feels they are not being adequately rewarded and recognized for their job. What should the project manager do?","opts":["Send a copy of the engagement contract to the remote team member and indicate that the team member is entitled to what is included in the contract","Inform the human resource (HR) manager of this situation and ensure this news does not spread","Engage the remote team member, empathize with them, and let them know their contribution is appreciated regardless of their remote work status","Make arrangements with the resource's functional manager to relocate the team member to another existing project"],"c":["C"],"e":"The PM should have emotional intelligence and empathy for team members. Engage the remote team member, empathize, and let them know their contribution is appreciated regardless of their remote working status."},
{"src":"PMP 2","n":172,"lang":"en","q":"A project team created user stories after understanding the customer requirements. As the project progresses, the project manager observes that there are too many change requests. The team feels that the outcomes they are working on are not aligned with the customer requirements. What should the project manager do?","opts":["Arrange for a workshop with the customer to understand the business values expected from the outcomes","Include a representative from the customer to work collaboratively with the team","Refer to the configuration management knowledge base for similar projects delivered in the past","Acknowledge that the current team needs improvement and hire more qualified team members"],"c":["A"],"e":"In agile, there's no objection to the development team engaging the client directly. With many client changes and lack of understanding by the team, arrange a workshop with the customer to understand the business values expected from the outcomes."},
{"src":"PMP 2","n":173,"lang":"en","q":"A company is starting a critical project with a strict deadline. During the first planning meeting, the team realizes that the list of requirements has too many items to allow on-time delivery. What should the project manager do first?","opts":["Update the risk register and present a contingency plan to meet the deadline","Ask the team to propose a minimum viable product (MVP) to meet the date","Increase the capacity of the team to finish the project on time","Suggest to the customer to reduce the backlog to meet the date"],"c":["B"],"e":"When a project has too many requirements to meet the deadline, the PM's first step is to ask the team to propose an MVP. The MVP is the minimum set of features that delivers value to the customer and can be delivered within the timeframe; the team focuses on core functionality and leaves non-essential features for later releases."},
{"src":"PMP 2","n":174,"lang":"en","q":"In a financial company, a strategic project has been planned to use a predictive approach. During project planning, the product leader requests that milestones be submitted for review. There is a defined scope and an established deadline to conclude the project. What should the project manager do first to meet the product leader's expectations?","opts":["Inform the product leader that they must wait until the end of the project","Meet with the software team to review the possibility of monthly milestone reviews","Change the project charter to indicate that the approach will be different","Modify the project management plan to use an agile framework to incorporate milestones"],"c":["B"],"e":"The PM should fulfill the product lead's request by meeting with the team to review the possibility of conducting monthly milestone reviews — accommodating the request without abandoning the predictive approach."},
{"src":"PMP 2","n":175,"lang":"en","q":"A project manager encounters a governance issue during the project execution phase. One of the team members complains about receiving assignments from multiple people and states they are not able to work effectively. What should the project manager establish first in the project governance structure?","opts":["Clear project work breakdown structure (WBS) and timeline","Risk management and issues resolution plan","Clear responsible, accountable, consult, and inform (RACI) matrix","Resource management and conflict resolution plan"],"c":["C"],"e":"The PM should first establish a clear RACI matrix. It defines each team member's roles and responsibilities — who is responsible, accountable, consulted, and informed — preventing the confusion of receiving assignments from multiple people."},
{"src":"PMP 2","n":176,"lang":"en","q":"An agile project is running its third iteration with a duration of 2 weeks. A new operations director, who only has a predictive background, started working at the company. In the first meeting with the project manager, the director demands a weekly project status report. What should the project manager do?","opts":["Share this with the team and ask them to develop reports for the director","Explain to the director that agile projects have live reports","Introduce the agile aspect to the director and agree on a solution","Invite the director to attend the daily standup meetings"],"c":["C"],"e":"One of the PM's tasks in agile is to clarify and explain agile working methods to those who don't understand them. Agile favors live information radiators over status reports, so the PM should introduce the agile aspect to the director and agree on a solution together."},
{"src":"PMP 2","n":177,"lang":"en","q":"A company changed the product owner on an existing project. The new product owner has less knowledge of the product than everybody else on the team. Due to the product owner's lack of knowledge, the team does not consider the opinions of the product owner seriously and mostly relies on the project manager's opinion. What should the project manager do?","opts":["Assume the role of the product owner while the product owner gets familiar with the product","Organize a team-building activity and ask the team to respect the opinions of the new product owner","Schedule time to share insights with the new product owner about the product and project information","Work with the project management office (PMO) manager to generate an onboarding plan for the new product owner"],"c":["C"],"e":"When a new PO joins, bring them up to speed. The PM should schedule knowledge-transfer time to share insights about the product and project information, ensuring the new PO feels comfortable asking questions and can form their own opinions and earn the team's confidence."},
{"src":"PMP 2","n":178,"lang":"en","q":"A project manager agreed to a team member's request to have an alternative working schedule whereby they begin working at 12:00 pm each day. This arrangement has worked well for the project, but has recently been scrutinized by members from other project teams because the company's core working hours begin at 9:00 AM. What should the project manager have done to prevent this situation?","opts":["Analyzed the boundaries of the negotiations for agreement","Broken down the situation to identify the root cause","Managed and rectified the ground rule violations","Determined communication methods and channels"],"c":["A"],"e":"An organization may have basic core working hours as policy. Any requested exemption should be discussed/negotiated within boundaries with the relevant department or function. The PM should have analyzed the boundaries of the negotiation for agreement before granting the exception."},
{"src":"PMP 2","n":179,"lang":"en","q":"A project manager is managing a project to deploy new software to 200 end users. During the implementation, there is a missed process that caused some of the users to lose their computer data. What should the project manager do first?","opts":["Meet with the team members and request they conduct a review of the case by providing feedback as lessons learned","Invite a consultant to review the process in order not to repeat the same issue and share the results with the team","Meet with the team to discuss the root cause and encourage the team to take note of it and continue to focus on the deployment","Record this case until the final lessons learned session is complete to prevent impacting the team's morale during implementation"],"c":["C"],"e":"The PM should first gather the team to discuss the root cause and determine how to prevent recurrence, while keeping focus on the deployment. This lets the team learn from the mistake and improve the process for the remaining users, minimizing impact on timeline and morale."},
{"src":"PMP 2","n":180,"lang":"en","q":"During a sprint, one of the team members found an issue. According to the team member and the product owner, the issue has a higher level of importance than the other features of the sprint but is not a roadblock to completing it. What should the project lead ask the project team do?","opts":["Avoid the temptation to make any changes and address the issue in the next sprint","Follow the sprint plan by delivering the features established for the sprint and prioritize the issue in the backlog","Address this issue only as it has a higher priority than the remaining features of the same sprint","Submit the issue to the change control board (CCB) to evaluate and formally approve a plan to address it"],"c":["B"],"e":"The key phrase is that the issue has higher importance but is NOT a roadblock — so it won't affect the current iteration plan. The team should follow the sprint plan, deliver the committed features normally, and prioritize the issue in the backlog (for a future sprint)."},
{"src":"PMP 3","n":1,"lang":"ar","q":"يقوم مدير مشروع بإدارة مشروع هجين، وبعد حضور مراجعة متكررة، يخبر أحد المعنيين مدير المشروع بأنه مشغول للغاية ويسأل عن سبب كثرة إجراء هذه المراجعات. كيف ينبغي لمدير المشروع الاستجابة؟","opts":["مناقشة منافع المشروع الهجين مع المعني، وكيف أن المراجعات الكثيرة تؤدي إلى قيمة أكبر والتقليل من إعادة العمل","التفاوض مع المعني بشأن تخفيض معدل حضور المراجعات وتحديث خطة مشاركة المعنيين لتعكس هذا الانخفاض في معدل الحضور","الشرح للمعني أن خطة مشاركة المعنيين تتطلب حضوره في كل المراجعات","تسجيل الإشكالية في سجل الإشكالات وتصعيد الخلاف إلى راعي المشروع للمساعدة"],"c":["A"],"e":"الإجابة الصحيحة (أ): ناقش مع المعني فوائد المشروع الهجين وكيف أن إجراء مراجعات متكررة يؤدي إلى تحقيق قيمة أكبر وتقليل الحاجة إلى إعادة العمل. يجب على مدير المشروع الاستجابة عن طريق الدخول في حوار مع المعني لمعالجة مخاوفه وشرح فوائد إجراء مراجعات متكررة للتقدم في مشروع هجين. هذا النهج التعاوني يعزز نجاح المشروع ويضمن أن المعني مطلع بشكل جيد على تقدم المشروع. (Discuss the value of frequent hybrid reviews — greater delivered value and less rework — rather than reducing attendance or escalating.)"},
{"src":"PMP 3","n":2,"lang":"ar","q":"يستمر مالك المنتج، منذ بداية المشروع، في السؤال خلال الاجتماعات حول الموازنة التي تُنفق لكل تكرار للمنتج. ويبدو أن مالك المنتج مهتم أكثر بالتكلفة بدلاً من المنتج نفسه. ماذا كان ينبغي على مدير المشروع فعله سابقاً لتغيير سلوك مالك المنتج؟","opts":["العمل على أساليب تقدير مختلفة لبث الثقة بالتكلفة التي تُنفق في كل إضافة للمنتج","العمل على خطة إدارة تقارير الموازنة التي تُنفق في كل تكرار مقابل التخطيط لتجنب هذه المناقشات أثناء الاجتماعات","العمل مع مالك المنتج لتوضيح دوره في مشروع الأسلوب الرشيق ونطاق اجتماعات الأسلوب الرشيق","العمل على عقد السعر الثابت لتحويل اهتمام مالك المنتج إلى القيمة بدلاً من المال"],"c":["C"],"e":"الإجابة الصحيحة (ج): العمل مع مالك المنتج لتوضيح دوره في المشروع الرشيق ونطاق اجتماعات الأسلوب الرشيق. سلوك مالك المنتج الذي يركز بشكل مفرط على التكلفة بدلاً من المنتج نفسه يجب معالجته بأن يعمل مدير المشروع مبكراً مع مالك المنتج لتوضيح دوره ونطاق الاجتماعات الرشيقة، فمالك المنتج يجب أن يركز على قيمة المنتج وأهدافه (توجيه فريق التطوير، تحديد أولويات الباكلوج، اتخاذ قرارات بشأن الميزات) لا على المال. (The PO should have been coached on his agile role and the scope/purpose of agile ceremonies so he focuses on product value, not cost.)"},
{"src":"Important","n":176,"lang":"ar","q":"في مراجعة التكرار لمشروع أجايل، يتلقى الفريق تعليقات من أصحاب المصلحة يمكن أن تؤدي إلى تغيير في المنتج. ما الإجراءان اللذان يجب على مدير المشروع اتخاذهما؟ (اختر اثنين)","opts":["يطلب من الراعي الموافقة على طلب التغيير","يسأل أصحاب المصلحة عن أولوية هذا التغيير","يطلب من أعضاء الفريق تحليل تأثير هذا التغيير","يطلب من أعضاء الفريق تضمين التغيير في التكرار التالي","يطلب من مالك المنتج مراجعة سجل أعمال المنتج"],"c":["B","E"],"e":"في الأجايل، التغييرات تُدار عبر مالك المنتج وتراكم المنتج (backlog) لا عبر التحكم الرسمي في التغيير. يسأل الفريق أصحاب المصلحة عن أولوية التغيير، ويطلب من مالك المنتج مراجعة سجل أعمال المنتج لإعادة ترتيب الأولويات. (Agile changes flow through the Product Owner/backlog: ask stakeholders for the change's priority, and have the PO re-prioritize the product backlog — not formal change control or sponsor sign-off.)"},
{"src":"Important","n":73,"lang":"ar","q":"أنت مدير مشروع تدير مشروع بناء. يتأثر جدول المشروع سلبًا بسبب أداء أحد أعضاء الفريق. إذا اقترب موعد تسليم المشروع، فماذا يجب أن تفعل كمدير للمشروع؟","opts":["قم بإزالة عضو الفريق من المشروع","قم بجدولة جلسات تدريبية لعضو الفريق لتحسين أدائه","اكتساب موارد أكثر مهارة","إرشاد عضو الفريق"],"c":["D"],"e":"القيادة الخادمة: أرشد (mentor/coach) عضو الفريق ضعيف الأداء بدلاً من إزالته أو معاقبته. التدريب الرسمي قد يستغرق وقتاً لا يسمح به اقتراب الموعد، والإرشاد المباشر هو الأسرع والأكثر دعماً. (Servant leadership — coach/mentor the underperformer directly rather than remove, punish, or schedule lengthy training near the deadline.)"},
{"src":"Important","n":143,"lang":"ar","q":"أنت مدير المشروع لتنفيذ مشروع الفواتير الإلكترونية الذي اكتمل للتو. أبلغ المدير المالي أن الفريق اكتشف ثلاث فواتير بها أخطاء. ما الوثيقتان اللتان يجب على مدير المشروع تحديثهما في أسرع وقت ممكن؟ (اختر اثنين)","opts":["سجل التغيير","سجل المخاطر","سجل أعمال المنتج","سجل الإشكالات","سجل المعنيين"],"c":["B","D"],"e":"الأخطاء المكتشفة هي مشكلات وقعت بالفعل → تُسجَّل في سجل الإشكالات (issue log)، واحتمال تكرارها أو تأثيرها على إغلاق المشروع يُسجَّل في سجل المخاطر (risk register) لمتابعته. (Discovered errors are issues that already occurred → issue log; the risk of further/recurring errors → risk register.)"},
{"src":"Important","n":159,"lang":"ar","q":"أثناء مرحلة التنفيذ، لاحظ مدير المشروع أن أحد أعضاء الفريق قليل الخبرة ويكافح لإكمال العمل المحدد ويتطلب بعض التوجيه الإضافي للعودة إلى المسار الصحيح. كمدير مشروع، ماذا يجب أن تفعل؟","opts":["الترتيب لمتابعته من أحد أعضاء الفريق الأكثر خبرة","قم بإزالة عضو الفريق من المشروع","اطلب من عضو الفريق العمل لوقت إضافي للوفاء بالموعد النهائي","أرسل عضو الفريق إلى تدريب إضافي"],"c":["A"],"e":"دعم العضو قليل الخبرة عبر الإرشاد/المتابعة من زميل أكثر خبرة (mentoring) هو الحل الخادم والأسرع، بدلاً من إزالته أو إرهاقه بساعات إضافية أو تأخيره بتدريب رسمي. (Pair/mentor the inexperienced member with a more senior teammate — servant leadership, faster than formal training, kinder than removal or overtime.)"},
{"src":"Important","n":160,"lang":"ar","q":"أحد أعضاء الفريق ليس نشطًا مثل باقي أعضاء الفريق أثناء جلسة العصف الذهني. يجري مدير المشروع محادثة منفصلة مع عضو الفريق ويتعلم أن عضو الفريق يعاني من ضعف سمعي طفيف. يقدّر عضو الفريق اهتمام مدير المشروع. يقرر مدير المشروع بعد ذلك تغيير الوقت والغرفة للاجتماع لضمان مشاركة الجميع. ما هي المهارة الشخصية التي استخدمها مدير المشروع؟","opts":["التشبيك","الذكاء العاطفي","التأثير","إدارة الخلافات"],"c":["B"],"e":"إدراك مشاعر واحتياجات الفرد والتصرف بتعاطف لتمكين مشاركته = الذكاء العاطفي (Emotional Intelligence). (Reading the member's needs and adapting empathetically to enable participation is emotional intelligence.)"},
{"src":"Important","n":161,"lang":"ar","q":"تم تعيين مدير مشروع جديد لمشروع بيئي. بعد مراجعة خطة المشروع، يلاحظ مدير المشروع أن متطلبات الصحة والسلامة لم تتم معالجتها بشكل صحيح. هذا مصدر قلق خطير، ويريد مدير المشروع إصلاح هذه المشكلة على الفور. لماذا هذا الوضع إشكالية؟","opts":["من الضروري استيفاء مقاييس الأداء المحددة","يجب أن يتبع مدير المشروع أصول العملية التنظيمية لصاحب العمل","مدير المشروع ملزم بالامتثال للمتطلبات التنظيمية لموقع المشروع","يمكن أن تؤدي حوادث الإصابات أو الخسائر إلى تأخير إنجازات المشروع"],"c":["C"],"e":"الإجابة الصحيحة (ج): الصحة والسلامة مسألة امتثال تنظيمي/قانوني إلزامي لموقع المشروع (Business Environment domain) — وليست مجرد خطر على الجدول الزمني. مدير المشروع ملزم بالامتثال للمتطلبات التنظيمية. (Health & safety is a mandatory regulatory/compliance obligation, not merely a schedule risk.)"},
{"src":"Important","n":162,"lang":"ar","q":"أنت مدير لمشروع مهم. مؤشر أداء الجدول الزمني (SPI) هو 1.8 ومؤشر أداء التكلفة (CPI) هو 0.1. أخبر راعي المشروع مديرَ المشروع أنه إذا استمر هذا الاتجاه، فسيضطر إلى إغلاق المشروع قبل الأوان. ماذا يجب أن يفعل مدير المشروع؟","opts":["مراقبة راعي المشروع لكن يجب فهم أنه من الطبيعي إنفاق المزيد من الأموال في مرحلة تصميم المشروع","استخدم تقنية تسوية الموارد","التوقف عن ضغط المشروع","إدارة راعي المشروع عن كثب ومراجعة خطة إدارة موارد المشروع لتحسين المؤشرات"],"c":["C"],"e":"الإجابة الصحيحة (ج): SPI=1.8 (متقدم جداً على الجدول) بينما CPI=0.1 (تجاوز هائل للتكلفة). المشروع ينفق بإفراط لتسريع جدول هو أصلاً متقدم — لذا يجب التوقف عن ضغط/تسريع الجدول (crashing) لوقف نزيف التكلفة. (SPI 1.8 = well ahead of schedule, CPI 0.1 = massively over budget; stop crashing/compressing the schedule since speeding up is what's burning the budget.)"},
{"src":"Important","n":163,"lang":"ar","q":"تعمل المنظمة بمنهجية أجايل في مشاريعها. خلال مناقشة غير رسمية، علم مدير المشروع أن أعضاء الفريق الذين يعملون حاليًا في فرق مختلفة يناقشون كيف يرغبون في أن يكونوا معًا في نفس الفريق. كمدير مشروع، ماذا يجب أن تفعل؟","opts":["يجب ألا يسمح مدير المشروع للموارد باختيار الفرق لأن مدير المشروع يعيّن الموارد بناءً على احتياجات المشروع","يجب أن يشير مدير المشروع إلى أن أعضاء الفريق قد تم تعيينهم بالفعل إلى فريق من خلال تحسين القوى العاملة","يجب أن يسمح مدير المشروع للموارد باختيار الفريق الذي يرغبون في العمل به بشكل دوري","يجب ألا يسمح مدير المشروع للموارد باختيار الفريق الذي يرغبون في العمل فيه لأنهم سيختارون دائمًا فريقهم المفضل"],"c":["C"],"e":"الإجابة الصحيحة (ج): الفرق الرشيقة ذاتية التنظيم (self-organizing)؛ السماح للأعضاء باختيار فرقهم يعزز الالتزام والتحفيز. (Agile teams self-organize — letting members choose their teams boosts ownership and motivation.)"},
{"src":"Important","n":164,"lang":"ar","q":"طُلب من مدير المشروع تقديم مؤشر إلى الوقت المتبقي في مشروع أجايل. ما هي الأدوات التي يمكن استخدامها لتوفير هذه المعلومات؟","opts":["مخطط باريتو","تحليل وثيقة متطلبات العمل","إصدار مخطط الاحتراق (Burndown chart)","نقاط القصة لقصص المستخدم المتبقية"],"c":["C"],"e":"مخطط الاحتراق (Burndown chart) هو الإشعاع المعلوماتي (information radiator) الذي يُظهر العمل/الوقت المتبقي في التكرار أو الإصدار. (A burndown chart visualizes remaining work/time — the classic agile information radiator.)"},
{"src":"Important","n":165,"lang":"ar","q":"يفضّل أصحاب المصلحة في بعض الصناعات التعامل مع بعض المبرمجين ومحللي النظام بشكل مباشر، متجاوزين مديري المشاريع. هذا يعقّد إدارة الموارد في المشاريع. كيف ينبغي لمدير المشروع حل هذه المشكلة؟","opts":["قم بالترويج لاعتماد خطة إدارة الاتصالات مع فريق المشروع","استضف حدثًا لبناء الفريق لتطوير تماسك الفريق وتحسين التواصل","اقتراح تنفيذ نظام معلومات إدارة مشروع جديد (PMIS)","طلب لقاء مع الإدارة العليا لتصعيد الموقف"],"c":["A"],"e":"تجاوز قنوات الاتصال يُعالَج باعتماد خطة إدارة الاتصالات (communications management plan) التي تحدد القنوات والأدوار الرسمية، لا ببناء الفريق أو التصعيد. (Bypassed channels are fixed by adopting a communications management plan that defines proper channels — not team-building or escalation.)"},
{"src":"Important","n":166,"lang":"ar","q":"يدعو مدير المشروع الرئيسَ التنفيذي للشركة والراعيَ وأعضاءَ الفريق وممثلَ العميل الخارجي إلى اجتماع لمراجعة خطة إدارة المشروع. يبدأ مدير المشروع في عرض الميزانية لكن الرئيس التنفيذي يطلب فجأة إنهاء الاجتماع. ما الذي كان يجب على مدير المشروع فعله لتجنب هذا الموقف؟","opts":["مراجعة خطة إدارة الاتصالات قبل دعوة ممثل العميل الخارجي إلى الاجتماع","أرسل شرائح العرض التقديمي إلى المشاركين الداخليين أولاً ثم إلى ممثل العميل الخارجي قبل الاجتماع","مراجعة خطة إدارة التكلفة مع المشاركين الداخليين والمدير التنفيذي قبل دعوة ممثل العميل الخارجي","أجرِ تحليل السبب الجذري مع ممثل العميل الخارجي ووثّق النتائج في سجل الدروس المستفادة"],"c":["A"],"e":"عرض معلومات مالية حساسة أمام طرف خارجي كان يجب تجنّبه بمراجعة خطة إدارة الاتصالات (من يتلقى أي معلومات) قبل توجيه الدعوات. (Sensitive budget data shown to an external party — the PM should have checked the communications management plan, which governs who receives what, before inviting the external client rep.)"},
{"src":"Important","n":167,"lang":"ar","q":"أثناء تنفيذ المشروع، يراقب صاحب المخاطر معدل السرعة الحرج لمحرك الطاقة لإطلاق استراتيجية الاستجابة للمخاطر. تم تحديد أن المحرك له الخصائص: 1600 دورة/دقيقة → توقف عن العمل وتعطّل النظام؛ 1500 دورة/دقيقة → تحذير من الحد الأقصى المسموح؛ 1400 دورة/دقيقة → يقلّل من قوة المحرك. أي من العبارات التالية صحيحة؟","opts":["الـ1600 دورة/دقيقة مشكلة مشروع (issue)، و1500 دورة/دقيقة حدث يُطلق استراتيجية استجابة (trigger)، و1400 دورة/دقيقة من مخاطر المشروع","الـ1600 من مخاطر المشروع، و1500 يطلق استراتيجية استجابة، و1400 مشكلة مشروع","الـ1600 يطلق استراتيجية استجابة، و1500 مشكلة مشروع، و1400 من مخاطر المشروع","الـ1600 مشكلة مشروع، و1500 من مخاطر المشروع، و1400 يطلق استراتيجية استجابة"],"c":["A"],"e":"1600 (تعطّل النظام فعلياً) = مشكلة/قضية وقعت (issue). 1500 (تحذير الحد الأقصى) = نقطة الإطلاق (risk trigger) التي تُفعّل خطة الاستجابة. 1400 (انخفاض القوة المحتمل) = خطر (risk) لم يقع بعد. (1600 = issue that occurred, 1500 = the trigger that launches the response, 1400 = a not-yet-occurred risk.)"},
{"src":"Important","n":168,"lang":"ar","q":"مدير مشروع كبير متعدد السنوات لديه أصحاب مصلحة متنوعون من مناطق جغرافية مختلفة. واجه مدير المشروع مؤخرًا مشكلة تتعلق بإشراك أصحاب المصلحة. ما الأداتان اللتان يجب أن يستخدمهما لتحديد السبب الكامن وراء عدم تحقيق المشاركة للتأثير المخطط؟ (اختر اثنين)","opts":["مخطط إيشيكاوا (Ishikawa / السبب والأثر)","خطة إدارة الاتصالات","الخمسة لماذا (5 Whys)","اجتماع في منطقة مفتوحة","سجل الإشكالات"],"c":["A","C"],"e":"كلا مخطط إيشيكاوا (عظم السمكة / السبب والأثر) و«الخمسة لماذا» هما أداتا تحليل السبب الجذري (root cause analysis) لتحديد سبب ضعف المشاركة. (Both Ishikawa/fishbone and the 5 Whys are root-cause-analysis tools.)"},
{"src":"Important","n":169,"lang":"ar","q":"أصدرت الحكومة مؤخرًا معايير جديدة قد تؤثر بشكل كبير على المشروع. يجب تغيير المخرجات لتتماشى مع معايير الجودة الجديدة. ماذا يجب أن يفعل مدير المشروع؟","opts":["قم بتقييم تأثير اللوائح الجديدة على المشروع وتقديم طلب التغيير","جدولة تدريب فريق المشروع على اللوائح الجديدة","تحديث سجل الدروس المستفادة وإبلاغ الإدارة العليا بالمعايير الجديدة","قم بإعداد عملية منظمة ومستقلة لتحديد ما إذا كانت أنشطة المشروع تتبع المعايير"],"c":["A"],"e":"عند ظهور متطلب امتثال جديد: قيّم تأثيره على المشروع ثم قدّم طلب تغيير رسمي عبر التحكم في التغيير. (New compliance requirement → assess impact and raise a change request through change control.)"},
{"src":"Important","n":170,"lang":"ar","q":"منظمة تنتقل من تنبؤي إلى رشيق. بناءً على المتطلبات، يقدّر فريق المشروع الميزانية ويقرر استخدام عقد بسعر ثابت مع البائع. ومع ذلك، أثناء التنفيذ تتطور المتطلبات وتتغير الأولويات باستمرار، مما يؤدي إلى خفض التقدير عند الانتهاء (EAC) بنسبة 50%. ماذا يجب أن يفعل مدير المشروع بعد ذلك؟","opts":["اجتمع مع أصحاب المصلحة للتوصية باستخدام عقد الوقت والمواد (T&M) لمعالجة المشكلة","أرسل تقرير حالة مفصلًا يسلّط الضوء على مشكلة التمويل لجميع أصحاب المصلحة","انتظر حتى الاجتماع التالي لمراجعة البوابة لتسليط الضوء على مخاطر انخفاض الأموال","تقديم طلب تغيير إلى المدير المالي لزيادة التمويل والمتابعة كما هو"],"c":["A"],"e":"المتطلبات المتطورة باستمرار لا تناسب عقد السعر الثابت؛ في السياق الرشيق يُوصى بعقد الوقت والمواد (T&M) المرن. (Continuously evolving requirements don't fit a fixed-price contract; an agile context calls for a flexible Time & Materials contract.)"},
{"src":"Important","n":171,"lang":"ar","q":"في مشروع رشيق، يجد مدير المشروع أن أعضاء الفريق يشاركون في عدة مشاريع مختلفة بسبب قيود الميزانية. كمدير مشروع، ماذا يجب أن تفعل؟","opts":["اطلب من أعضاء الفريق أن يتوزعوا بالتساوي عبر المشاريع حتى لا تحدث أخطاء ضمن الميزانية الحالية","قدّم خطة حوافز حتى يعمل أعضاء الفريق ساعات إضافية أكثر في مشاريع متعددة","اطلب من راعي المشروع تحديد الأولويات وإذا لزم الأمر تخصيص ميزانية إضافية حتى يتمكن الفريق من العمل في مشروع واحد","تبادل الأفكار مع أعضاء الفريق حول كيفية تنفيذ مشاريع متعددة ضمن الميزانية المحددة"],"c":["C"],"e":"الفرق الرشيقة يجب أن تكون مكرّسة (dedicated) لمشروع واحد؛ تعدد المهام يقتل الإنتاجية. اطلب من الراعي تحديد الأولويات وتخصيص الميزانية اللازمة لتفريغ الفريق لمشروع واحد. (Agile teams should be dedicated; multitasking kills throughput. Ask the sponsor to prioritize and fund a single-project focus.)"},
{"src":"Important","n":172,"lang":"ar","q":"ما الإجراءان اللذان يجب على مدير المشروع مراعاتهما أثناء إطلاق فريق افتراضي (virtual) جديد؟ (اختر اثنين)","opts":["قم بتقييم نقاط القوة والضعف لدى مدير المشروع كقائد افتراضي للفريق لتحديد المزالق التي يمكن تجنبها","قم بتجنيد أفضل الموارد داخل المنظمة لتكون جزءًا من الفريق الافتراضي","تحديد أنواع وكميات الموارد المطلوبة لكل حزمة عمل أو نشاط","حدد في البداية كيف سيتم رصد التقدم وأفضل الوسائل لإبلاغ التقدم","تحقق مما إذا كان أعضاء الفريق في أكثر من فريق ولا يمكن تخصيص الوقت الكافي لهذا الفريق"],"c":["D","E"],"e":"عند إطلاق فريق افتراضي مشتت جغرافياً، الأولوية للتواصل والتوافر: حدّد مبكراً كيف سيُرصَد التقدم ويُبلَّغ عنه، وتحقق من ألا يكون الأعضاء موزعين على فرق متعددة بحيث لا يتوفر لهم وقت كافٍ. (For a virtual team, the two priorities are establishing how progress is monitored/communicated and confirming members aren't overcommitted across teams.)"},
{"src":"Important","n":173,"lang":"ar","q":"يحتاج أصحاب المصلحة في المشروع الرشيق إلى توضيح مستمر لقيمة أعمال المشروع. كيف يجب على مدير المشروع المضي قدمًا؟","opts":["قم بتضمين العروض التوضيحية للجزء المبني من المنتج في نهاية كل سباق (sprint)","إعداد تقرير المشروع لأصحاب المصلحة وتوزيعه أسبوعيًا","قم بتأكيد الحد الأدنى من المنتج القابل للتطبيق (MVP) واعرضه على الشركة عندما يكون جاهزًا","إعداد تقارير قيمة الأعمال بشكل مستمر"],"c":["A"],"e":"في الأجايل، أفضل طريقة لإظهار القيمة باستمرار هي عرض المنتج العامل (demo) في نهاية كل سباق — العمل البرمجي العامل هو المقياس الأساسي للتقدم، لا التقارير. (Agile shows value via working-software demos at each sprint review — working product, not reports, is the primary measure of progress.)"},
{"src":"Important","n":174,"lang":"ar","q":"أثناء تنفيذ المشروع، يحتاج أحد أعضاء فريق المشروع الرئيسيين إلى أخذ إجازة غير متوقعة. المشروع متقدم على الجدول الزمني وتحت الميزانية. ما الإجراءان اللذان يجب على مدير المشروع اتخاذهما في البداية لحل هذه المشكلة؟ (اختر اثنين)","opts":["إعادة تعيين المسؤوليات عبر الأعضاء المتبقين باستخدام العوامة المتاحة للتدريب","تحقق من مصفوفة تخصيص المسؤولية (RACI / RAM) لتحديد الأنشطة التي ستتأثر","تحقق مما إذا كانت أنشطة أعضاء الفريق موثقة بشكل كافٍ لتسهيل التسليم","تحقق من سجل المخاطر للحصول على استجابة جاهزة لمورد غير متوفر فجأة","الحصول على دعم راعي المشروع ليحل محل عضو الفريق في الإجازة"],"c":["B","D"],"e":"لفهم ومعالجة غياب مورد رئيسي: راجع مصفوفة المسؤولية (RACI) لتحديد الأنشطة المتأثرة، وراجع سجل المخاطر لأنه قد يحتوي على استجابة مخططة مسبقاً لخطر عدم توفر مورد. (Check the RACI to see which activities are hit, and the risk register for a pre-planned response to resource unavailability.)"},
{"src":"Important","n":175,"lang":"ar","q":"يشارك أحد أعضاء الفريق مع مديره الوظيفي بعض التفاصيل السلبية حول أداء عضو آخر في الفريق والتي نوقشت خلال الاستعراض بأثر رجعي (retrospective). يكتشف مدير المشروع هذه المناقشة. ما الإجراءان اللذان يجب على مدير المشروع اتخاذهما؟ (اختر اثنين)","opts":["تواصل مع عضو الفريق لشرح ماهية المعلومات التي يمكن مشاركتها خارج الفريق","اتصل بالمدير الوظيفي لعضو الفريق وادعُه إلى الاستعراض بأثر رجعي التالي","قابِل الفريق بأكمله لمراجعة القواعد الأساسية حول البيئات الآمنة","قابل صاحب المنتج للتحدث عن الموقف","لقاء مع الراعي لمراجعة الوضع"],"c":["A","C"],"e":"الاستعراض بأثر رجعي بيئة آمنة (safe space) سرّية. عالج التسريب بالتواصل مع العضو لتوضيح ما يمكن مشاركته خارجياً، ثم راجع القواعد الأساسية حول الأمان مع الفريق كله — دون تصعيد للراعي. (A retrospective is a confidential safe space; coach the member on what may be shared, and revisit the safety ground rules with the whole team — no escalation needed.)"},
{"src":"Important","n":176,"lang":"ar","q":"أنت مدير لمشروع مهم. بسبب مشكلة فنية مع المورد الفرعي، هناك تأخير محتمل في التسليم. للبقاء في الموعد المحدد، اقترح البائع استبدال مكوّن المورد الفرعي بجزء مشابه يختلف عن الجزء المنصوص عليه في مواصفات المشروع. بصفتك مدير مشروع، ماذا يجب أن تفعل بعد ذلك؟ (سؤال آخر برقم 176)","opts":["قبول الاستبدال المقترح من أجل الحفاظ على أنشطة الشراء في المسار الصحيح","قم بإرشاد البائع لاستخدام الجزء المتوافق مع مواصفات المشروع وتحديث الجدول الزمني","الإصرار على استخدام المكوّن حسب مواصفات المشروع والشحن الجوي للمعدات لتخفيف التأخير","استشر خبيرًا في الموضوع (SME) لمعرفة ما إذا كان التغيير المقترح مقبولاً"],"c":["D"],"e":"قبل قبول أو رفض بديل يخالف المواصفات، افهم أولاً: استشر خبير الموضوع (SME) لتقييم ما إذا كان البديل مقبولاً فنياً، ثم يُدار أي تغيير عبر التحكم في التغيير. (Before accepting/rejecting an off-spec substitute, consult an SME to judge technical acceptability — then route any change through change control.)"},
{"src":"Important","n":178,"lang":"ar","q":"اثنان من أعضاء الفريق يتجادلان مع بعضهما البعض بشكل مفتوح في الاجتماعات. ولدفع أفكارهما، تحدّث كل عضو في الفريق بشكل فردي مع الراعي مباشرة واتخذ قرارات دون إبلاغ مدير المشروع. ما الإجراءان اللذان يجب على مدير المشروع اتخاذهما؟ (اختر اثنين)","opts":["كرّر القواعد الأساسية في الاجتماع التالي وتأكد من أن جميع أعضاء الفريق على دراية بهذه القواعد","ناقش هذا الموقف مع مديري المشاريع الآخرين واطلب النصيحة","مناقشة السلوك مباشرة مع مديريهم واطلب منهم تصحيح السلوك","اطلب من أعضاء الفريق حلّها بأنفسهم","عالج المشكلة السلوكية مباشرة مع كل عضو في الفريق"],"c":["A","E"],"e":"تجاوز قنوات الاتصال والقرارات الفردية يُعالَج بإعادة تأكيد القواعد الأساسية (ground rules) للفريق كله، والتحدث المباشر مع كل عضو لمعالجة السلوك — لا بالتصعيد إلى مديريهم. (ملاحظة: صندوق الإجابة في المصدر كان مقتطعاً جزئياً؛ الخيار «أ» مؤكد، والثاني مُستنتَج وفق منطق PMI — استخدم زر «حلّل مع Claude» للمقارنة.) (Reinforce ground rules with the whole team + address the behavior directly with each member; no escalation to their managers. Source answer box was partly cut — option A confirmed, second derived.)"},
{"src":"Important","n":180,"lang":"ar","q":"إذا أراد مدير المشروع التأكد من أن العمل المنجَز والمنتج الذي يتم إنشاؤه لا ينحرف عن قيمة العمل المحددة، فما الذي يجب أن يوليه مدير المشروع اهتمامًا خاصًا لتحقيق هذا الهدف؟","opts":["مراقبة وتحديث خطة تحقيق الفوائد (benefits realization plan)","مراقبة هيكل تجزئة العمل (WBS)","إجراء عمليات تدقيق الجودة وتحليل الفشل","مراجعة وتحديث خطة إدارة المتطلبات"],"c":["A"],"e":"لضمان عدم انحراف العمل عن قيمة الأعمال المحددة، يراقب مدير المشروع خطة تحقيق الفوائد (benefits realization plan) ويحدّثها — فهي التي تربط مخرجات المشروع بالقيمة/المنافع المستهدفة. (The benefits realization plan ties deliverables to the intended business value, so monitoring it keeps work aligned with defined value.)"},
{"src":"Important","n":19,"lang":"ar","q":"من المقرر أن يلتقي مدير المشروع بعميل في الاجتماع الأسبوعي لإدارة المخاطر. لاحظ مدير المشروع أن إحدى المهام التي لها اعتماديات خارجية تأخرت لمدة أسبوع. يدرك مدير المشروع أن هذا الأمر سيُحدث تأثيرًا ولكنه ليس على دراية بالتفاصيل الدقيقة. ما الأمران اللذان ينبغي لمدير المشروع القيام بهما؟ (حدد خيارين)","opts":["تحديث سجل المخاطر وطرح هذا الأمر في الاجتماع مع العميل","الانتظار إلى أن يُعرب العميل عن مخاوفه وجمع المزيد من المعلومات","جمع معلومات إضافية وتقدير التأثير","مراجعة الدروس المستفادة من المشاريع المماثلة السابقة","مناقشة المشكلة مع الجهة ذات السلطة على المورد الخارجي"],"c":["A","C"],"e":"خطر التأخير الخارجي يُدار بشكل استباقي وشفاف: حدّث سجل المخاطر واطرح الأمر مع العميل، واجمع معلومات إضافية لتقدير التأثير الحقيقي قبل التصرف — لا الانتظار السلبي. (Proactively update the risk register and raise it with the client, and gather more info to estimate the impact — not passively wait.)"},
{"src":"Important","n":20,"lang":"ar","q":"يهدف مشروع كبير لتصنيع وسائل النقل العام إلى تقليل الانبعاثات بشكل كبير. وقد تأخر المشروع بشكل كبير بسبب عدم الامتثال للقوانين واللوائح البيئية. ما الذي كان ينبغي لمدير المشروع فعله لمنع هذا الوضع؟","opts":["دمج احتياطيات الجدول الزمني الكافية بناءً على تأخيرات مماثلة في المشاريع السابقة","تعيين أحد كبار مسؤولي الامتثال البيئي ليقدم تقاريره مباشرة إلى مدير المشروع","الاتصال بالجهة المنظِّمة للحصول على تنازل جزئي في ضوء الأهمية البيئية للمشروع","تحديد متطلبات الامتثال البيئي والمخاطر التي تحول دون تحقيقها وإعداد إجراءات التخفيف"],"c":["D"],"e":"الوقاية الاستباقية: تحديد متطلبات الامتثال البيئي مبكراً، وتحديد المخاطر التي قد تعيق تحقيقها، وإعداد إجراءات التخفيف — بدلاً من مجرد إضافة احتياطي زمني أو طلب تنازل. (Proactively identify the compliance requirements and the risks to meeting them, and plan mitigations — Business Environment domain.)"},
{"src":"Important","n":23,"lang":"ar","q":"يقوم مدير مشروع بإدارة أحد المشاريع المعقّدة من الناحية الفنية. يعد المشروع جزءًا من برنامج عمل استراتيجي وأول مشروع يستخدم الأسلوب الرشيق. يهتم أحد أعضاء مجلس الإدارة بحضور اجتماع لمعرفة مدى تقدم تسليم نطاق المشروع. ما الذي ينبغي لقائد المشروع فعله؟","opts":["دعوة عضو مجلس الإدارة إلى حضور الاجتماع اليومي (daily standup)","دعوة عضو مجلس الإدارة إلى جلسة مراجعة الإطلاق (review) التالية","دعوة عضو مجلس الإدارة إلى الجلسة التالية لتقييم الإطلاق بأثر رجعي (retrospective)","دعوة عضو مجلس الإدارة إلى عرض توضيحي منفصل"],"c":["B"],"e":"لإطلاع صاحب مصلحة على تقدّم النطاق، الجلسة المناسبة هي مراجعة الإصدار/التكرار (Sprint/Release Review) حيث يُعرض المنتج العامل لأصحاب المصلحة — لا الوقوف اليومي (للفريق) ولا الاسترجاعي (داخلي للفريق). (The sprint/release review is the ceremony where working product is shown to stakeholders — not the daily standup or the team-internal retrospective.)"},
{"src":"Important","n":32,"lang":"ar","q":"هناك مشروع في تكراره الرابع من أصل ستة تكرارات. في أثناء تخطيط التكرار، يقوم أحد أعضاء الفريق بإبلاغ مدير المشروع أن جهاز الكمبيوتر الخاص به لا يعمل. ما الذي ينبغي لمدير المشروع فعله بعد ذلك؟","opts":["مطالبة أعضاء الفريق الآخرين بالتدخل والقيام بالعمل","اقتراح أن يتواصل عضو الفريق مع قسم تكنولوجيا المعلومات","تيسير حل المشكلة على أنها أولوية مع قسم تكنولوجيا المعلومات","تحديث سجل المشكلات وتصعيد المشكلة إلى مالك المنتج"],"c":["C"],"e":"مدير المشروع كقائد خادم (servant leader) يزيل المعوقات (impediments) بنفسه: يُيسّر حل مشكلة الجهاز كأولوية مع قسم تقنية المعلومات بدلاً من تحميل العضو العبء أو التصعيد. (Servant leadership — the PM removes the impediment by facilitating an urgent fix with IT, not by offloading it onto the member or escalating.)"},
{"src":"Important","n":66,"lang":"ar","q":"تمت الموافقة على ميثاق مشروع وتم تعيين مدير للمشروع. يعمل مدير المشروع حاليًا مع فريق المشروع لتقسيم حزم العمل في المشروع. ما الذي يفعله مدير المشروع؟","opts":["التجزئة (Decomposition)","هيكل تجزئة العمل (WBS)","موازنة الموارد","رسم تخطيطي للشبكة"],"c":["A"],"e":"تقسيم حزم العمل إلى مكونات أصغر هو أسلوب «التجزئة» (Decomposition) المستخدم لإنشاء هيكل تجزئة العمل (WBS هو الناتج، والتجزئة هي الأسلوب/الفعل). (Breaking work packages into smaller pieces is the technique of decomposition; the WBS is its output.)"},
{"src":"Important","n":101,"lang":"ar","q":"يتولى مدير مشروع إدارة أحد المشاريع ذات القيود الزمنية الصارمة. وفي اجتماعات الفريق، يلتزم أحد أعضاء الفريق الصمت دائمًا. يقرر مدير المشروع أن عضو الفريق هذا ليس على دراية بالحل التقني. كما يشعر بالقلق من تأثير هذه المشكلة في الإنتاجية. ما الذي ينبغي لمدير المشروع فعله؟","opts":["السماح للفريق بتولي قيادة مناقشات الاجتماع بحيث تتاح لكل عضو فرصة الإعداد والتحدث بصراحة","الترتيب لإعداد تدريب رسمي لهذا العضو لاكتساب المعرفة الكافية","تعيين عضو متمرس في الفريق للعمل مع هذا العضو على أساس يومي لكي يصبح على دراية بالتقنية","السماح لعضو الفريق هذا بفهم الضرورة الملحّة وتشجيعه على قضاء وقت إضافي ليصبح على دراية بالتقنية"],"c":["C"],"e":"ضعف المعرفة التقنية يُعالَج بالإرشاد المباشر: عيّن زميلاً متمرّساً للعمل معه يومياً (mentoring/pairing) — أسرع وأكثر دعماً من التدريب الرسمي أو الوقت الإضافي وسط قيود زمنية صارمة. (Pair the quiet, less-experienced member with a senior teammate daily — faster and more supportive than formal training or overtime under tight deadlines.)"},
{"src":"Important","n":108,"lang":"ar","q":"يخطط فريق مشروع لمراجعة الإطلاق التالي. يتلقى مدير المشروع رسالة بريد إلكتروني من أحد كبار المديرين يطلب فيها معرفة حالة المشروع باستخدام قالب أكثر تفصيلاً من القالب المستخدم للتقارير الشهرية المرسلة إلى مكتب إدارة المشاريع. ويستخدم الفريق سجل أعمال منتجات إلكتروني ولوحة كانبان لتتبع التقدم المحرز. ما الذي ينبغي لمدير المشروع فعله بعد ذلك؟","opts":["منح كبير المديرين إمكانية الوصول إلى سجل أعمال المنتجات","تنظيم مراجعة للوحة كانبان مع المدير","الاتصال بكبير المديرين ومناقشة احتياجاته","تعديل القالب المستخدم لمكتب إدارة المشاريع لتضمين المعلومات"],"c":["C"],"e":"افهم قبل أن تتصرف: اتصل بكبير المديرين وناقش احتياجاته الحقيقية من المعلومات أولاً، قبل منح صلاحيات أو تعديل قوالب. (Understand before acting — contact the senior manager to clarify the actual information need before granting access or changing templates.)"},
{"src":"Important","n":111,"lang":"ar","q":"قررت مؤسسة تبنّت مؤخرًا التبديلَ إلى أسلوب تسليم رشيق (أجايل) أن يواصل مديرو المشاريع إدارة المشاريع الجارية، نظرًا إلى التجاوزات غير المتوقعة في ميزانية المشروع 'أ'. فالإدارة العليا غير واثقة من استعداد فرق مشاريع الأسلوب الرشيق لإدارة ميزانية المشروع بنفسها. ما الإجراءان اللذان ينبغي على مدير المشروع اتخاذهما بخصوص المشروع 'أ'؟ (اختر اثنين)","opts":["دعوة الإدارة العليا لحضور الاجتماعات اليومية المختصرة لتقديم تقرير حالة عن النفقات","تطوير مستويات التصعيد مع الإدارة العليا فيما يتعلق بالنفقات","مطالبة مدرب الأسلوب الرشيق بإعداد تقرير أسبوعي عن نفقات المشروع للإدارة العليا","دعوة الإدارة العليا إلى إجراء التقييم بأثر رجعي لكل إطلاق بهدف تقييم النفقات","إنشاء شاشات معلومات (information radiators) بمساعدة مدرب الأسلوب الرشيق لتصوّر النفقات"],"c":["B","E"],"e":"لطمأنة الإدارة العليا القلقة بشأن الميزانية مع احترام مبادئ الأجايل: اتفق على مستويات تصعيد واضحة للنفقات، وأنشئ شاشات معلومات (information radiators) شفافة تُظهر النفقات بصرياً — بدلاً من إقحام الإدارة في الطقوس اليومية/الاسترجاعية المخصصة للفريق أو إثقال المدرب بتقارير أسبوعية. (Agree escalation thresholds for spend and build transparent cost information radiators — don't pull leadership into team-internal ceremonies or burden the coach with weekly reports.)"},
{"src":"Important","n":29,"lang":"ar","q":"سؤال مطابقة (Matching) — طابِق كل موقف بالحدث الرشيق الصحيح: (1) مالك المنتج يتلقى متطلبات جديدة من أصحاب المصلحة ويدرجها في تراكم المنتج. (2) الفريق حدّد أن سرعته انخفضت بنحو 30% في التكرار الأخير ثم وثّق السبب الجذري. (3) أحد الأعضاء يوضح أنه لن يُنهي مهمته لأن ليس لديه خبرة في النشاط. (4) الفريق قرّر الالتزام بنسبة 10% أكثر من قدرته في التكرار التالي وتقديمه لمالك المنتج. أي ترتيب صحيح؟","opts":["1=مراجعة التكرار، 2=الاسترجاعي (بأثر رجعي)، 3=الوقوف اليومي، 4=تخطيط التكرار","1=تخطيط التكرار، 2=الوقوف اليومي، 3=الاسترجاعي، 4=مراجعة التكرار","1=الاسترجاعي، 2=مراجعة التكرار، 3=تخطيط التكرار، 4=الوقوف اليومي","1=الوقوف اليومي، 2=تخطيط التكرار، 3=مراجعة التكرار، 4=الاسترجاعي"],"c":["A"],"e":"(سؤال مطابقة محوّل إلى اختيار متعدد — الإجابة مستنتجة من تعريفات أحداث سكرم.) مراجعة التكرار (Review): يُعرض المنتج لأصحاب المصلحة ويُجمع تعليقهم للتراكم. الاسترجاعي (Retrospective): يفحص الفريق أداءه/سرعته ويحدد التحسينات وأسبابها الجذرية. الوقوف اليومي (Daily Standup): يرفع الأعضاء المعوّقات (لن أُنهي المهمة...). تخطيط التكرار (Planning): يلتزم الفريق بحجم العمل للتكرار القادم. (Review=stakeholder feedback to backlog; Retrospective=inspect velocity/root cause; Daily Standup=raise impediments; Planning=commit next iteration's work.)"},
{"src":"Important","n":30,"lang":"ar","q":"سؤال مطابقة (Matching) — طابِق كل نشاط بالمنهجية الصحيحة: (1) تعيين نقاط القصة (story points) للقصص في سجل الأعمال. (2) تقدير مدة حزم العمل وإدراجها في الجدول الزمني للمشروع. (3) التأكد من أن كل مرحلة من مراحل المشروع تنتهي عند انتهاء التكرار والعكس صحيح. (4) الحصول على دعم أعضاء الفريق لتقدير المدة أو التعقيد. أي ترتيب صحيح؟","opts":["1=الأسلوب الرشيق، 2=التنبؤية/التقليدية، 3=الأسلوب الهجين، 4=جميع المنهجيات","1=التنبؤية، 2=الرشيق، 3=جميع المنهجيات، 4=الهجين","1=الهجين، 2=جميع المنهجيات، 3=الرشيق، 4=التنبؤية","1=جميع المنهجيات، 2=الهجين، 3=التنبؤية، 4=الرشيق"],"c":["A"],"e":"(سؤال مطابقة محوّل.) نقاط القصة = ممارسة رشيقة (Agile). تقدير مدة حزم العمل وإدراجها في جدول زمني = تنبؤية/تقليدية (Predictive). مواءمة المراحل مع التكرارات = هجين (Hybrid، يمزج المرحلية مع التكرار). إشراك الفريق في التقدير = ممارسة في جميع المنهجيات (تقدير تشاركي). (Story points=Agile; duration estimating into a schedule=Predictive; phases aligned to iterations=Hybrid; team-based estimating=All methodologies.)"},
{"src":"Important","n":31,"lang":"ar","q":"سؤال مطابقة (Matching) — طابِق كل وصف بالمصطلح الصحيح المتعلق بالمخزن المؤقت/القيود: (1) يتردد العمال في إنهاء المهام مبكرًا حتى لا يكشفوا كفاءتهم ويضعوا سوابق للمهام المستقبلية. (2) التسويف في بدء النشاط يستهلك المخزن المؤقت المدمج في مدته. (3) يستخدم العمال كل الوقت المخصص للنشاط بغض النظر عن الوقت الفعلي اللازم. (4) النشاط غير جاهز للبدء عند انتهاء سابقه مبكرًا، مما يضيّع الوقت بين الأنشطة. أي ترتيب صحيح؟","opts":["1=حماية الذات، 2=متلازمة الطالب، 3=قانون باركنسون، 4=فقد التسليم","1=قانون باركنسون، 2=فقد التسليم، 3=حماية الذات، 4=متلازمة الطالب","1=متلازمة الطالب، 2=حماية الذات، 3=فقد التسليم، 4=قانون باركنسون","1=فقد التسليم، 2=قانون باركنسون، 3=متلازمة الطالب، 4=حماية الذات"],"c":["A"],"e":"(سؤال مطابقة محوّل — مفاهيم سلسلة الحَرِجة Critical Chain.) حماية الذات (Self-protection): إخفاء الإنجاز المبكر تجنباً لرفع التوقعات. متلازمة الطالب (Student Syndrome): التأجيل حتى آخر لحظة فيستهلك المخزن. قانون باركنسون (Parkinson's Law): العمل يتمدّد ليملأ الوقت المتاح. فقد التسليم (Hand-off loss): ضياع الوقت في تسليم العمل بين الأنشطة. (Critical-chain buffer concepts: self-protection, student syndrome, Parkinson's law, hand-off loss.)"},
{"src":"Important","n":32,"lang":"ar","q":"سؤال مطابقة (Matching) — سيبدأ الفريق أول مشروع رشيق ولا أحد لديه معرفة بالمنهجية الرشيقة. طابِق دور الفريق بالدورة التدريبية الأنسب: (1) مالك المنتج (Product Owner). (2) قائد سكروم (Scrum Master). أي ترتيب صحيح؟","opts":["1=تحليل الأعمال في الممارسة، 2=القيادة الخدمية للفريق","1=القيادة الخدمية للفريق، 2=تحليل الأعمال في الممارسة","1=القيادة الخدمية للفريق، 2=القيادة الخدمية للفريق","1=تحليل الأعمال في الممارسة، 2=تحليل الأعمال في الممارسة"],"c":["A"],"e":"(سؤال مطابقة محوّل.) مالك المنتج مسؤول عن القيمة والمتطلبات وترتيب التراكم → يناسبه تدريب «تحليل الأعمال في الممارسة» (Business Analysis). قائد سكروم خادمٌ ييسّر ويزيل المعوقات → يناسبه «القيادة الخدمية للفريق» (Servant Leadership). (PO → Business Analysis; Scrum Master → Servant Leadership.)"},
{"src":"Important","n":33,"lang":"ar","q":"سؤال مطابقة (Matching) — شركة متعددة الجنسيات تطرح منتجًا في دول مختلفة ولاحظ مدير المشروع أن ديناميكيات الفريق تؤثر سلبًا في الأداء. طابِق كل إجراء بالنتيجة المرجوّة لضمان ترابط الفريق: (1) تنظيم اجتماع للفريق لتحديد الأهداف. (2) إشراك كل الفرق بشكل منسّق. (3) جمع الملاحظات من أعضاء الفريق. (4) احترام الاختلافات الثقافية. أي ترتيب هو الأقرب؟","opts":["1=يزيد الفهم المشترك، 2=يقلّل فجوات التواصل، 3=يشجّع التعاون، 4=يُظهر الشمولية","1=يُظهر الشمولية، 2=يشجّع التعاون، 3=يقلّل فجوات التواصل، 4=يزيد الفهم المشترك","1=يشجّع التعاون، 2=يزيد الفهم المشترك، 3=يُظهر الشمولية، 4=يقلّل فجوات التواصل","1=يقلّل فجوات التواصل، 2=يُظهر الشمولية، 3=يزيد الفهم المشترك، 4=يشجّع التعاون"],"c":["A"],"e":"(سؤال مطابقة محوّل — والمطابقة هنا اجتهادية إلى حدٍّ ما؛ استخدم زر «حلّل مع Claude» لمناقشتها.) تحديد الأهداف معًا → يزيد الفهم المشترك؛ الإشراك المنسّق لكل الفرق → يقلّل فجوات التواصل؛ جمع الملاحظات → يشجّع التعاون؛ احترام الثقافات → يُظهر الشمولية. (Somewhat subjective mapping: define goals→shared understanding, coordinated engagement→fewer comms gaps, gather feedback→collaboration, respect cultures→inclusivity.)"},
{"src":"Important","n":34,"lang":"ar","q":"سؤال مطابقة (Matching) — طابِق هدف مدير المشروع بمعلمة الخطر (risk parameter) ذات الصلة: (1) تقدير المدة بعد وقوع الخطر وقبل اكتشاف تأثيره. (2) تقييم مدى السرعة المطلوبة لتنفيذ الاستجابة لكي تكون فعّالة. (3) تقييم ما إذا كان الخطر مرتبطًا بمخاطر المشروع الفردية الأخرى. (4) تقدير الوقت قبل أن يؤثر الخطر في هدف أو أكثر من أهداف المشروع. أي ترتيب صحيح؟","opts":["1=السكون (Dormancy)، 2=الضرورة الملحّة (Urgency)، 3=إمكانية الترابط (Connectivity)، 4=التقارب (Proximity)","1=التقارب، 2=السكون، 3=الضرورة الملحّة، 4=إمكانية الترابط","1=الضرورة الملحّة، 2=إمكانية الترابط، 3=التقارب، 4=السكون","1=إمكانية الترابط، 2=التقارب، 3=السكون، 4=الضرورة الملحّة"],"c":["A"],"e":"(سؤال مطابقة محوّل — معلمات تقييم المخاطر في PMBOK.) السكون (Dormancy): الفترة بين وقوع الخطر واكتشاف تأثيره. الضرورة الملحّة (Urgency): الوقت المتاح لتنفيذ استجابة فعّالة. إمكانية الترابط (Connectivity): مدى ارتباط الخطر بمخاطر فردية أخرى. التقارب (Proximity): الوقت قبل أن يؤثر الخطر في الأهداف. (PMBOK risk parameters: Dormancy, Urgency, Connectivity, Proximity.)"},
# __APPEND_HERE__
]
